{"id": "qa_sats_0000_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the emergency signs in pediatric patients according to the SATS guidelines?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage Scale\n(SATS)\nTraining manual 2012\nTable of Contents\nTABLE OF COnTEnTs\nTable of Contents I\nAcknowledgements II\n1. Introduction 1\n1.1 The benefits of implementing SATS 1\n1.2 Triage requirements 2\n1.3 Who should be the triage provider? 2\n1.4 Terminology and key concepts 3\nAssessment Questions 5\n2. The SATS process flowchart 6\n2.1 The five step approach 6\n2.2 The SATS process flowchart 6\n2.3 SATS priority levels 6\n2.4 Terminology and key concepts 7\nAssessment Questions 7\nAdult SATS Chart 8\nPaediatric SATS Chart 10\n3. Adult Clinical signs 12\n3.1 Emergency signs 12\n3.2 Very urgent signs 12\n3.3 Urgent signs 13\nAssessment Questions 13\n4. Paediatric Clinical signs 14\n4.1 Emergency signs: the abc-c-c-do approach 14\n4.1.1 Ab airway and breathing are usually assessed together 14\n4.1.2 C circulation assessment 16\n4.1.3 C-c coma and convulsions assessment 16\n4.1.4 D severe dehydration assessment 17\n4.1.5 O other emergency signs 18\n4.2 Very urgent signs 18\n4.3 Urgent signs 21\nAssessment Questions 24"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0000", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0001_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key components of the Triage Early Warning score (TEWs)?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "5. Triage Early Warning score TEWs 25\n5.1 Observations at triage 26\n5.2 Terminology and key concepts 26\nAssessment Questions 27\n6. Additional Investigations 28\nAssessment Questions 29\n7. Additional Tasks 30\nAssessment Questions 32"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0001", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0002_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key components of effective triage in a hospital setting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "8. Triage in Context 33\n8.1 Pre-Hospital 33\n8.2 Patient streaming 33\n8.3 Infrastructure 33\n8.4 Alignment of staff to temporal flow of patients 33\n9. summary 34\n10. References 34\nSATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 I\nAcknowledgements Introduction\nACKnOWLEDGEMEnTs 1 InTRODUCTIOn\nOn behalf of the\nPaediatric Triage\nThe South African Triage Group (SATG) would like to Working Group Learning Objectives:\nand the\nthank the Paediatric Triage Working Group (PTWG) of south African Triage • Understand the purpose of triage and the benefi ts of implementing the SATS\nthe Western Cape Government (WCG) of South Africa Group • Be familiar with the requirements for standardised triage implementation\n(SA) (under the auspices of the Paediatric Provincial • Understand the terminology and key concepts around triage\nPaediatric Triage\nCo-ordinating Committee & the Emergency Medicine\nWorking Group\nProvincial Co-ordinating Committee) for their efforts and A nine-month old baby boy is carried into the children’s section of the outpatient department in his\nAnthony Westwood\nmother’s arms. He appears to be asleep. At the triage desk he is seen by a nurse and found to have\nhard work in developing paediatric triage based on two Baljit Cheema lips and tongue that are grey/blue in colour, and he is taken straight into the resuscitation room as an\nexisting triage tools: the Emergency Triage Assessment Heloise Buys emergency.\nand Treatment (ETAT) of the World Health Organization Jean Augustyn In the resuscitation room he"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0002", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0003_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the initial resuscitation steps for a critically ill child in the emergency room?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "esuscitation room as an\nexisting triage tools: the Emergency Triage Assessment Heloise Buys emergency.\nand Treatment (ETAT) of the World Health Organization Jean Augustyn In the resuscitation room he is given oxygen at 15 litres/minute by face mask with a non-rebreather\n(WHO) and the South African Triage Scale (SATS). Heather Tuffi n reservoir bag. He is noted to be grunting and breathing very fast. His hands are cold to touch and the\nMichael Lee capillary refi ll time is prolonged to four seconds. An intravenous cannula is placed. A blood sample is\nThe SATG and PTWG wishes to acknowledge the WHO Andrew Argent taken at the same time for blood glucose, full blood count and blood culture. An intravenous infusion\nof normal saline is commenced at 20ml/kg to run as fast as it can go.\nDepartment of Child and Adolescent Health and Shaheem de Vries\nDevelopment and Professor Elizabeth Molyneux, who Lee Wallis Other treatments are given, depending on the result of the investigations and the response to\nthe treatment he receives. It is now 18 minutes since the baby came through the outpatient\nZanele Nxumalo\ndeveloped the ETAT training course on which these department’s door, and his situation is stable. It is now time to take a full history and carry out a full\nLouise Cooke examination to make a defi nitive diagnosis. He is diagnosed as having very severe pneumonia, and\ncourse materials are based. We are also grateful to the\nPeter Lesch receives specifi c treatment for this. However, be"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0003", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0004_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the importance of timely triage and emergency management in very sick patients, especially children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ake a defi nitive diagnosis. He is diagnosed as having very severe pneumonia, and\ncourse materials are based. We are also grateful to the\nPeter Lesch receives specifi c treatment for this. However, before coming to this diagnosis, no time was wasted, his\nETAT-South Africa (ETAT-SA) working group for all their Majedah Ismail status was stabilized, based on a few leading signs and symptoms, even when the medical staff did\nefforts and hard work in adapting and developing Liesl Strauss not know exactly what was wrong with him.\nthe generic ETAT materials in to the ETAT-SA materials Angela de Sá This was good triage and emergency management. Would it have happened like this in your\nJaco Slabbert hospital? In this training course, you are going to acquire the necessary knowledge for the correct\nspecifi cally for the South African setting. triage of sick children and adults.\nWendy Rosenthal\nAll reasonable precautions have been taken by the Michèle Twomey Many deaths in hospital occur within 24 hours of admission. Some of these deaths can be prevented\nif very sick patients (especially children) are quickly identifi ed on their arrival and treatment is started\nPTWG, the ETAT-SA working group, WHO and SATS group\nETAT-sA without delay. In many hospitals around the world, children are not checked before a senior health\nto verify the information contained in this publication. Working Group worker examines them; as a result, some seriously ill patients have to wait a very long time before t"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0004", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0005_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the purpose of triage in healthcare settings?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "cked before a senior health\nto verify the information contained in this publication. Working Group worker examines them; as a result, some seriously ill patients have to wait a very long time before they\nare seen and treated. Children are known to have died of a treatable condition when waiting in the\nHowever, the published material is being distributed Lesley Bamford queue for their turn. The idea of triage is to prevent this from happening.\nwithout warranty of any kind, either express or implied. Gerry Boon\nThe purpose of triage is to prioritise patients based on medical urgency in contexts where there is a\nThe responsibility for the interpretation and use of the Heloise Buys mismatch between demand and capacity (i.e. patient load overwhelms the available resources).\nBaljit Cheema\nmaterial lies with the reader. In no event shall the PTWG,\nSue Harris\nthe ETAT-SA working group, WHO or the SATG be liable 1.1 The benefi ts of implementing SATS\nMark Patrick\nfor damages arising from its use. Cindy Stephen 1. expedite the delivery of time-critical treatment for patients with life-threatening conditions.\n2. ensure that all patients are appropriately prioritised according to their medical urgency.\nChris Sutton\nDr Baljit Cheema and Dr Michèle Twomey 3. improve patient fl ow.\n4. improve patient satisfaction.\n5. decrease the patient’s overall length of stay.\n6. facilitate streaming of less urgent patients.\n7. provide a user-friendly tool for all levels of health care professionals.\nBy"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0005", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0006_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What impact did introducing the SATS have on patient waiting times at a public urban hospital in Cape Town?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "atient satisfaction.\n5. decrease the patient’s overall length of stay.\n6. facilitate streaming of less urgent patients.\n7. provide a user-friendly tool for all levels of health care professionals.\nBy introducing the SATS at a public urban hospital in Cape Town, mean waiting times were reduced\nsignifi cantly for all priority levels except the non-urgent green category. The most dramatic reduction\nin waiting times was seen in patients coded as red (82%).1\nII SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 1\nIntroduction Introduction\n1.2 Triage requirements 1.4 Terminology and key concepts"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0006", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0007_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the purpose of triage in a hospital or clinic setting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage is simple to do, but in order to standardise the process and comprehensively implement the 1. Triage, from the French word “trier”, literally means: “to sort”. The aim is to bring “the greatest\nSATS as a validated tool certain requirements need to be met. Table 1 shows the equipment needed good to the greatest number of people” – this is achieved through prioritising limited resources\nfor the process and Appendix A on page 4 includes a detailed checklist of requirements. to achieve the greatest possible benefit. Patients are sorted with a scientific triage scale in\norder of urgency - the end result is that the patient with the greatest need is helped first.\nLocation Equipment Additional equipment 2. Patient to triage: for the hospital or clinic context this refers to a patient that appears relatively\nstable and is able to mobilise him/herself to the designated triage area. This will be the type of\nPrivacy: Screen, partition or Gloves, face masks & other Pulse oximeter with paediatric triage used for most hospital and clinic cases.\nseparate room. barrier protective devices probes"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0007", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0008_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Where should an unstable patient who cannot mobilize be triaged?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "3. Triage to patient: here the patient is usually unstable. The patient is unable to mobilise him/\nherself to the designated triage area and will need to be triaged where they are found. They\nSafety: Security/protected Wall clock ECG\nmay need to be referred directly to the resuscitation (resus) area if they are at a health facility."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0008", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0009_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Can triage be performed at the bedside and documented later?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage may also be performed at the bedside and documented in retrospect. This type of\nSize of area: pushchairs, Low reading electronic/ Finger prick machine,"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0009", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0010_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the Triage Early Warning (TEW) score and how is it calculated?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "triage will be used less often in the hospital context and predominantly in the pre-hospital\nwheelchairs, stretchers mercury thermometer haemoglobin and\nglucometer measurement context.\n4. Physiology (i.e. vital signs): refers to the normal functioning of the different body systems.\nAccessibility Vital signs monitor OR bauma- Urine collection containers,\nSome of the physiology can be readily measured (e.g. pulse, blood pressure, respiratory rate,\nnometer with paediatric cuffs urine dipsticks & urine\ntemperature).\npregnancy tests\n5. TEWs: Triage Early Warning score. This is a composite score of the patient’s physiology. The\nBaby-changing facilities Dry dressings/ bandages score is derived by assigning a number between 0 and 2 for each of the patient’s vital signs.\nThe higher the score the greater the urgency.\nTable 1: Requirements for adequate / efficient triage\n6. streaming: the use of dedicated healthcare resources for each priority group of patients. For\n1.3 Who should be the triage provider?\ngreen patients, this may be a doctor or nurse practitioner: this person needs their own space\nNurse-based triage has been successfully implemented worldwide in the countries of North America, to see these patients.\nEurope, the Middle East and Australasia since the development of Emergency Medicine as a\nspeciality about 30 years ago. Table 2 shows the number of medical practitioners and nurses per unit 7. Pain: Severe pain is unbearable, the worst pain the patient has ever felt. It may b"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0010", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0011_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the definitions of severe, moderate, and mild pain in the context of triage?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "edicine as a\nspeciality about 30 years ago. Table 2 shows the number of medical practitioners and nurses per unit 7. Pain: Severe pain is unbearable, the worst pain the patient has ever felt. It may be associated\nof population in South Africa, compared to some “developed” countries. Given the significantly lower with sweatiness, paleness, and altered level of consciousness. Moderate pain is intense, but\ndoctor: nurse ratio in South Africa compared to countries where nurse triage is widely practiced, it is bearable. Mild pain is any other pain. Remember to do a pain assessment on every patient\napparent that the development of nurse-based triage should be a priority in our setting. that you see.\n8. Additional Investigation: You will find this exclamation icon in later chapters.\nCountry Rate per 100,000 population/ year ! It represents an additional investigation which may lead to a change in the\npatient’s triage priority level. Checking the blood glucose concentration or\nDoctors nurses Doctor: nurse ratio measuring the oxygen saturation level are examples of additional investigations.\nSection six outlines all key additional investigations important at triage. They can\nSouth Africa 56.3 471.2 1 : 8.0 also be found on the SATS charts.\n9. Warning: The lightbulb icon indicates a warning that usually follows immediately\nCanada 229 897 1 : 4.0\nafter an additional investigation, implying that some immediate action is required\nAustralia 240 830 1 : 3.4 (e.g. a child whose oxygen satura"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0011", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0012_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are examples of additional tasks that are beneficial to initiate at triage but do not change the patient’s triage priority level?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "g that usually follows immediately\nCanada 229 897 1 : 4.0\nafter an additional investigation, implying that some immediate action is required\nAustralia 240 830 1 : 3.4 (e.g. a child whose oxygen saturation levels are found to be 80% requires oxygen\nadministration and should be taken to the resuscitation area).\nIsrael 385 613 1 : 1.6\n10. Additional tasks: The icon with a red cross represents additional tasks that are\nbeneficial to the patient if initiated at triage. These additional tasks do not change\nUK 164 479 1 : 3.0\nthe patient’s triage priority level. Examples include starting oral rehydration\nTable 2: D octor and nurse rates per 100,000 population per annum for selected countries therapy for a child that is dehydrated, cooling a burn that occurred within 3 hours\nor applying direct pressure to an uncontrolled haemorrhage.\nNurses are the first medical contact for the patients attending the Emergency Centre in most\ninstances. In South African studies, adequately trained Enrolled Nursing Assistants (ENAs) have been\nshown to be accurate to a degree comparable with international standards of nursing triage.2 3\nThe South African Triage Scale should be known and applied by all health care professionals\ninvolved in the Emergency Centre. The triage provider can be the medical officer, the registered\nnurse, enrolled nurse or the ENA. The purpose of this training program is to empower the individual\nwho participates with the knowledge to triage. It will only be through practice and"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0012", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0013_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the purpose of triage according to the triage emergency centre requirements checklist?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "the registered\nnurse, enrolled nurse or the ENA. The purpose of this training program is to empower the individual\nwho participates with the knowledge to triage. It will only be through practice and repetition that a\nprovider will become skilled with triage. Successful providers are therefore encouraged to participate\nin triaging as frequently as possible in order to stay in practice and up to date.\n2 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 3\nIntroduction Introduction\nAPPEnDIX A: Checklist of triage emergency centre requirements Assessment Questions\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\nstructural Requirements\n1. The purpose of triage is to prevent deterioration or death of a patient while waiting in the\nqueue for their turn.\nDoes the triage area meet the following criteria: Yes No\nTrue False\n1 Is the triage area a dedicated space?\n2. The triage method should be known and applied by clinical nurse practitioners only.\n2 Is the triage area well signed? True False\n3 Is the triage area secure (i.e. behind the security gate, or in easy view of 3. Streaming is the process of getting patients to wait for as long as possible.\nsecurity staff)?\nTrue False\nIs the triage area at least 10 square meters in size (i.e. should be able to\n4\naccommodate a nurse, patient in a wheelchair and relative or carer)?\nChoose the correct answer:\nChecklist of triage Infrastructure Requirements\n4. The benefits of triage are:\n(a) To facilitate streaming of green p"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0013", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0014_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the benefits of triage in an emergency setting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "nurse, patient in a wheelchair and relative or carer)?\nChoose the correct answer:\nChecklist of triage Infrastructure Requirements\n4. The benefits of triage are:\n(a) To facilitate streaming of green patients\nContent Requirements\n(b) To decrease the waiting time of life-threatening conditions\nDoes the triage area contain the following: Yes No (c) To ensure that patients are appropriately prioritized by urgency\n(d) To prevent children from dying in the waiting room\n1 A desk and chair?\n(e) All of the above"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0014", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0015_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the essential items and equipment required for triage in an emergency department?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "2 Triage paperwork for adult, children and infants? 5. TEWS is short for:\n(a) Triage Early Warning System\n3 A wall clock with a second hand? (b) Trauma Early Warning Scale\n(c) Triage Emergency Warning System\n4 A stethoscope? (d) Triage Early Warning Score\n(e) Trauma Emergency Waiting Score\n5 A low reading thermometer?\n6 Dry dressings and bandages?\n7 Gloves?\n8 Sphygmomanometer (manual, digital or electronic)?\n9 Blood glucose monitor?\nA measuring tape OR marks displayed on wall in triage area to measure\n10\nchildren (i.e one mark at 95cm and one at 150 cm)?\n11 2 x different SATS posters prominently displayed in triage area?\n12 SATS manual readily available for triage office as a source of info?\n13 SATS patient info leaflet prominently displayed in the waiting area?"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0015", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0016_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the five steps involved in the SATS triage process?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "14 Triage register or computer with register?\n15 White board to track and communicate to other staff acuity of those triaged?\n4 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 5\nThe SATS process flowchart\n2 ThE sATs PROCEss FLOWChART\nLearning Objectives:\n• Understand the five step approach and SATS process flowchart\n• Be familiar with the two versions of the SATS chart\n• Be familiar with the priority levels of SATS\n2.1 The five step approach\nstep 1: Look for emergency signs and ask for the presenting complaint\nStep 2: Look for very urgent OR urgent signs\nStep 3: Measure the vital signs and calculate the TEWS\nStep 4: Check key additional investigations\nStep 5: Assign final triage priority level\nFigure 1: SATS five step approach\nThe process of triage starts with a question to the mother/carer/patient as to the reason for coming to th e\nemergency centre. As this question is being asked and answered the triage process already commence s\nwith the triage practitioner rapidly assessing the patient for any Emergency clinical signs. The Airway,\nBreathing, Circulation, Coma, Convulsion, Dehydration, Other (ABC-c-c-DO) approach is used for\npaediatric patients. If emergency clinical signs are found, the patient is assigned a Red priority level\nand taken straight to the resuscitation area without delay.\nIf no Emergency clinical signs are present then check for any Very Urgent or Urgent clinical signs.\nWhether these are present or not, vital signs are measured, the TEWS is calculated,"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0016", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0017_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the steps in the SATS process for determining triage priority when emergency clinical signs are not present?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "without delay.\nIf no Emergency clinical signs are present then check for any Very Urgent or Urgent clinical signs.\nWhether these are present or not, vital signs are measured, the TEWS is calculated, key additional\ninvestigations are checked and the triage priority adjusted as\nshown in Figure 2.\nLOOK FOR\nIt is important to note that if a patient has any emergency signs EMERGENCY\nthen a TEWS does NOT need to be calculated at triage. There SIGNS AND ASK FOR PRESENTING COMPLAINT\nshould be no delay in taking the patient to the resuscitation area.\nTAKE TO NO\nFinally the senior healthcare professional’s (SHCP) discretion as seen RESUS\nin Figure 2, allows the clinical nurse practitioner or senior doctor to\noverride the final triage priority assigned.\nNO 2.2 The SATS process flowchart\nThere are two versions of the SATS chart as seen in Figure 4 and 5 .\nThe paediatric version of the SATS chart is used to triage all patients\nyounger than 12 years and smaller than 150 cm. The adult version of\nYES NO\nthe SATS chart is used to triage patients older than 12 years or taller\nthan 150 cm.\nBoth age specific versions have the exact same SATS process EMERGENCY\nflowchart as depicted in Figure 2. The five steps in Figure 1 are TEWS 7 OR\nintegrated into this process flowchart. The differences are found in MORE\ntheir respective clinical signs and their age appropriate TEWS. The\nnext two sections describe in detail the adult and paediatric clinical\nsigns respectively.\n2.3 SATS priority levels\nT T h a"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0017", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0018_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the difference between Red and Resus patients in the SATS triage system?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in MORE\ntheir respective clinical signs and their age appropriate TEWS. The\nnext two sections describe in detail the adult and paediatric clinical\nsigns respectively.\n2.3 SATS priority levels\nT T h a e b l S e o 3 u . t E h a A c f h ri c p a ri n o r T it ri y a l g e e v e S l c s a h l o e u h ld a s id f e o a ur ll y p b rio e r i m ty a le n v a e g ls e a d s w sh it o h w in n t h in e U 5 R V T E O G E W R R E Y S N 6 T U 3 R T E O G W R E S N 4 T 0 R , O T 1 E U W O TI S R N E 2\ntarget time to treatment.\n6 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 7\nDESAECED\nThe SATS process flowchart\n2.4 Terminology and key concepts\n1. Red vs Resus: Patients may be triaged Red on the basis of their presentation, but not\nnecessarily be a full resuscitation case. Conversely, if a patient presents to you as a resus you\ndo not need a triage tool to tell you that they are a Red case. For those patients who present\nlike this (e.g Cardiac arrest), triage before treatment is not necessary – if a patient is a resus,\nthey are Red by definition.\n2. Majors vs Minors: The majors area in a hospital is staffed by appropriately trained personnel\nand advanced equipment to deal with emergency, very urgent and urgent patients. The\nminors area is staffed by appropriately trained personnel and the respective equipment and\nresources to deal with routine or non-urgent patients.\nPriority COLOUR Target time Management\nRED IMMEDIATE Take to the resuscitation room for emergency management\nORAnGE <"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0018", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0019_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the SATS priority levels and their corresponding target times for patient assessment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "he respective equipment and\nresources to deal with routine or non-urgent patients.\nPriority COLOUR Target time Management\nRED IMMEDIATE Take to the resuscitation room for emergency management\nORAnGE < 10 mins Refer to majors for very urgent management\nYELLOW < 1 hour Refer to majors for urgent management\nGREEn < 4 hours Refer to designated area for non-urgent cases\nBLUE < 2 hours Refer to doctor for certification\nTable 3: SATS priority levels and target times to be seen with-in\nAssessment Questions\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\n1. There are two SATS charts – one for paediatric patients and one for adult patients.\nTrue False\nYES\n2. If an emergency sign is identified in the first step, the patient is taken to the resuscitation area\nimmediately.\nLOOK FOR True False\nVERY URGENT\nSIGNS 3. If no emergency signs are identified in step one, but an urgent sign is identified in step two, the\nYES patient is immediately triaged yellow and asked to wait.\nLOOK FOR URGENT True False\nSIGNS\nChoose the correct answer:\nMEASURE 4. The SATS priority level Orange is defined as:\nVITAL SIGNS\n(a) Emergency requiring immediate intervention\nCALCULATE (b) Very Urgent requiring intervention within 10 minutes\nTEWS (c) Urgent requires intervention within 60 minutes\n(d) Routine requiring intervention within 240 minutes\nADDITIONAL\n(e) Life threatening but not requiring any intervention\nINVESTIGATION\n5. The senior healthcare professional’s discretion refers to:\n(a) The ju"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0019", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0020_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Which healthcare professionals can override the final triage decision according to the senior healthcare professional’s discretion?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "tine requiring intervention within 240 minutes\nADDITIONAL\n(e) Life threatening but not requiring any intervention\nINVESTIGATION\n5. The senior healthcare professional’s discretion refers to:\n(a) The junior nurse overriding the final triage decision (b) The clinical nurse practitioner overriding the final triage decision\n(c) The medical student overriding the final triage decision\nPRO S FE E S N S IO IO R N H A E L A ’S L T D H IS C C A R R E E TION (d) The medical officer overriding the final triage decision\n(e) b and d above\nFigure 2: The SATS process flowchart\nThe SATS process fl owchart The SATS process fl owchart\n8 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 9\ntrahC\nSTAS\ntludA\nROF\nKOOL\nYCNEGREME\nROF\nKSA\nDNA\nSNGIS\nTNIALPMOC\nGNITNESERP\nON\nOT\nEKAT\nSUSER\nON\nON\nSEY\nYCNEGREME\nSWET RO\n7\nEROM YREV\nENITUOR\nTNEGRU\nTNEGRU\nSWET\nSWET\nSWET\n2\nRO\n1\n,0\n4\nRO\n3\n6\nRO\n5\ntrahc\nSTAS\ntludA\n:4\nerugiF\nDESAECED\nYCNEGREME gnihtaerb ton - yawriA detcurtsbO tnerruc -eruzieS\nSEY\nnoitalahni / laicaf - nruB 3 naht ssel esoculg - aimeacylgopyH tserra caidraC TNEGRU YREV )yrujni fo msinahcem ereves( refsnart ygrene hgiH etuca - htaeb fo ssentrohS desufnoc / decuder ssensuoicsnoc fo leveL doolb gnihguoC niap tsehC kcen debbatS )deelb lairetra( dellortnocnu - egahrromeaH\nROF\nKOOL\nlatci tsop -eruzieS )ekorts( etuca - ygoloruen lacoF\nTNEGRU\nYREV\nnoisserggA bmil denetaerhT yrujnI eyE\nSNGIS\n)eot ro regnif ton( tnioj regral fo noitacolsiD )niks ni kaerb a htiw( dnuopmoc - erutcarF %02 revo nruB lacirtcele"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0020", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
