{"id": "qa_sats_0047_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should the size of a burn be determined in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ll can deteriorate\nDislocation of larger joint rapidly.\n(not finger or toe)\n18 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 19\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediatric Clinical Signs\nThe size of the burn needs to be determined using the Rule APPEnDIX B: Examples of different pain scales\nof Nines method (the palmar method is more suitable for Behavioral Observation Pain Rating Scale\nsmaller burns - see page 23). For any child with a major burn\nresuscitation must be commenced. Follow the provincial burns Categories scoring"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0047", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0048_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a recent burn be cooled and managed in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "management guidelines 2011 including analgesia. 0 1 2\nFace No particular Occasional grimace or Frequent to constant\nThe example left is of a one year-old child. Refer to Table in the\nexpression or smile; frown, withdrawn frown, clenched jaw,\nprovincial Emergency Management of Severe Burns manual for\ndisinterested quivering chin\ndifferent ages.\nLegs No position or relaxed Uneasy, restless, tense Kicking, or legs drawn up\nActivity Lying quietly, normal Squirming, shifting back Arched, rigid or jerking\nposition, moves easily and forth, tense\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nIf the burn occurred recently (within 3hrs)  Cry No crying Moans or whimpers, Crying steadily, screams or\n(awake or asleep) occasional complaint sobs, frequent complaints\nit is still worthwhile to cool the burnt area with\nwater, for example, by running cool tap water Consolability Content, relaxed Reassured by occasional Difficult to console or\nover the burnt area for 30 minutes. The child should then touching, hugging, or comfort\nbe dried and wrapped in a clean sheet or blanket to avoid talking to. Distractable\nhypothermia. The burn can be covered in cling wrap if\navailable, or a clean dry sheet or towel will also be suitable. Each of the five categories: (F) Face; (L) Legs; (A) Activity; (C) Cry; (C) Consolabilit y\nis scored from 0 - 2, which results in a total score between 0 and 10.\nFigure 10: Rule of Nines for burn\nsurface area estimation in a one Observe the child and score the child’s pain according to the ‘FLACC’ sca"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0048", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
