i n r s d u gar ! I h n is V to E r s y T o IG f d A ia TI b O e n tes  do a"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0051", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0052_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the signs of some dehydration in children with diarrhoea or vomiting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "h w en it h th d e ia y b a e re te u s n o w ft e e l n l f r lo o s m e a c c o u n t t e ro i l l l o ne f t s h s e a i n r s d u gar ! I h n is V to E r s y T o IG f d A ia TI b O e n tes  do a\ncome with practice and experience. If you have any doubts, move to resuscitation area infections e.g. acute respiratory infections, finger prick glucose test\nconsult a senior health care professional immediately. (upper or lower) urinary tract infections or diarrhoea.\nThey often develop high or low\nSome Dehydration - Diarrhoea or diarrhoea and vomiting + 1 or more signs of dehydration\nblood glucose levels because WARnInG WARnInG\nInfants and children with diarrhoea or vomiting may have lost enough fluid to show one or more of their insulin needs may increase If glucose is below If glucose result\nthe following signs of dehydration: or decrease whilst they are 3 mmol/L  move is ‘hI’ \nunwell and they are not eating to resuscitation check with shCP\n(i) sunken eyes- ask the mother (iv) dry mouth\nas usual because of loss of\n(ii) restless/ irritable (v) crying without tears appetite, nausea or vomiting.\n(iii) thirsty/ decreased urine output (vi) skin pinch slow - less than 2 seconds\nBurn less than 10% of body surface area\nBecause these children have losses that are ongoing and difficult to quantify, they need to The size of the burn needs to be determined using the palmar\ncommence oral rehydration therapy as soon as possible to avoid becoming severely dehydrated or method (patient’s o"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0052", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0053_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should burn size be estimated in children using the palmar method?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "o quantify, they need to The size of the burn needs to be determined using the palmar\ncommence oral rehydration therapy as soon as possible to avoid becoming severely dehydrated or method (patient’s outstretched open palm including the\nshocked i.e. signs that would now place them into the RED fingers is ≈ to 1% of the body surface area).\ncategory. Most of them are thirsty and their mothers need\nto offer them extra fluids according to the Oral Rehydration ADDITIOnAL TAsK ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nCorner guidelines. Infants and young children that are Diarrhoe  start oral Pain  check with shCP\nbreast-fed should be encouraged to continue to do so rehydration therapy (ORT) for analgesia initiation\nwhilst awaiting further urgent assessment.\nUnable to drink/feed OR vomits everything\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nInfants and children that are not well enough to feed or are vomiting everything may have serious Burn less than 10%  follow the provincial\nmedical or surgical conditions affecting any of the body systems. Examples include septicaemia, burns management guidelines. If the burn\nmeningitis, heart conditions, acute surgical occurred recently, within the last 3 hours, it\nVomiting only and dehydration: abdomen, pneumonia etc. is still worthwhile to cool the burnt area with\nwater, for example, with cool tap water for at least\n• beware the child may have an acute surgical\nbowel problem ADDITIOnAL TAsK 30 minutes. The burn should then be dried and covered\nwith cling wrap or a clean dry sheet. The child"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0053", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0054_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key steps in managing a burn injury in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ap water for at least\n• beware the child may have an acute surgical\nbowel problem ADDITIOnAL TAsK 30 minutes. The burn should then be dried and covered\nwith cling wrap or a clean dry sheet. The child should\n• oral fluids may be contraindicated V de o h m y i d tin ra g t i o o n n l y  a nd also be wrapped in a blanket and kept warm F ill i u g s u tr r a e t 1 in 1 g : P 1 a % lm of o t r h m e e b t o ho d d y\n• seek advice from a senior health professional\nconsult with shCP\nClosed fracture (no break in the skin)\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nMalnutrition ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn WARnInG T to h e m se o v c e h i t ld he re a n f o fe ft c e t n e d p r li e m se b n . t T h w e it r h e p m a a in y o b n e m de o f v o in rm g it o y r o re f f t u h s e a l Pain  check with shCP\nwith visible ! Malnutrition with visible severe If glucose is below limb evident. for analgesia initiation\nsevere wasting  do a finger prick glucose 3 mmol/L  move\nwasting test and check with shCP to resuscitation\nSevere ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nwasting is a form of severe malnutrition. These children are easy to pick up because their muscles are ver y Closed fracture  If required get a wheelchair or stretcher and immobilise the affected limb\nwasted and they have loose skin folds particularly noticeable around their upper thighs, buttocks and with a simple splint e.g. a padded wire splint or triangular bandage that does not interfere with\nupper arms. If you are concerned that a child might be malnourished look rap"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0054", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0055_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the clinical signs and immediate management steps for severely malnourished children with pitting oedema of both feet?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "pper thighs, buttocks and with a simple splint e.g. a padded wire splint or triangular bandage that does not interfere with\nupper arms. If you are concerned that a child might be malnourished look rapidly at these body area s. the blood supply or nerve bundle, and provide analgesia according your site’s guidelines.\nSeverely malnourished children are prone to hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to\nbe managed urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition guidelines to prevent complications .\nDislocation of finger or toe ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nMalnutrition with pitting oedema of both feet ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn This often causes an obvious deformity; the dislocated digit Pain  check with shCP\nThis is another form of severe malnutrition. It is ! Malnutrition with visible severe will need to be reduced under some form of anaesthesia. for analgesia initiation\nas dangerous as the one described above and wasting  do a finger prick glucose\nis recognised by the pitting oedema of both test and check with shCP\nfeet; they may also present with general body\nswelling. If you are concerned that a child might\nbe malnourished look rapidly at the child’s feet and check for WARnInG\noedema of both feet. These children often appear listless and If glucose is less than\napathetic and have skin and hair changes. They are also prone 3 mmol/L  move\nto hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to be to resuscitation\nmanaged urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition\nguid"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0055", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0056_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the urgent management considerations for children with severe malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "e skin and hair changes. They are also prone 3 mmol/L  move\nto hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to be to resuscitation\nmanaged urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition\nguidelines to prevent complications.\n22 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 23\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Triage Early Warning Score TEWS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0056", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0057_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should the Triage Early Warning Score (TEWS) be used to determine a child's triage category?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "5 TRIAGE EARLY WARnInG sCORE TEWs\n• If any URGENT signs have been found the TEWS should be calculated and additional\ninvestigations checked to ensure that the child does not need to be assigned to the\nRED or ORAnGE category and taken for emergency or very urgent care. Learning Objectives:\n• Children within the Yellow category should be seen within 60 minutes.\n• Be familiar with the different age appropriate versions of the TEWS\n• Always check for additional tasks that should be done.\n• If there are no URGENT signs, calculate the TEWS and check for additional investigations to • Understand how to calculate the TEWS in paediatric and adult patients\ndetermine what the child’s final triage colour is.\nThe TEWS is a composite score representing physiologic parameters at triage. There are different age\nappropriate versions: the younger child TEWs is for patients smaller than 95cm or younger than 3 year s\nAssessment Questions\n(see Figure 12); the older child TEWs is for patients 96cm to 150cm or 3 years to around 12 years (see Figur e\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\n13); and the adult TEWs is for patients older than 12 years or taller than 150cm. Older children, wher e\n1. Grunting and nasal flaring are signs of severe respiratory distress. you are unsure which form to use, should be measured. If they are over 150cm then the adult versio n\nshould be used. This standardised scoring system has been validated and many of the boxes in the TEW S\nTrue Fa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0057", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0058_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does c-c-D stand for in the ABC-c-c-DO approach?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ure which form to use, should be measured. If they are over 150cm then the adult versio n\nshould be used. This standardised scoring system has been validated and many of the boxes in the TEW S\nTrue False\ncalculwr are shaded grey. These boxes cannot be assigned a score. This means that for temperature, fo r\nexample, it is only possible to score 0 or 2 points, depending on the value of the recording.\n2. Oxygen saturation levels should always be checked in a child that looks blue.\nTrue False YOUnGER YOUnGER ChILD TEWs\nThAn 3 YEARs\n/ sMALLER\n3. If no emergency sign is identified in step one, but an urgent sign is identified in step two, the ThAn 95 cm 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\npatient is immediately taken to the majors area for urgent management.\nUnable to\nTrue False Mobility Normal move as\nfor age\nnormal\nChoose the correct answer:\nless than 50 or\n4. In the ABC-c-c-DO approach ABC stands for airway, breathing, circulation. RR 20 20 - 25 26 - 39 40 - 49 more\nWhat does c-c-D stand for?\nless than 160 or\n(a) convulsions, chest pain, dehydration hR 70 70 - 79 80 - 130 131 - 159 more\n(b) coma, cancer, disabilities\nFeels Feels Hot\n(c) coma, convulsions, dehydration\nTemp Cold 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\n(d) coma, craniopharyngioma, dehydration Under 35˚ 38.4˚\n(e) chronic pain, constipation, dehydration\nReacts to Reacts to Unres-\nAVPU Alert\n5. The following are emergency signs Voice Pain ponsive\n(a) oxygen saturation levels more than 92% Trauma No Yes\n(b) facial or inhalation burn Figure 12: Younger Child TEWS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0058", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0059_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the emergency signs in children younger than 3 years according to the Younger Child TEWS?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "eacts to Unres-\nAVPU Alert\n5. The following are emergency signs Voice Pain ponsive\n(a) oxygen saturation levels more than 92% Trauma No Yes\n(b) facial or inhalation burn Figure 12: Younger Child TEWS (younger than 3 years)\n(c) stridor, snoring and secretions\n(d) closed fracture\n3 to 12 OLDER ChILD TEWs\n(e) b and c above YEARs OLD /\n95 to 150 cm\ntall 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\nUnable to\nNormal\nMobility walk as\nfor age\nnormal\nless than 27 or\nRR 15 -16 17 - 21 22 - 26\n15 more\nless than 130 or\nhR 60 - 79 80 - 99 100 - 129\n60 more\nFeels Feels Hot\nTemp Cold 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ 38.4˚\nReacts to Reacts to Unres-\nAVPU Confused Alert\nVoice Pain ponsive\nTrauma No Yes\nFigure 13: Older Child TEWS (age 3 - 12 years)\n24 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 25"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0059", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0060_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How is mobility assessed in the Triage Early Warning Score (TEWS) for children and adults?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage Early Warning Score TEWS Triage Early Warning Score TEWS\nMOBILITY\nOLDER ThAn ADULT TEWs\n12 YEARs / Mobility is observed by noting the mode in which the patient has to be mobilised. A small baby is\nTALLER ThAn carried by the carer – as might a small infant or child. You need to assess if the level of movement\n150 cm tall) 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\nis normal for that particular child. In the event that the child or adult is in a wheelchair due to\nStretcher/ permanent paralysis 2 points are given for mobility as the child in a wheelchair is more at risk.\nMobility Walking With Help\nImmobile\nTRAUMA\nless than more\nRR 9 - 14 15 - 20 21 - 29 Trauma is present if there is ANY injury to the patient within the past 48 hours.\n9 than 29\nless than more 5.2 Terminology and key concepts\nhR 41 - 50 51 - 100 101 - 110 111 - 129\n41 than 129 1. Confusion: An older child may be reported to have confused behaviour or this may be\nLess than more discovered at triage. A confused child appears disorientated, s/he may not be interacting\nsBP 71 71 - 80 81-100 101 -199 than 199 normally with the carer or the environment, s/he may be delirious or hallucinating. A confused\nchild may be talking a lot and aggressive or s/he may be quiet and fearful looking. This sign is\nHot OR\nCold OR difficult to detect in younger children who are usually pre-verbal - so it is only included on the\nTemp 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ TEWS for the older child.\n38.4˚\n2. Acute vs Chronic: Many patients in South Africa are chronically un"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0060", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0061_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should vital signs be recorded for younger and older children in the TEWS to ensure accurate scoring?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "hildren who are usually pre-verbal - so it is only included on the\nTemp 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ TEWS for the older child.\n38.4˚\n2. Acute vs Chronic: Many patients in South Africa are chronically unwell with serious conditions\nAVPU Confused Alert Reacts to Reacts to Unres- such as TB or HIV/AIDS. In these patients, daily resting physiology such as respiratory rate may\nVoice Pain ponsive be abnormal. This is important to bear in mind, as their TEWS will be high and they will be given\nTrauma No Yes an overly high triage code (they may be overtriaged). It is not for the inexperienced triage\nprovider to decide whether this is the case or not, as sick patients may be missed. However, it is\nFigure 14: Adult TEWS (older than 12 years) appropriate to ask a senior doctor or sister to review the patient’s triage code.\n5.1 Observations at triage 3. The younger and older child: Younger and older children have different resting vital signs to\nadults. These are often difficult to obtain, and the patients often cry, which pushes up their\nTo calculate the TEWS the first step is to perform the DO nOT UPsET ThE ChILD respiratory and heart rate. The values still need to be recorded as you measure them, so that\nobservations required by the TEWS. this affects your observations for TEWS a correct score can be given in the TEWS, but tell the senior doctor or sister that the child was\ncrying when they were recorded. These staff members can then decide whether the child has\nREsPIRATORY RATE\nbeen ov"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0061", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0062_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child's respiratory rate be measured according to the guidelines?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ore can be given in the TEWS, but tell the senior doctor or sister that the child was\ncrying when they were recorded. These staff members can then decide whether the child has\nREsPIRATORY RATE\nbeen overtriaged or not.\nThe patient’s respiratory rate is calculated by counting the breaths for 30 seconds and then multiplying\nby two. This should be done at the first opportunity when the child is quiet. If the child is small it is bes t Assessment Questions\nto leave him/her in the mother’s lap. Do not undress the child to count the respiratory rate as doing this Clearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\nmay upset him/her. Count the breathing rate by observing rather than touching the child. 1. If a child is nine years old we use the older child TEWS.\nhEART RATE True False\nTo measure the heart rate, the triage provider can manually count the heart rate by feeling the pulse\n2. Always undress a child and weigh them so that it is easier to obtain their vital signs.\nfor 30 seconds and then multiplying by two. Alternatively a heart rate monitor can be used (ideally\nnot from a saturation monitor – as the heart rate on these monitors can be unrelaible and can True False\nchange frequently if the child moves).\n3. To accurately obtain a respiratory rate always start when the patient is at rest, count\nTEMPERATURE respirations for 30 seconds and multiply by two.\nThe temperature is measured using either an electronic or mercury thermometer (preferably a low-\nTrue False"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0062", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0063_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the components of the adult Track and Trigger Early Warning Score (TEWS)?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "n the patient is at rest, count\nTEMPERATURE respirations for 30 seconds and multiply by two.\nThe temperature is measured using either an electronic or mercury thermometer (preferably a low-\nTrue False\nreading thermometer). Rectal temperature should NOT be taken routinely.\nAVPU Choose the correct answer:\nAVPU is done by seeing if the child is alert. If not obviously alert then the triage provider needs to 4. The adult TEWS consists of the following parameters:\nobserve the child’s response to their (or the parent’s) voice. If the child responds to voice and is then (a) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\nalert – he is an ‘A’ – but if he remains drowsy\n(b) Mobility, capillary refill time, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\nthen he is a ‘V’’. If he makes no response to V means the patient is not fully alert and\n(c) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, systolic blood pressure, AVPU and trauma\nvoice then the triage provider need to assess REsPOnDs OnLY TO YOUR VOICE. Another way\nthe response to a painful stimulus (e.g. nailbed of describing this = ‘lethargic’ (d) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, AVPU and trauma\npressure or sternal rub) – if the child responds to (e) Mobility, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation level and blood glucose concentration\nP means the patient is not alert\nthis s/he is a ‘P’. If there is no response to either\nand does not respond to your voice but\nverbal or pain stimuli the patie"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0063", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0064_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What additional investigation is required for patients with a blood glucose concentration of 11 mmol/L or more?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ion level and blood glucose concentration\nP means the patient is not alert\nthis s/he is a ‘P’. If there is no response to either\nand does not respond to your voice but\nverbal or pain stimuli the patient is labelled as 5. The older child TEWS consists of the following parameters:\nREsPOnDs OnLY TO A PAInFUL sTIMULUs\nunresponsive. (a) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\n(b) Mobility, capillary refill time, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\n(c) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and trauma\n(d) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, AVPU and trauma\n(e) Mobility, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation level and blood glucose concentration\n26 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 27\nAdditional Investigations Additional Investigations\nDIABETEs AnD hYPERGLYCAEMIA\n6 ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOns ADDITIOnAL\n(GLUCOTEST 11 OR MORE)\n! InVEsTIGATIOn\nAll patients with a glucose concentration of 11 mmol/L or Perform a urine dipstick to\nLearning Objectives: more require a urine dipstick to check for ketones. check for ketones\n• Be familiar with the additional investigations and when to perform them ChILD hAs MALnUTRITIOn WITh sEVERE VIsIBLE\nWAsTInG or WITh PITTInG OEDEMA OF BOTh FEET\n• Know how additional investigations may change the triage priority level\nThis child is at risk of hypoglycaemia, as well as hypothermia\nWARnInG\nFor all patients (especially children) immediate additional investigations may be indica"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0064", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0065_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the indications for performing a finger prick glucotest in children during triage?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ons may change the triage priority level\nThis child is at risk of hypoglycaemia, as well as hypothermia\nWARnInG\nFor all patients (especially children) immediate additional investigations may be indicated to identify ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn If glucose is below 3 mmol/L\n!\npotentially serious complications of their presenting conditions. Check if the patient has any of the Perform a finger prick glucotest  move to resuscitation and\nconditions listed below and act accordingly. immediately to exclude hypoglycaemia hand over to SHCP\nRESPIRATORY RATE (RR) SCORES MORE THAN\n1 POInT On TEWs:\nWARnInG ABDOMInAL PAIn OR BACKAChE In FEMALEs\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn For children if oxygen saturation PERFORM A FInGER PRICK The adult female with abdominal pain may have an ectopic\n! Measure oxygen saturation (for is below 92% on room air  GLUCOTEsT In ThE pregnancy leading to severe pain and discomfort as time\nchildren on finger, toe, hand or Move to resuscitation area and FOLLOWInG CAsEs: progresses. She may be at risk for a ruptured ectopic or othe r\nearlobe, depending on available administer nasal prong or • Reduced level of complications in pregnancy requiring emergency surgery.\nsaturation probe and co-operation) facemask oxygen consciousness These additional investigations may rapidly identify\n• Unable to sit or move as usual very ill patients and change their category. They also\nA raised RR for age may indicate serious underlying pathology such as chest infection requiring prevent and"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0065", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0066_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key signs that indicate a child may have a serious underlying medical condition requiring immediate investigation?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "tify\n• Unable to sit or move as usual very ill patients and change their category. They also\nA raised RR for age may indicate serious underlying pathology such as chest infection requiring prevent and/or\nsupplemental oxygen • Current or recent seizure identify serious ADDITIOnAL\n• Known with diabetes complications. ! InVEsTIGATIOn\nREDUCED LEVEL OF COnsCIOUsnEss ADDITIOnAL Perform a urine dipstick\n• Severe malnutrition\nAll patients that are not fully alert (i.e. confused or only ! InVEsTIGATIOn and urine pregnancy test\nresponding to verbal or painful stimulus) need to have a Perform a finger prick\nfinger prick glucotest done and should be handed over glucotest immediately to Assessment Questions\nto the senior healthcare professional. exclude hypoglycaemia\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\nIf the child is not alert, or the caregiver volunteers that\nthe child is more sleepy than normal, this may indicate WARnInG 1. A patient with a glucose of 11 mmol/L needs to have a urine dipstick done to check for\na serious evolving medical condition such as meningitis If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  ketones in the urine.\nor if associated with a history of trauma there could be move to resuscitation and True False\na traumatic brain injury. hand over to SHCP\nAny sick child who has not been feeding well or 2. Check the finger prick haemoglobin on all patients that have a history of diabetes.\nhas been vomiting may become drowsy because of a low blood sugar level"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0066", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0067_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the indications for performing a finger prick glucotest in children and patients with diabetes?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ick child who has not been feeding well or 2. Check the finger prick haemoglobin on all patients that have a history of diabetes.\nhas been vomiting may become drowsy because of a low blood sugar level. Tiny babies and\nTrue False\nmalnourished children are particularly at risk of hypoglycaemia.\n3. Do a urine dipstick and urine pregnancy test on all adult females presenting with abdominal\nUnABLE TO sIT OR MOVE As nORMAL\nADDITIOnAL pain.\nIf the patient is unable to walk or move as normal or the ! InVEsTIGATIOn\ncaregiver reports that the child is lethargic or unable to Perform a finger prick True False\nmove as usual, this may also be a sign of serious illness or glucotest immediately to\nof a low blood sugar level. exclude hypoglycaemia Choose the correct answer:\nRECEnT sEIZURE/FIT 4. Perform a finger prick glucotest on the following cases:\nThe patient who is actively fitting will have been taken WARnInG (a) Current or recent seizure\nto the resuscitation area. In any child with a history of If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  (b) Facial burn\nrecent seizure there could be easily identifiable and move to resuscitation and (c) Reduced level of consciousness\ntreatable causes, including hypoglycaemia, pyrexia hand over to SHCP\n(d) History of diabetes\n(febrile seizure) or high blood pressure.\n(e) a, c and d\nhIsTORY OF DIABETEs\nAll patients with diabetes are at risk of either becoming hypoglyacaemic (usually drowsy or confused) o r 5. The following two additional investigations may upgrade a p"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0067", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0068_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the additional investigations that may upgrade a patient with diabetes to the Red category?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "a, c and d\nhIsTORY OF DIABETEs\nAll patients with diabetes are at risk of either becoming hypoglyacaemic (usually drowsy or confused) o r 5. The following two additional investigations may upgrade a patient to the Red category:\nhyperglycaemic with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). All therefore need a glucotest done at presentation. (a) Oxygen saturation levels in children and finger prick haemoglobin\n(b) Finger prick haemoglobin levels and urine dipstick\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn WARnInG\n! Perform a finger prick glucotest If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  (c) Urine dipstick test and oxygen saturation levels\nimmediately to exclude move to resuscitation and (d) Finger prick glucotest and oxygen saturation levels\nhypoglycaemia hand over to SHCP (e) Finger prick glucotest and finger prick haemoglobin\nIf glucose is hi  hand over to SHCP\n28 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 29\nAdditional Tasks Additional Tasks\nVOMITInG WIThOUT DIARRhOEA AnD ThE ChILD Is DEhYDRATED\n7 ADDITIOnAL TAsKs ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nVomiting alone which is severe enough to result in dehydration\nVomiting without\nmay indicate a diagnosis other than simple gastroenteritis e.g. diarrhoea  refer to\nLearning Objectives: For all patients (especially children) whether triaged urinary tract infection; a surgical problem with bowel obstruction,; shCP for assessment\ndiabetic ketoacidosis or even meningitis or another severe\nRED, ORAnGE, YELLOW OR GREEn immediate\n• Be familiar with the additional tasks additional ta"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0068", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0069_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should senior advice be sought before starting oral rehydration in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "lem with bowel obstruction,; shCP for assessment\ndiabetic ketoacidosis or even meningitis or another severe\nRED, ORAnGE, YELLOW OR GREEn immediate\n• Be familiar with the additional tasks additional tasks may be indicated to stabilise the infection. It may not be appropriate to automatically commence\nthis child on a trial of oral rehydration – and so senior advice should be sought.\n• Know when to perform additional tasks patient and/or identify or prevent potentially serious"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0069", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
