{"id": "qa_sats_0071_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the immediate management for a child presenting with chest pain?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "symptoms are often non-specific, they have lower immunity than Tiny baby under pain - both medication and wheelchair or stretcher if required\nother children so are more prone to infections, and they deteriorate 2 months  immobilization of the affected\nmore quickly. They therefore need to be assessed as a priority. refer to shCP limb will provide some relief from the pain and should be initiated prior to formal assessment\nPOIsOnInG OR OVERDOsE ADDITIOnAL TAsK ChEsT PAIn\nToddlers are inquisitive and like to explore their environments. ADDITIOnAL TAsK Chest Pain  perform an Patients with chest pain may be having an acute\nThey may accidentally ingest a variety of household substances, Poisoning /overdose  ECG to rule out potential myocardial infarct (AMI). An immediate ECG is required to\npesticides or medications. A child who has ingested a poison refer to shCP AMI and hand over to SHCP rule out a potential AMI.\ncan deteriorate quickly. They may require a specific antidote\nand if a poison or medication has been ingested recently ACTIVE OnGOInG BLEEDInG ADDITIOnAL TAsK\ni c m h m ar e c d o i a a l t ) e . It in i t s e t r h v e e r n e t f i o o r n e m im a p y o b rt e a n n t e t e o d c e o d n ( s e u . l g t . a a S c H t C iva P t f e o d r children ADDITIOnAL TAsK Children have a small circulating blood A to c t t h iv e e s b ite le e o d f t r  au a m p a p l w y i d th ir e a c d t r p y r e d s re su ss re in g\nand adults even if the patient appears stable. Pain o"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0071", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0072_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should analgesia be initiated in children and adults presenting with pain or inconsolable crying?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "have a small circulating blood A to c t t h iv e e s b ite le e o d f t r  au a m p a p l w y i d th ir e a c d t r p y r e d s re su ss re in g\nand adults even if the patient appears stable. Pain or inconsolable volume, and are at risk of becoming and perform finger prick haemoglobin to\ncrying  check with shocked quickly if there is ongoing bleeding obtain a baseline and hand over to SHCP\nIF ChILD APPEARs TO BE In PAIn or Is InCOnsOLABLY CRYInG shCP for initiation of from a trauma site\nThis may be due to a medical cause like an ear infection or analgesia and review hIsTORY OF RECEnT BLEEDInG - EIThER ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nfrom severe headache due to meningitis. Or there may be an RECTAL, ORAL OR FROM A sITE OF TRAUMA history of bleeding  check finger prick\nobvious cause such as a fracture or laceration. Pain is obviously unpleasant for both the child and This patient may be anaemic from blood loss haemoglobin.\ncarer. It is good practice to initiate analgesia as soon as possible for children and adults. If less than 8 g/dl then hand over to SHCP\nBURn PREsEnTInG COMPLAInT Is ABDOMInAL PAIn\nThe patient will be experiencing pain and especially ADDITIOnAL TAsK Abdominal pain is a common complaint in ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nchildren have the potential to deteriorate rapidly Pain  check with shCP for children and it has a wide variety of possible causes including For a younger child\nfrom significant fluid losses and may develop initiation of analgesia and review urinary tract infection, dia"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0072", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0073_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the immediate management for a burn that occurred within the last 3 hours in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "CP for children and it has a wide variety of possible causes including For a younger child\nfrom significant fluid losses and may develop initiation of analgesia and review urinary tract infection, diabetic ketoacidosis, hepatitis or other  Place a urine bag,\nhypothermia. causes. A urine dipstick for children and adults will assist in if olderchild, give\nexcluding or diagnosing these. container\nIf the burn is recent (<3hrs) immediate intervention may limit the extent of tissue damage.\nThese additional tasks may assist to rapidly identify very ill patients and\nADDITIOnAL TAsK prevent and identify serious complications. They also improve quality of care by providing relief from pain.\nIf the burn occurred recently (within 3hrs)  it is still worthwhile to cool the burnt area with\nwater, for example, by running cool tap water over the burnt area for 30 minutes. The child A senior healthcare professional should be alerted in the following cases:\nshould then be dried and wrapped in a clean sheet or blanket to avoid hypothermia . • Tiny baby younger than 2 months old • Very high or very low temperatures\nThe burn can be covered in cling wrap if available, or a clean dry sheet or towel will also be suitable. • Reduced level of consciousness • Vomiting only with dehydration\n• A child in pain including fractures and burns • The severely malnourished child\nTEMPERATURE 38.5˚C OR MORE • Poison ingestion or overdose • A child with active bleeding\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nA very high temperature will"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0073", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0074_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the immediate and additional tasks for managing a child with a temperature of 38.5°C or more during triage?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in including fractures and burns • The severely malnourished child\nTEMPERATURE 38.5˚C OR MORE • Poison ingestion or overdose • A child with active bleeding\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nA very high temperature will result in physiological\nhigh temperature  remove excessive\nchanges that may affect the TEWS, and make the Additional Tasks at triage\nclothing and check with shCP for\npatient feel uncomfortable and in some children initiation of analgesia and review ADULT PATIEnTs\nmay be associated with a febrile seizure.\nPROBLEM IMMEDIATE TAsKs\nTEMPERATURE 35.5˚C OR LESS ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n1. Temperature 38.5° or more Paracetamol 1 g orally stat (document in the notes)\nHypothermia might be a sign of severe sepsis with Low temperature  warm\nvery small babies, ex-premature babies and severely the patient with additional 2. Temperature 35° or less Warm the patient with blankets if available\nmalnourished children being the most at risk. blankets for children with a 3. Diabetes and hyperglycaemia Urine dipstick to check for ketones\ncap if available and hand over to SHCP (glucotest 11 mmol/L or more)\nDIARRhOEA & VOMITInG\n4. History of bleeding Finger prick haemoglobin\nEven if the child has no or some dehydration, the child ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n5. Bleeding PR, PO or from the site of trauma Finger prick haemoglobin\nis at risk of becoming dehydrated whilst waiting to be Diarrhoea & vomiting  take child\nseen and should receive oral rehydration to treat and/o r to ORT corner and advise caregiver 6. Abdominal pa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0074", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0075_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the immediate tasks for a child with a burn in a pre-hospital triage setting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in\nis at risk of becoming dehydrated whilst waiting to be Diarrhoea & vomiting  take child\nseen and should receive oral rehydration to treat and/o r to ORT corner and advise caregiver 6. Abdominal pain or backache in males Urine dipsticks\nprevent further dehydration to start ORT by cup and spoon 7. PV bleeding Urine dipsticks, Urine preganancy test\nFinger prick haemoglobin\n30 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 31\nAdditional Tasks Triage in Context\nPAEDIATRIC PATIEnTs 8 TRIAGE In COnTEXT\nPROBLEM IMMEDIATE TAsKs\n1. Poisoning OR overdose Refer to Senior Healthcare Professional (SHCP) 8.1 Pre-hospital\n2. Child in pain OR inconsolable crying Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation The use of triage within a pre-hospital setting is generally well accepted and understood universally .\n3. Child with a burn Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation Whilst this need is quite obvious (even critical) during major incidents, it’s the daily application of triage\nIf burn occurred within 3 hours, cool the burnt area principles in the despatch of ambulance resources where it has the most benefit. In these scenarios ,\nCover burn in clingwrap or clean dry sheet"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0075", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0076_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the EMS triage priorities and actions for common pediatric conditions in the pre-hospital setting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "triage permits the EMS despatcher to apply rules based decision making to what is an otherwise\n4. Temperature 38.5 ° or more Remove excessive clothing & discuss with SHCP impossible choice.\n5. Temperature 35 ° or less Warm the child with blankets if available. Refer to SHCP\nThe pre-hospital use of triage in the field varies from region to region, but is generally categorised into fou r\n6. Diarrhoea Take to ORT corner and advise caregiver to give ORT by cup priorities (represented by the colours red, yellow, green and blue). Such triage typically uses instability o f\nand spoon vital signs to differentiate high from low priority patients. Discrepancies in triage appear when personne l\n7. Vomiting without diarrhoea but with dehydration Refer to SHCP for assessment of differing levels of medical experience and qualifications need to assess patients as there are no clea r\ndefinitions of ‘unstable’ physiology. The terms ‘stable’ and ‘unstable’ are poorly understood and fail to\n8. Presenting complaint - abdominal pain younger child - urine bag / older child - urine container\naccurately reflect the patient’s clinical condition.\n9. Closed fracture Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation\nAccurate pre-hospital triage is essential for appropriate call out of secondary resources;, accurate\nImmobilize affected limb with a simple padded splint or\ntriagular bandage notification of receiving hospitals, and quality assessment and audit of the ambulance service. This i s\nparticularly pertinent in aeromedical callout requests and use of this specialised resource. For these\n10. Active ongoing bleeding Apply pressure to the site of trauma\nreasons triage tools based on objective physiological discriminators are essential.\nPerform finger prick haemoglobin to obtain a baseline\nRefer to SHCP\n8.2 Patient streaming\n11. History of recent bleeding Perform finger prick haemoglobin"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0076", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0077_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should patients be streamed based on their acuity level in an emergency facility?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage assigns the patient to an acuity level, which then dictates the amount of time the patient can wa it\nIf less than 8 g/dl then refer to SHCP\nsafely before being seen: Red immediate, Orange within 10 minutes, Yellow within an hour and Green within\nfour hours. It is the recommended practice to “stream” these patient categories to different areas and /\nAssessment Questions or healthcare providers within the facility. The normal streaming pattern would be Reds to resus, Orange s\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false: and Yellows to Majors and Greens to Minors, which would be manned by staff dedicated to these area s.\nFor the most part, streaming patients according to acuity will also stream them according to resourc e\n1. A tiny baby under two months should always be referred to the SHCP once they have been use: full monitoring, access to high-powered drugs and interventions with full team response is necessa ry\ncomprehensively triaged. to effectively treat the Red patient, while a room with a chair and a single practitioner may be all th at\nTrue False is necessary to see and treat the patients in the Green stream. How and where patients are streame d\ndepends on the load, manning and infrastructure of the Emergency Centre or Health Facility.\n2. A patient with an active ongoing bleed should place the bleeding area under running water. It is important in any system that prioritises order-to-be-seen by anything other than “first-come-\nfirst-served” to have a plan to see the lower priority patients. Streaming is a possible mechanism to\nTrue False\nachieve this. Without streaming, the Green patient will keep being pushed to the back of the queue\n3. A child that is vomiting only with no diarrhoea should first be referred to the SHCP for further by the patient of higher acuity, who by necessity should be seen first. With streaming, the higher\nassessment before commencing the child on a trial oral rehydration. acuity patients are seen before they die while the lower acuity patients are seen in another area\nbefore they leave!\nTrue False\n8.3 Infrastructure"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0077", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0078_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the appropriate initial management steps for a child with a burn injury from boiling oil?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage is a process, not a place, but for the most part will need an area for triage of those patients not\nChoose the correct answer:\nsent directly through to resus or majors. This area should allow for privacy and be set up in order to\n4. In a child with a burn injury from boiling oil that occurred 20 minutes ago: perform the vital signs for the TEWS, additional investigations and tasks.\n(a) The burnt area may be covered in cling wrap if available The room should preferably allow for one-way flow of patients from the waiting room into a\n(b) Cool running tap water over the burnt area for 30 minutes may subwaiting area in the area to which they have been streamed. If not possible, some sort of\nlimit the extent of tissue damage demarcation of the area or patients should be made in order to separate those already triaged from\n(c) The burnt area should be wrapped in a clean sheet to prevent hypothermia those who have not been assessed by the triage officer.\n(d) a and b\n8.4 Alignment of staff to temporal flow of patients\n(e) a,b and c\nIt is important that triage, the tool to ensure that patients are assessed timeously, does not become\nthe bottleneck in the system. Importantly, if the doctor is ready for the next patient, but cannot see\n5. The senior healthcare professional should be consulted in the following cases:\nthem as they are “first getting triaged”, the object is being defeated!\n(a) Poison ingestion and overdose\nMore often the bottleneck occurs due to failure to align staff with the flow of patients. For the most\n(b) A severely malnourished child\npart, it is possible to predict the times of day when the flow of patients into the EC is heavier (typical\n(c) Vomiting only with dehydration “saddle-shaped” curve). Staffing for triage needs to reflect this flow.\n(d) a and c\nThere should also be some sort of plan in place to deal with unexpected influx of patients: each\n(e) a, b and c facility should have an upper limit of patients that they are willing to have waiting for triage, over\nwhich a contingency plan needs to be activated: eg a regional hospital has an agreement that\nif there are five or more patients needing triage, a nurse is called from majors to help triage the\npatients until the level is back down to less than five waiting.\n32 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 33\nSummary and References\n9 sUMMARY"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0078", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0079_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the purpose and validation of the South African Triage Scale (SATS)?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage is an essential first step in efficient and effective emergency care –whether on the roadside\nor in the public or private hospital arena. A robust triage tool will help to save lives and reduce\nmorbidity. The South African Triage Scale has been derived by a panel of experts in Emergency\nMedicine (doctors, nurses and paramedics), and is scientifically proven. It has been shown to improve\nwaiting times and make the emergency centre run more smoothly. However, attention needs to be\npaid to those patients triaged Green, especially in peak times, and the SATG recommends the use of\nstreaming with a clinical nurse practitioner or doctor to see this group.\nThe SATS has been validated as part of a Masters in Philosophy (MPhil) with 700 public sector patients,\nan MPhil with 2000 private sector patients and a PhD. Feedback following publication in four major\njournals has contributed to the process.\nThis is Edition 3 and we accept that the tool may not be perfect, that is why your feedback is so\nimportant. In addition, there will be ongoing research aimed at keeping the tool accurate and\nappropriate. If necessary, subsequent editions will follow.\nOnline resources:\nFor further information please visit\nwww.emssa.org.za/sats\nContact details:\nMichele Twomey\nSATS Implementation Advisor\n082 850 3281\nmicheletwomey@gmail.com\n10 REFEREnCEs\n1. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC.Effect of introduction of nurse triage on waiting times in a South African emergency\ndepartment. Emerg Med J 2008;25:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0079", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0080_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the reliability of the South African Triage Scale (adult version) when used by healthcare professionals?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "850 3281\nmicheletwomey@gmail.com\n10 REFEREnCEs\n1. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC.Effect of introduction of nurse triage on waiting times in a South African emergency\ndepartment. Emerg Med J 2008;25:395-397.\n2. Twomey M, Wallis LA, Thompson ML, Myers JE. The South African Triage Scale (adult version) provides reliable acuity\nratings. Int Emerg Nurs. Published Online First: 19 September 2011.doi:10.1016/j.ienj.2011.08.002.\n3. Twomey M, Wallis LA, Thompson ML, Myers JE. The South African Triage Scale (adult version) provides valid acuity\nratings when used by doctors and enrolled nursing assistants. African J Emerg Med 2012;2:3-12.\n4. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC. A prospective evaluation of the Cape triage score in the emergency department of\nan urban public hospital in South Africa. Emerg Med J. 2008;25:398-402.\n5. Policy for implementation of the triage of patients in Western Cape emergency units. Circular H7 of 2006.\nDepartment of Health, Provincial Government, update 2012.\n6. Gottschalk S, Wood D, De Vries S, Wallis L, Bruijns S, On behalf of the Cape Triage Group . The cape triage score: a\nnew triage system South Africa. Proposal from the Cape triage group. Emerg Med J 2006;23:149-153.\n7. Augustyn J. The South African Triage Scale: a tool for emergency nurses. Prof Nurs Today 2011;15:24–29.\n8. Isaacs AA, Hellenberg D. Implementing a structured triage system at a community health centre (CHC) using\nKaizen. SA Fam Pract. 2009;51\n9. Goudge J, Cornell J, McIntyre D, et al."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0080", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0081_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the inter-rater reliability of the South African Triage Scale when used by different cadres of health care workers?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "day 2011;15:24–29.\n8. Isaacs AA, Hellenberg D. Implementing a structured triage system at a community health centre (CHC) using\nKaizen. SA Fam Pract. 2009;51\n9. Goudge J, Cornell J, McIntyre D, et al. Private sector financing. In: Ntuli A, Suleman F, Barron P, McCoy D: editors.\nSouth African Health Review 2001. Durban:Health Systems Trust; 2001.\n10. Bradshaw D, Groenewald P, Laubscher R, Nannan N, Nojilana B, Norman R, Pieterse D and Schneider M. Initial\nBurden of Disease Estimates for South Africa, 2000. Cape Town: South African Medical Research Council, 2003.\n11. Iserson KV, Moskop JC. Triage in Medicine, Part I: Concept, History, and Types. Ann Emerg Med. 2007;49:275-281.\n12. FitzGerald G, Jelinek GA, Scott D, Gerdtz MF. Emergency department Triage revisited. Emerg Med J 2010;27:86-92.\n13. Moll HA. Challenges in the validation of Triage systems at emergency departments. J Clin Epi 2010;63:384-88.\n14. Twomey M, de Sa A, Wallis LA, Myers JE. Inter-rater reliability of the South African Triage Scale: Assessing two\ndifferent cadres of health care workers in a real time environment. African J Emerg Med 2011;1:113-118.\n34 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012\nDepartment of Health\nPO Box 2060, Cape Town, 8000\nFor more information contact:\nMichele Twomey (SATS Implementation Advisor)\nMobile: +27 850 3281 email: micheletwomey@gmail.com\nDepartmental Website: www.health.gov.za\nProvincial Website: www.westerncape.gov.za\nThe South African"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0081", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0000_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key signs to look for in identifying complications of measles in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "COMPLICATIONS**** If mouth ulcers, treat with gentian violet\nFollow-up in 3 days\nMeasles now or within the last 3 Green: Give Vitamin A treatment\nmonths. MEASLES\n(cid:13)(cid:3)(cid:55)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:91)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:53)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:91)(cid:76)(cid:80)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:19)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:131)(cid:38)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:17)\n**Look for local tenderness; oral sores; refusal to use a limb; hot tender swelling; red tender skin or boils; lower abdominal pain or pain on passing urine in older children.\n*** If no malaria test available: High malaria risk - classify as MALARIA; Low malaria risk AND NO obvious cause of fever - classify as MALARIA.\n**** Other important complications of measles - pneumonia, stridor, diarrhoea, ear infection, and acute malnu"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0000", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0001_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child with an ear problem be classified and managed according to the guidelines?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "classify as MALARIA; Low malaria risk AND NO obvious cause of fever - classify as MALARIA.\n**** Other important complications of measles - pneumonia, stridor, diarrhoea, ear infection, and acute malnutrition - are classified in other tables.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nDoes the child have an ear problem?\nIf yes, ask: Look and feel: Tender swelling behind the Pink: Give first dose of an appropriate antibiotic\nIs there ear pain? Look for pus draining from ear. MASTOIDITIS Give first dose of paracetamol for pain\nIs there ear discharge? the ear. Classify EAR PROBLEM Refer URGENTLY to hospital\nIf yes, for how long? Feel for tender swelling\nPus is seen draining from Yellow: Give an antibiotic for 5 days\nbehind the ear.\nthe ear and discharge is ACUTE EAR Give paracetamol for pain\nreported for less than 14 INFECTION Dry the ear by wicking\ndays, or Follow-up in 5 days\nEar pain.\nPus is seen draining from Yellow: Dry the ear by wicking\nthe ear and discharge is CHRONIC EAR Treat with topical quinolone eardrops for 14 days\nreported for 14 days or INFECTION Follow-up in 5 days\nmore.\nNo ear pain and Green: No treatment\nNo pus seen draining from NO EAR INFECTION\nthe ear.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nCHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION Oedema of both feet Pink: Give first dose appropriate antibiotic\nLOOK AND FEEL: Classify"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0001", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0002_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child with acute malnutrition and no medical complications be treated?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": ")(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nCHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION Oedema of both feet Pink: Give first dose appropriate antibiotic\nLOOK AND FEEL: Classify OR COMPLICATED Treat the child to prevent low blood\nLook for signs of acute malnutrition NUTRITIONAL WFH/L less than -3 z- SEVERE ACUTE sugar\nLook for oedema of both feet. STATUS scores OR MUAC less MALNUTRITION Keep the child warm\nDetermine WFH/L* ___ z-score. than 115 mm AND any Refer URGENTLY to hospital\nMeasure MUAC**____ mm in a child 6 months or older. one of the following:\nMedical\nIf WFH/L less than -3 z-scores or MUAC less than 115 complication present\nmm, then: or\nCheck for any medical complication present: Not able to finish RUTF\nAny general danger signs or\nAny severe classification Breastfeeding\nproblem.\nPneumonia with chest indrawing\nIf no medical complications present: WFH/L less than -3 z- Yellow: Give oral antibiotics for 5 days\nChild is 6 months or older, offer RUTF*** to scores UNCOMPLICATED Give ready-to-use therapeutic food for a child\neat. Is the child: OR SEVERE ACUTE aged 6 months or more\nMUAC less than 115 mm MALNUTRITION Counsel the mother on how to feed the child.\nNot able to finish RUTF portion? Assess for possible TB infection\nAND\nAble to finish RUTF portion? Advise mother when to return immediately\nAble to finish RUTF.\nFollow up in 7 days\nChild is less than 6 months, assess\nbreastfeeding: WFH/L between -3 and - Yellow: Assess the child's feeding and co"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0002", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0003_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a mother return immediately for a child with moderate acute malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "n? Advise mother when to return immediately\nAble to finish RUTF.\nFollow up in 7 days\nChild is less than 6 months, assess\nbreastfeeding: WFH/L between -3 and - Yellow: Assess the child's feeding and counsel the\n2 z-scores MODERATE ACUTE mother on the feeding recommendations\nDoes the child have a breastfeeding OR MALNUTRITION If feeding problem, follow up in 7 days\nproblem? MUAC 115 up to 125 mm. Assess for possible TB infection.\nAdvise mother when to return immediately\nFollow-up in 30 days\nWFH/L - 2 z-scores or Green: If child is less than 2 years old, assess the\nmore NO ACUTE child's feeding and counsel the mother on\nOR MALNUTRITION feeding according to the feeding\nrecommendations\nMUAC 125 mm or more.\nIf feeding problem, follow-up in 7 days\n*WFH/L is Weight-for-Height or Weight-for-Length determined by using the WHO growth standards charts.\n** MUAC is Mid-Upper Arm Circumference measured using MUAC tape in all children 6 months or older.\n***RUTF is Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food for conducting the appetite test and feeding children with severe acute malanutrition.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ANAEMIA\nCheck for anaemia Severe palmar pallor Pink: Refer URGENTLY to hopsital\nLook for palmar pallor. Is it: SEVERE ANAEMIA\nSevere palmar pallor*? Classify\nSome palmar pallor? ANAEMIA Classification Some pallor Yellow: Give iron**\narrow ANAEMIA Give mebendazole if child is 1 year or older and\nhas not"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0003", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0004_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child with severe palmar pallor be classified and managed for anemia?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Is it: SEVERE ANAEMIA\nSevere palmar pallor*? Classify\nSome palmar pallor? ANAEMIA Classification Some pallor Yellow: Give iron**\narrow ANAEMIA Give mebendazole if child is 1 year or older and\nhas not had a dose in the previous 6 months\nAdvise mother when to return immediately\nFollow-up in 14 days\nNo palmar pallor Green: If child is less than 2 years old, assess the\nNO ANAEMIA child's feeding and counsel the mother according\nto the feeding recommendations\nIf feeding problem, follow-up in 5 days\n*Assess for sickle cell anaemia if common in your area.\n**If child has severe acute malnutrition and is receiving RUTF, DO NOT give iron because there is already adequate amount of iron in RUTF.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR HIV INFECTION\nUse this chart if the child is NOT enrolled in HIV care.\nPositive virological test in Yellow: Initiate ART treatment and HIV care\nASK child CONFIRMED HIV Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis*\nClassify OR INFECTION (cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nHas the mother or child had an HIV test? HIV Positive serological test i"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0004", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0005_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended management for an HIV-exposed infant who is breastfeeding or was breastfed within 6 weeks before testing?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nHas the mother or child had an HIV test? HIV Positive serological test in a counselling to the mother\nstatus\nIF YES: child 18 months or older Advise the mother on home care\nDecide HIV status: Asess or refer for TB assessment and INH\nMother: POSITIVE or NEGATIVE preventive therapy\nChild: Follow-up regularly as per national guidelines\nVirological test POSITIVE or NEGATIVE Mother HIV-positive AND Yellow: Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis\nSerological test POSITIVE or NEGATIVE negative virological test in HIV EXPOSED Start or continue ARV prophylaxis as\na breastfeeding child or only recommended\nIf mother is HIV positive and child is negative or stopped less than 6 weeks Do virological test to confirm HIV status**\nunknown, ASK: ago (cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nWas the child breastfeeding at the time or 6 weeks before OR counselling to the mother\nthe test? Mother HIV-positive, child Advise the mother on home care\nIs the child breastfeeding now? not yet tested Follow-up regularly as per national guide"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0005", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0006_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What should be done if a mother is HIV-positive and the child is breastfeeding?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "6 weeks before OR counselling to the mother\nthe test? Mother HIV-positive, child Advise the mother on home care\nIs the child breastfeeding now? not yet tested Follow-up regularly as per national guidelines\nIf breastfeeding ASK: Is the mother and child on ARV\nOR"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0006", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0007_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should cotrimoxazole prophylaxis be given to HIV-exposed or HIV-infected children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "prophylaxis?\nPositive serological test in a\nIF NO, THEN TEST:\nchild less than 18 months\nMother and child status unknown: TEST mother. old\nMother HIV positive and child status unknown: TEST child.\nNegative HIV test in mother Green: Treat, counsel and follow-up existing infections\nor child HIV INFECTION\nUNLIKELY\n* Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis to all HIV infected and HIV-exposed children utill confirmed negative after cessation of breastfeeding.\n** If virological test is negative, repeat test 6 weeks after the breatfeeding has stopped; if serological test is positive, do a virological test as soon as possible.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK THE CHILD'S IMMUNIZATION, VITAMIN A AND DEWORMING STATUS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0007", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "prevention", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0008_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended immunization schedule for children, including the timing and types of vaccines and supplements?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "IMMUNIZATION SCHEDULE: Follow national guidelines\nAGE VACCINE\nBirth BCG* OPV-0 Hep B0 VITAMIN A\n6 weeks DPT+HIB-1 OPV-1 Hep B1 RTV1 PCV1*** SUPPLEMENTATION\nGive every child a\n10 weeks DPT+HIB-2 OPV-2 Hep B2 RTV2 PCV2 dose of Vitamin A\nevery six months\nfrom the age of 6\nmonths. Record the\ndose on the child's\nchart.\n14 weeks DPT+HIB-3 OPV-3 Hep B3 RTV3 PCV3 ROUTINE WORM"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0008", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "prevention", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0009_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How often should mebendazole be administered to children starting at one year of age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "TREATMENT\nGive every child\nmebendazole every 6\n9 months Measles **\nmonths from the age\nof one year. Record\nthe dose on the\n18 months DPT child's card.\n*Children who are HIV positive or unknown HIV status with symptoms consistent with HIV should not be vaccinated.\n**Second dose of measles vaccine may be given at any opportunistic moment during periodic supplementary immunization activities as early as one month following the first dose.\n***HIV-positive infants and pre-term neonates who have received 3 primary vaccine doses before 12 months of age may benefit from a booster dose in the second year of life.\nASSESS OTHER PROBLEMS:\nMAKE SURE CHILD WITH ANY GENERAL DANGER SIGN IS REFERRED after first dose of an appropriate antibiotic and other urgent treatments. Treat all children with a general danger sign to prevent low\nblood sugar.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:28)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nHIV TESTING AND INTERPRENTING RESULTS\nHIV testing is RECOMMENDED for:\n(cid:36)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:81)(cid:78)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(c"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0009", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0010_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Why is a positive serological HIV test not reliable for diagnosing HIV infection in children under 18 months of age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "3)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:16)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:86)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:44)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:92)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:78)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:76)(cid:85)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:12)\nTypes of HIV Tests\nWhat does the test detect? How to interpret the test?\nSEROLOGICAL These tests detect antibodies made by HIV antibodies pass from the mother to the child. Most antibodies have gone by 12 months of age, but in some instances they do not\nTESTS immune cells in response to HIV. disappear until the child is 18 months of age.\n(Including rapid They do not detect the HIV virus itself. This means that a positive serological test in children less than 18 months in NOT a reliable way to check for infection of the child"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0010", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0011_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the reliability of virological tests (such as PCR) for detecting HIV infection in children under 18 months of age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "of age.\n(Including rapid They do not detect the HIV virus itself. This means that a positive serological test in children less than 18 months in NOT a reliable way to check for infection of the child.\ntests)\nVIROLOGICAL These tests directly detect the presence of Positive virological (PCR) tests reliably detect HIV infection at any age, even before the child is 18 months old.\nTESTS the HIV virus or products of the virus in the If the tests are negative and the child has been breastfeeding, this does not rule out infection. The baby may have just become infected.\n(Including DNA blood. (cid:55)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:91)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:78)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:178)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0011", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0012_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should HIV antibody test results be interpreted in children less than 18 months of age who are breastfeeding?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:17)\nor RNA PCR)\nFor HIV exposed children 18 months or older, a positive HIV antibody test result means the child is infected.\nFor HIV exposed children less than 18 months of age:\nIf PCR or other virological test is available, test from 4 - 6 weeks of age.\nA positive result means the child is infected.\nA negative result means the child is not infected, but could become infected if they are still breast feeding.\nIf PCR or other virological test is not available, use HIV antibody test. A positive result is consistent with the fact that the child has been exposed to HIV, but does not tell us if the child is definitely infected.\nInterpreting the HIV Antibody Test Results in a Child less than 18 Months of Age\nBreastfeeding status POSITIVE (+) test NEGATIVE (-) test\nNOT BREASTFEEDING, and has not in HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they could be infected. HIV negative Child is not HIV infected\nlast 6 weeks Repeat test at 18 months.\nBREASTFEEDING HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they Child can still be infected by breastfeeding. Repeat test once breastfeeding has been\ncould be infected. Repeat test at 18 months or once discontin"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0012", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0013_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should an HIV-exposed or HIV-infected child be retested after discontinuing breastfeeding?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they Child can still be infected by breastfeeding. Repeat test once breastfeeding has been\ncould be infected. Repeat test at 18 months or once discontinued for more than 6 weeks.\nbreastfeeding has been discontinued for more than 6 weeks.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nWHO PAEDIATRIC STAGING FOR HIV INFECTION\n(cid:55)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:83)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:36)(cid:53)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:80)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:39)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0013", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0014_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the purpose of the clinical pathway for the management of severe dehydration in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:81)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:92)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:86)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:47)(cid:82)(cid:82)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:73)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:70)(cid:75)\n(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:39)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(ci"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0014", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0015_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the criteria for staging severe acute malnutrition in children according to the guideline?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "71)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:92)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:17)\nStage 1 Stage 2 Stage 3 Stage 4\nAsymptomatic Mild Disease Moderate Disease Severe Disease (AIDS)\n- - Unexplained severe Severe unexplained wasting/stunting/severe acute\nacute malnutrition not responding malnutrition not responding to standard therapy\nto standard therapy"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0015", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0016_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the symptoms and signs indicating advanced HIV disease in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Symptoms/Signs No symptoms, or only: Enlarged liver and/or spleen Oral thrush (outside neonatal Oesophageal thrush\nPersistent generalized Enlarged parotid period). More than one month of herpes simplex ulcerations.\nlymphadenopathy (PGL) Skin conditions (prurigo, seborraic dermatitis, extensive Oral hairy leukoplakia. Severe multiple or recurrent bacteria infections > 2\nmolluscum contagiosum or warts, fungal nail infection Unexplained and unresponsive episodes in a year (not including pneumonia) pneumocystis\nherpes zoster) to standard pneumonia (PCP)*\nMouth conditions recurrent mouth ulcerations, linea therapy: Kaposi's sarcoma.\ngingival Erythema) Diarhoea for over 14 days Extrapulmonary tuberculosis.\nRecurrent or chronic upper respiratory tract infections Fever for over 1 month Toxoplasma brain abscess*\n(sinusitis, ear infection, tonsilitis, Thrombocytopenia*(under Cryptococcal meningitis*\nortorrhea) 50,000/mm3 for 1month Acquired HIVassociated rectal\nNeutropenia* (under fistula\n500/mm3 for 1 month) HIV encephalopathy*\nAnaemia for over 1 month\n(haemoglobin under 8 gm)*\nRecurrent severe bacterial\npneumonia\nPulmonary TB\nLymp node TB\nSymptomatic lymphoid\ninterstitial pneumonitis (LIP)*\nAcute necrotising ulcerative\ngingivitis/periodontitis\nChronic HIV associated lung\ndiseses including\nbronchiectasis*\n*Conditions requiring diagnosis by a doctor or medical officer - should be referred for appropriate diagnosis and treatment.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:20)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE CHILD\nCARRY OUT THE TREATMENT STEPS IDENTIFIED ON THE ASSESS AND CLASSIFY CHART\nTEACH THE MOTHER TO GIVE ORAL DRUGS AT HOME\nFollow the instructions below for every oral drug to be given at home. Give an Appropriate Oral Antibiotic\nAlso follow the instructions listed with each drug's dosage table. FOR PNEUMONIA, ACUTE EAR INFECTION:\nFIRST-LINE ANTIBIOTIC: Oral Amoxicillin\nDetermine the appropriate drugs and dosage for the child's age or weight. AMOXICILLIN*\nTell the mother the reason for giving the drug to the child. Give two times daily for 5 days\nAGE or WEIGHT\nDemonstrate how to measure a dose. TABLET SYRUP\nWatch the mother practise measuring a dose by herself. 250 mg 250mg/5 ml\nAsk the mother to give the first dose to her child. 2 months up to 12 months (4 - <10 kg) 1 5 ml\nExplain carefully how to give the drug, then label and package the drug. 12 months up to 3 years (10 - <14 kg) 2 10 ml\nIf more than one drug will be given, collect, count and package each drug 3 years up to 5 years (14-19 kg) 3 15 ml\nseparately. * Amoxicillin is the recommended first-line drug of choice in the treatment of pneumonia due to its efficacy and\nincreasing high resistance to cotrimoxazole.\nExplain that all the oral drug tablets or syrups must be used to finish the course of\nFOR PROPHYLAXIS IN HIV CONFIRMED OR EXPOSED CHILD:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0016", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0017_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended dosage of oral ciprofloxacine for children aged 6 months to 5 years with dysentery?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment, even if the child gets better.\nANTIBIOTIC FOR PROPHYLAXIS: Oral Cotrimoxazole\nCheck the mother's understanding before she leaves the clinic.\nCOTRIMOXAZOLE\n(trimethoprim + sulfamethoxazole)\nAGE Give once a day starting at 4-6 weeks of age\nSyrup Paediatric tablet Adult tablet\n(40/200 mg/5ml) (Single strength 20/100 mg) (Single strength 80/400 mg)\nLess than 6 months 2.5 ml 1 -\n6 months up to 5 years 5 ml 2 1/2\nFOR DYSENTERY give Ciprofloxacine\nFIRST-LINE ANTIBIOTIC: Oral Ciprofloxacine\nCIPROFLOXACINE\nAGE Give 15mg/kg two times daily for 3 days\n250 mg tablet 500 mg tablet\nLess than 6 months 1/2 1/4\n6 months up to 5 years 1 1/2\nFOR CHOLERA:\nFIRST-LINE ANTIBIOTIC FOR CHOLERA: ____________________________________________________\nSECOND-LINE ANTIBIOTIC FOR CHOLERA: ____________________________________________________\nERYTHROMYCIN TETRACYCLINE\nGive four times daily for 3 days Give four times daily for 3 days\nAGE or WEIGHT\nTABLET TABLET\n250 mg 250 mg\n2 years up to 5 years (10 - 19 kg) 1 1\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:21)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTEACH THE MOTHER TO GIVE ORAL DRUGS AT HOME\nFollow the instructions below for every oral drug to be given at home. Give Oral Antimalarial for MALARIA\nAlso follow the instructions listed with each drug's dosage table.\nIf Artemether-Lumefantrine (AL)\nGive the first dose of artemether-lumefantrine in the clinic and observe for one hour. If the child\nvomits within an hour repe"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0017", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0018_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should Artemether-Lumefantrine be administered in children who vomit within one hour of the first dose?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ons listed with each drug's dosage table.\nIf Artemether-Lumefantrine (AL)\nGive the first dose of artemether-lumefantrine in the clinic and observe for one hour. If the child\nvomits within an hour repeat the dose.\nGive Inhaled Salbutamol for Wheezing Give second dose at home after 8 hours.\nThen twice daily for further two days as shown below.\nArtemether-lumefantrine should be taken with food.\nUSE OF A SPACER*\nIf Artesunate Amodiaquine (AS+AQ)\nA spacer is a way of delivering the bronchodilator drugs effectively into the lungs. No child under 5 years\nshould be given an inhaler without a spacer. A spacer works as well as a nebuliser if correctly used. Give first dose in the clinic and observe for an hour, if a child vomits within an hour repeat the\ndose.\n(cid:41)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:69)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:20)(cid:19)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:151)(cid:74)(cid:18)(cid:83)(cid:88)(cid:73)(cid:73)(cid:12)(cid:3)(cid:74)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:88)(cid:73)(cid:73)(cid:86)(cid:17)\nThen daily for two days as per table below using the fixed dose combination.\nRepeat up to 3 times every 15 minutes before classifying pneumonia.\nSpacers can be made in the following way: Artemether-Lumefantrine Artesunate plus Amodiaq"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0018", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0019_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a spacer be made for use with an inhaler in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "le below using the fixed dose combination.\nRepeat up to 3 times every 15 minutes before classifying pneumonia.\nSpacers can be made in the following way: Artemether-Lumefantrine Artesunate plus Amodiaquine tablets\nUse a 500ml drink bottle or similar. tablets Give Once a day for 3 days\n(20 mg artemether and 120\nCut a hole in the bottle base in the same shape as the mouthpiece of the inhaler.\nmg lumefantrine)\nThis can be done using a sharp knife. (25 mg AS/67.5 (50 mg AS/135 mg\nWEIGHT (age)\nCut the bottle between the upper quarter and the lower 3/4 and disregard the upper quarter of the Give two times daily for 3 mg AQ) AQ)\nbottle. days\nCut a small V in the border of the large open part of the bottle to fit to the child's nose and be used as Day Day\na mask. Day 1 Day 2 day 3 1 Day 2 Day 3 1 Day 2 Day 3\nFlame the edge of the cut bottle with a candle or a lighter to soften it.\n5 - <10 kg (2 months up\nIn a small baby, a mask can be made by making a similar hole in a plastic (not polystyrene) cup. 1 1 1 1 1 1 - - -\nto 12 months)\nAlternatively commercial spacers can be used if available.\n10 - <14 kg (12 months\n1 1 1 - - - 1 1 1\nTo use an inhaler with a spacer: up to 3 years)\nRemove the inhaler cap. Shake the inhaler well. 14 - <19 kg (3 years up to\n2 2 2 - - - 1 1 1\nInsert mouthpiece of the inhaler through the hole in the bottle or plastic cup. 5 years)\nThe child should put the opening of the bottle into his mouth and breath in and out through the mouth.\nA carer then presses down the"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0019", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0020_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a spacer be used with a metered-dose inhaler for children aged 5 years and younger?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "the inhaler through the hole in the bottle or plastic cup. 5 years)\nThe child should put the opening of the bottle into his mouth and breath in and out through the mouth.\nA carer then presses down the inhaler and sprays into the bottle while the child continues to breath\nnormally.\nWait for three to four breaths and repeat. (cid:42)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:70)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:41)(cid:72)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:33)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:27)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:131)(cid:38)(cid:12)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:40)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:76)(cid:81)\nFor younger children place the cup over the child's mouth and use as a spacer in the same way.\nGive paracetamol every 6 hours until high fever or ear pain is gone.\n* If a spacer is being used for the first time, it should be primed by 4-5 extra puffs from the inhaler.\nPARACETAMOL\nAGE or WEIGHT\nTABLET (100 mg) TABLET (500 mg)\n2 months up to 3 years (4 - <14 kg) 1 1/4\n3 years up to 5 years (14 - <19 kg) 1 1/2 1/2\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:22)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTEACH THE MOTHER TO GIVE ORAL DRUGS AT HOME\nFollow the instructions below for every oral drug to be given at home.\nAlso follow the instructions listed with each drug's dosage table.\nGive Iron*\nGive one dose daily fo"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0020", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0021_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended dosage of iron for children aged 2 months up to 5 years when given at home?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "R TO GIVE ORAL DRUGS AT HOME\nFollow the instructions below for every oral drug to be given at home.\nAlso follow the instructions listed with each drug's dosage table.\nGive Iron*\nGive one dose daily for 14 days.\nIRON/FOLATE\nIRON SYRUP\nTABLET\nAGE or WEIGHT Ferrous sulfate\n(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:14)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:24)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:151)(cid:74) Ferrous fumarate 100 mg per 5 ml (20 mg\nFolate (60 mg elemental iron per ml)\nelemental iron)\n2 months up to 4 months (4 -\n1.00 ml (< 1/4 tsp.)\n<6 kg)\n4 months up to 12 months\n1.25 ml (1/4 tsp.)\n(6 - <10 kg)\n12 months up to 3 years\n1/2 tablet 2.00 ml (<1/2 tsp.)\n(10 - <14 kg)\n3 years up to 5 years (14 -\n1/2 tablet 2.5 ml (1/2 tsp.)\n19 kg)\n* Children with severe acute malnutrition who are receiving ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) should\nnot be given Iron.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTEACH THE MOTHER TO TREAT LOCAL INFECTIONS AT HOME\nTreat for Mouth Ulcers with Gentian Violet (GV)\nExplain to the mother what the treatment is and why it should be given.\nDescribe the treatment steps listed in the appropriate box. Treat for mouth ulcers twice daily.\nWatch the mother as she does the first treatment in the clinic (except for remedy for Wash hands.\ncough or sore throat). Wash the child's mouth with clean soft cloth wrapped around the finger and wet with salt water.\nTell her how often to do the treatment at home. Paint t"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0021", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0022_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended treatment for thrush in infants?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "or remedy for Wash hands.\ncough or sore throat). Wash the child's mouth with clean soft cloth wrapped around the finger and wet with salt water.\nTell her how often to do the treatment at home. Paint the mouth with half-strength gentian violet (0.25% dilution).\nIf needed for treatment at home, give mother the tube of tetracycline ointment or a Wash hands again.\nContinue using GV for 48 hours after the ulcers have been cured.\nsmall bottle of gentian violet.\nGive paracetamol for pain relief.\nCheck the mothers understanding before she leaves the clinic.\nTreat Thrush with Nystatin\nSoothe the Throat, Relieve the Cough with a Safe Remedy\nTreat thrush four times daily for 7 days\nSafe remedies to recommend:\nWash hands\nBreast milk for a breastfed infant.\n(cid:58)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:75)\n_____________________________________________________________________________\nInstill nystatin 1ml four times a day\n__________________________________________"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0022", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0023_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended treatment regimen for eye infection in this guideline?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": ")(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:75)\n_____________________________________________________________________________\nInstill nystatin 1ml four times a day\n_____________________________________________________________________________\nAvoid feeding for 20 minutes after medication\nHarmful remedies to discourage: (cid:44)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:85)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:75)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:44)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:81)(cid:92)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:81)\n_____________________________________________________________________________ Advise mother to wash breasts after feeds. If bottle fed advise change to cup and spoon\n_____________________________________________________________________________ Give paracetamol if needed for pain\n_____________________________________________________________________________\nTreat Eye Infection with Tetracycline Eye Ointment\nClean both eyes 4 times daily.\nWash hands.\nUse clean cloth and water to gently wipe away pus.\nThen apply tetracycline eye ointment in both eyes 4 times daily.\nSqu"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0023", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0024_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should tetracycline eye ointment be applied for eye discharge in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ection with Tetracycline Eye Ointment\nClean both eyes 4 times daily.\nWash hands.\nUse clean cloth and water to gently wipe away pus.\nThen apply tetracycline eye ointment in both eyes 4 times daily.\nSquirt a small amount of ointment on the inside of the lower lid.\nWash hands again.\nTreat until there is no pus discharge.\nDo not put anything else in the eye.\nClear the Ear by Dry Wicking and Give Eardrops*\nDry the ear at least 3 times daily.\nRoll clean absorbent cloth or soft, strong tissue paper into a wick.\nPlace the wick in the child's ear.\nRemove the wick when wet.\nReplace the wick with a clean one and repeat these steps until the ear is dry.\nInstill quinolone eardrops after dry wicking three times daily for two weeks.\n* Quinolone eardrops may include ciprofloxacin, norfloxacin, or ofloxacin.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE VITAMIN A AND MEBENDAZOLE IN CLINIC\nExplain to the mother why the drug is given\nDetermine the dose appropriate for the child's weight (or age)\nMeasure the dose accurately\nGive Vitamin A Supplementation and Treatment\nVITAMIN A SUPPLEMENTATION:\nGive first dose any time after 6 months of age to ALL CHILDREN\nThereafter vitamin A every six months to ALL CHILDREN\nVITAMIN A TREATMENT:\nGive an extra dose of Vitamin A (same dose as for supplementation) for treatment if the child has MEASLES or PERSISTENT DIARRHOEA. If the child has had a dose of vitamin A within the past\nmonth or is on"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0024", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0025_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended Vitamin A dose for children with measles or persistent diarrhea based on their age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ve an extra dose of Vitamin A (same dose as for supplementation) for treatment if the child has MEASLES or PERSISTENT DIARRHOEA. If the child has had a dose of vitamin A within the past\nmonth or is on RUTF for treatment of severe acute malnutrition, DO NOT GIVE VITAMIN A.\nAlways record the dose of Vitamin A given on the child's card.\nAGE VITAMIN A DOSE\n6 up to 12 months 100 000 IU\nOne year and older 200 000 IU\nGive Mebendazole\nGive 500 mg mebendazole as a single dose in clinic if:\nhookworm/whipworm are a problem in children in your area, and\nthe child is 1 years of age or older, and\nthe child has not had a dose in the previous 6 months.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE THESE TREATMENTS IN THE CLINIC ONLY\nGive Artesunate Suppositories or Intramuscular Artesunate or\nExplain to the mother why the drug is given.\nQuinine for Severe Malaria\nDetermine the dose appropriate for the child's weight (or age).\nUse a sterile needle and sterile syringe when giving an injection.\nFOR CHILDREN BEING REFERRED WITH VERY SEVERE FEBRILE DISEASE:\nMeasure the dose accurately.\nCheck which pre-referral treatment is available in your clinic (rectal artesunate suppositories,\nGive the drug as an intramuscular injection. artesunate injection or quinine).\nIf child cannot be referred, follow the instructions provided. Artesunate suppository: Insert first dose of the suppository and refer child urgently\nIntramuscular artesunate"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0025", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0026_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended treatment for a child with severe malaria who cannot be referred to a hospital immediately?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "te injection or quinine).\nIf child cannot be referred, follow the instructions provided. Artesunate suppository: Insert first dose of the suppository and refer child urgently\nIntramuscular artesunate or quinine: Give first dose and refer child urgently to hospital.\nIF REFERRAL IS NOT POSSIBLE:\nGive Intramuscular Antibiotics For artesunate injection:\nGive first dose of artesunate intramuscular injection\nGIVE TO CHILDREN BEING REFERRED URGENTLY Repeat dose after 12 hrs and daily until the child can take orally\nGive Ampicillin (50 mg/kg) and Gentamicin (7.5 mg/kg). Give full dose of oral antimlarial as soon as the child is able to take orally.\nFor artesunate suppository:\nAMPICILLIN Give first dose of suppository\nRepeat the same dose of suppository every 24 hours until the child can take oral antimalarial.\nDilute 500mg vial with 2.1ml of sterile water (500mg/2.5ml).\nGive full dose of oral antimalarial as soon as the child is able to take orally\nIF REFERRAL IS NOT POSSIBLE OR DELAYED, repeat the ampicillin injection every 6 hours.\nFor quinine:\nWhere there is a strong suspicion of meningitis, the dose of ampicillin can be increased 4\nGive first dose of intramuscular quinine.\ntimes.\nThe child should remain lying down for one hour.\nRepeat the quinine injection at 4 and 8 hours later, and then every 12 hours until the child is able\nGENTAMICIN\nto take an oral antimalarial. Do not continue quinine injections for more than 1 week.\n7.5 mg/kg/day once daily\nIf low risk of malaria, do not g"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0026", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0027_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the rectal diazepam dosage for a child aged 2 to 3 years (10 - <14 kg) to stop convulsions?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "then every 12 hours until the child is able\nGENTAMICIN\nto take an oral antimalarial. Do not continue quinine injections for more than 1 week.\n7.5 mg/kg/day once daily\nIf low risk of malaria, do not give quinine to a child less than 4 months of age.\nAMPICILLIN GENTAMICIN\nAGE or WEIGHT\n500 mg vial 2ml/40 mg/ml vial\nRECTAL ARTESUNATE INTRAMUSCULAR INTRAMUSCULAR\n2 up to 4 months (4 - <6 kg) 1 m 0.5-1.0 ml SUPPOSITORY ARTESUNATE QUININE\n4 up to 12 months (6 - <10 kg) 2 ml 1.1-1.8 ml 50 mg 200 mg\nAGE or WEIGHT 60 mg 150 mg/ml* 300 mg/ml*\nsuppositories suppositories\n12 months up to 3 years (10 - <14 kg) 3 ml 1.9-2.7 ml vial (20mg/ml) 2.4 (in 2 ml (in 2 ml\nDosage 10 Dosage 10\nmg/kg ampoules) ampoules)\n3 years up to 5 years (14 - 19 kg) 5 m 2.8-3.5 ml mg/kg mg/kg\n2 months up to 4\n1 1/2 ml 0.4 ml 0.2 ml\nmonths (4 - <6 kg)\nGive Diazepam to Stop Convulsions 4 months up to 12 2 1 ml 0.6 ml 0.3 ml\nmonths (6 - <10 kg)\nTurn the child to his/her side and clear the airway. Avoid putting things in the mouth. 12 months up to 2\n2 - 1.5 ml 0.8 ml 0.4 ml\nGive 0.5mg/kg diazepam injection solution per rectum using a small syringe without a needle (like a years (10 - <12 kg)\ntuberculin syringe) or using a catheter.\n2 years up to 3\nCheck for low blood sugar, then treat or prevent. 3 1 1.5 ml 1.0 ml 0.5 ml\nyears (12 - <14 kg)\nGive oxygen and REFER\nIf convulsions have not stopped after 10 minutes repeat diazepam dose 3 years up to 5 3 1 2 ml 1.2 ml 0.6 ml\nyears (14 - 19 kg)\nDIAZEPAM\nAGE or WEIGHT\n10mg/2"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0027", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0028_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the correct dose of diazepam for a child aged 3 to 5 years (14 - 19 kg) experiencing convulsions?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "years (12 - <14 kg)\nGive oxygen and REFER\nIf convulsions have not stopped after 10 minutes repeat diazepam dose 3 years up to 5 3 1 2 ml 1.2 ml 0.6 ml\nyears (14 - 19 kg)\nDIAZEPAM\nAGE or WEIGHT\n10mg/2mls * quinine salt\n2 months up to 6 months (5 - 7 kg) 0.5 ml\n6 months up to 12months (7 - <10 kg) 1.0 ml\n12 months up to 3 years (10 - <14 kg) 1.5 ml\n3 years up to 5 years (14-19 kg) 2.0 ml\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:26)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE THESE TREATMENTS IN THE CLINIC ONLY\nTreat the Child to Prevent Low Blood Sugar\nIf the child is able to breastfeed:\nAsk the mother to breastfeed the child.\nIf the child is not able to breastfeed but is able to swallow:\nGive expressed breast milk or a breast-milk substitute.\nIf neither of these is available, give sugar water*.\nGive 30 - 50 ml of milk or sugar water* before departure.\nIf the child is not able to swallow:\nGive 50 ml of milk or sugar water* by nasogastric tube.\nIf no nasogastric tube available, give 1 teaspoon of sugar moistened with 1-2 drops of water\nsublingually and repeat doses every 20 minutes to prevent relapse.\n* To make sugar water: Dissolve 4 level teaspoons of sugar (20 grams) in a 200-ml cup of clean\nwater.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE EXTRA FLUID FOR DIARRHOEA AND CONTINUE FEEDING\nPLAN B: TREAT SOME DEHYDRATION WITH ORS\n(See FOOD advice on COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0028", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0029_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How much ORS should be given during the first 4 hours for a child with some dehydration based on weight or age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "0)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE EXTRA FLUID FOR DIARRHOEA AND CONTINUE FEEDING\nPLAN B: TREAT SOME DEHYDRATION WITH ORS\n(See FOOD advice on COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart)\nIn the clinic, give recommended amount of ORS over 4-hour period\nDETERMINE AMOUNT OF ORS TO GIVE DURING FIRST 4 HOURS\nPLAN A: TREAT DIARRHOEA AT HOME\nWEIGHT < 6 kg 6 - <10 kg 10 - <12 kg 12 - 19 kg\nAGE* Up to 4 4 months up to 12 12 months up to 2 2 years up to 5\nCounsel the mother on the 4 Rules of Home Treatment:\nmonths months years years\n1. Give Extra Fluid In ml 200 - 450 450 - 800 800 - 960 960 - 1600\n2. Give Zinc Supplements (age 2 months up to 5 years) * Use the child's age only when you do not know the weight. The approximate amount of ORS\n3. Continue Feeding required (in ml) can also be calculated by multiplying the child's weight (in kg) times 75.\n4. When to Return. If the child wants more ORS than shown, give more.\nFor infants under 6 months who are not breastfed, also give 100 - 200 ml clean water during this\n1. GIVE EXTRA FLUID (as much as the child will take) period if you use standard ORS. This is not needed if you use new low osmolarity ORS.\nSHOW THE MOTHER HOW TO GIVE ORS SOLUTION.\nTELL THE MOTHER:\nGive frequent small sips from a cup.\nBreastfeed frequently and for longer at each feed.\nIf the child vomits, wait 10 minutes. Then continue, but more slowly.\nIf the child is exclusively breastfed, give ORS or clean water in addition to breast milk.\nContinue breastfeed"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0029", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0030_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended home treatment for children with diarrhea, including fluid and ORS administration?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "onger at each feed.\nIf the child vomits, wait 10 minutes. Then continue, but more slowly.\nIf the child is exclusively breastfed, give ORS or clean water in addition to breast milk.\nContinue breastfeeding whenever the child wants.\nIf the child is not exclusively breastfed, give one or more of the following:\nORS solution, food-based fluids (such as soup, rice water, and yoghurt drinks), or clean AFTER 4 HOURS:\nwater. Reassess the child and classify the child for dehydration.\nIt is especially important to give ORS at home when: Select the appropriate plan to continue treatment.\nthe child has been treated with Plan B or Plan C during this visit. Begin feeding the child in clinic.\nthe child cannot return to a clinic if the diarrhoea gets worse. IF THE MOTHER MUST LEAVE BEFORE COMPLETING TREATMENT:\nTEACH THE MOTHER HOW TO MIX AND GIVE ORS. GIVE THE MOTHER 2 PACKETS OF Show her how to prepare ORS solution at home.\nORS TO USE AT HOME. Show her how much ORS to give to finish 4-hour treatment at home.\nSHOW THE MOTHER HOW MUCH FLUID TO GIVE IN ADDITION TO THE USUAL FLUID Give her enough ORS packets to complete rehydration. Also give her 2 packets as recommended\nINTAKE: in Plan A.\nUp to 2 years 50 to 100 ml after each loose stool Explain the 4 Rules of Home Treatment:\n2 years or more 100 to 200 ml after each loose stool 1. GIVE EXTRA FLUID\nTell the mother to: 2. GIVE ZINC (age 2 months up to 5 years)\nGive frequent small sips from a cup. 3. CONTINUE FEEDING (exclusive breastfeeding if age"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0030", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0031_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended zinc dosage for children with diarrhea aged 2 months to 5 years?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "200 ml after each loose stool 1. GIVE EXTRA FLUID\nTell the mother to: 2. GIVE ZINC (age 2 months up to 5 years)\nGive frequent small sips from a cup. 3. CONTINUE FEEDING (exclusive breastfeeding if age less than 6 months)\nIf the child vomits, wait 10 minutes. Then continue, but more slowly. 4. WHEN TO RETURN\nContinue giving extra fluid until the diarrhoea stops.\n2. GIVE ZINC (age 2 months up to 5 years)\nTELL THE MOTHER HOW MUCH ZINC TO GIVE (20 mg tab):\n2 months up to 6 months 1/2 tablet daily for 14 days\n6 months or more 1 tablet daily for 14 days\nSHOW THE MOTHER HOW TO GIVE ZINC SUPPLEMENTS\nInfants - dissolve tablet in a small amount of expressed breast milk, ORS or clean water in a\ncup.\nOlder children - tablets can be chewed or dissolved in a small amount of water.\n3. CONTINUE FEEDING (exclusive breastfeeding if age less than 6 months)\n4. WHEN TO RETURN\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:28)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE EXTRA FLUID FOR DIARRHOEA AND CONTINUE FEEDING\nPLAN C: TREAT SEVERE DEHYDRATION QUICKLY\nFOLLOW THE ARROWS. IF ANSWER IS \"YES\", GO ACROSS. IF \"NO\", GO\nDOWN.\nSTART HERE Start IV fluid immediately. If the child can drink, give ORS by\nCan you give mouth while the drip is set up. Give 100 ml/kg Ringer's Lactate\nintravenous (IV) fluid (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314) Solution (or, if not available, normal saline), divided as follows\nimmediately? AGE First give Then give\n30 ml/kg in: 70 ml/kg in:\nNO\nInfants"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0031", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0032_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should intravenous and oral rehydration be administered to infants and children with dehydration based on age and clinical response?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "intravenous (IV) fluid (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314) Solution (or, if not available, normal saline), divided as follows\nimmediately? AGE First give Then give\n30 ml/kg in: 70 ml/kg in:\nNO\nInfants (under 12 1 hour* 5 hours\n(cid:315)\nmonths)\nChildren (12 months up 30 minutes* 2 1/2 hours\nto 5 years)\n* Repeat once if radial pulse is still very weak or not\ndetectable.\nReassess the child every 1-2 hours. If hydration status is\nnot improving, give the IV drip more rapidly.\nAlso give ORS (about 5 ml/kg/hour) as soon as the child can\ndrink: usually after 3-4 hours (infants) or 1-2 hours (children).\nReassess an infant after 6 hours and a child after 3 hours.\nClassify dehydration. Then choose the appropriate plan (A, B,\nor C) to continue treatment.\nIs IV treatment Refer URGENTLY to hospital for IV treatment.\navailable nearby (within (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314) If the child can drink, provide the mother with ORS solution and\n30 minutes)? show her how to give frequent sips during the trip or give ORS\nby naso-gastric tube.\nNO\n(cid:315)\nAre you trained to use Start rehydration by tube (or mouth) with ORS solution:\na naso-gastric (NG) (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314) give 20 ml/kg/hour for 6 hours (total of 120 ml/kg).\ntube for rehydration? Reassess the child every 1-2 hours while waiting for\nNO\ntransfer:\n(cid:315) If there is repeated vomiting or increasing abdominal\ndistension, give the fluid more slowly.\nCan the child drink? (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314)\nIf hydration statu"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0032", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0033_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What should be done if a child's hydration status does not improve after 3 hours of oral rehydration?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "iting for\nNO\ntransfer:\n(cid:315) If there is repeated vomiting or increasing abdominal\ndistension, give the fluid more slowly.\nCan the child drink? (cid:60)(cid:40)(cid:54)(cid:314)\nIf hydration status is not improving after 3 hours, send the\nNO child for IV therapy.\n(cid:315)\nAfter 6 hours, reassess the child. Classify dehydration. Then\nchoose the appropriate plan (A, B or C) to continue treatment.\nRefer URGENTLY to NOTE:\nhospital for IV or NG If the child is not referred to hospital, observe the child at least"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0033", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0034_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) be administered to a child with severe acute malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment 6 hours after rehydration to be sure the mother can maintain\nhydration giving the child ORS solution by mouth.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE READY-TO-USE THERAPEUTIC FOOD\nGive Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food for SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nWash hands before giving the ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF).\nSit with the child on the lap and gently offer the ready-to-use therapeutic food.\nEncourage the child to eat the RUTF without forced feeding.\n(cid:42)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:80)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:74)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:53)(cid:56)(cid:55)(cid:41)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:24)(cid:177)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:68)(cid:92)(cid:17)\nIf still breastfeeding, continue by offering breast milk first before every RUTF feed.\nGive only the RUTF for at least two weeks, if breastfeeding continue to breast and gradually introduce foods recommended for the age (See Feeding recommendations in COUNSEL THE"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0034", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0035_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How much Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) should be given daily to a child weighing 7.0-8.4 kg?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "before every RUTF feed.\nGive only the RUTF for at least two weeks, if breastfeeding continue to breast and gradually introduce foods recommended for the age (See Feeding recommendations in COUNSEL THE MOTHER\nchart).\nWhen introducing recommended foods, ensure that the child completes his daily ration of RUTF before giving other foods.\nOffer plenty of clean water, to drink from a cup, when the child is eating the ready-to-use therapeutic food.\nRecommended Amounts of Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food\nPackets per day\nCHILD'S WEIGHT (kg) Packets per Week Supply\n(92 g Packets Containing 500 kcal)\n4.0-4.9 kg 2.0 14\n5.0-6.9 kg 2.5 18\n7.0-8.4 kg 3.0 21\n8.5-9.4 kg 3.5 25\n9.5-10.4 kg 4.0 28\n10.5-11.9 kg 4.5 32\n>12.0 kg 5.0 35\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:20)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nSteps when Initiating ART in Children\nAll children less than 5 years who are HIV infected should be initiated on ART irrespective of CD4 count or clinical stage.\nRemember that if a child has any general danger sign or a severe classification, he or she needs URGENT REFERRAL. ART initiation is not urgent, and the child should be stabilized first.\nSTEP 1: DECIDE IF THE CHILD HAS CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION STEP 3: DECIDE IF ART CAN BE INITIATED IN YOUR FACILITY\nChild is under 18 months: If child is less than 3 kg or has TB, Refer for ART initiation.\nHIV infection is confirmed if virological test (PCR) is positive If child weighs 3 kg or mo"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0035", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0036_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the criteria for confirming HIV infection in children under and over 18 months of age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "D IN YOUR FACILITY\nChild is under 18 months: If child is less than 3 kg or has TB, Refer for ART initiation.\nHIV infection is confirmed if virological test (PCR) is positive If child weighs 3 kg or more and does not have TB, GO TO STEP 4\nChild is over 18 months:\nTwo different serological tests are positive\nSend any further confirmatory tests required\nIf results are discordant, refer\nIf HIV infection is confirmed, and child is in stable condition,\nGO TO STEP 2\nSTEP 2: DECIDE IF CAREGIVER IS ABLE TO GIVE ART STEP 4: RECORD BASELINE INFORMATION ON THE CHILD'S HIV TREATMENT CARD\nCheck that the caregiver is willing and able to give ART. The Record the following information:\n(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:74)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86) Weight and height\nto another adult who can assist with providing ART, or be part Pallor if present\nof a support group.\nFeeding problem if present\nCaregiver able to give ART: GO TO STEP 3 Laboratory results (if available): Hb, viral load, CD4 count and percentage. Send for any laboratory tests\nCaregiver not able: classify as CONFIR"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0036", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0037_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the preferred antiretroviral regimens for initiating ART in HIV-infected children based on age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "em if present\nCaregiver able to give ART: GO TO STEP 3 Laboratory results (if available): Hb, viral load, CD4 count and percentage. Send for any laboratory tests\nCaregiver not able: classify as CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION that are required. Do not wait for results. GO TO STEP 5\nbut NOT ON ART. Counsel and support the\ncaregiver. Follow-up regularly. Move to the step 3 once the\ncaregiver is willing and able to give ART.\nSTEP 5: START ON ART, COTRIMOXAZOLE PROPHYLAXIS AND ROUTINE TREATMENTS\nInitiate ART treatement:\nChild up to 3 years: ABC or AZT +3TC+ LPV/R or recommended first-line regimen\nChild 3 years or older: ABC + 3TC + EFV, or recommended first-line regimen.\nGive co-trimoxazole prophylaxis\nGive other routine treatments, including Vitamin A and immunizations\nFollow-up regularly as per national guidelines\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:21)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nPreferred and Alternative ARV Regimens\nAGE Preferred Alternative Children with TB/HIV Infection\nBirth up to 3 YEARS ABC or AZT + 3TC + LPV/r ABC or AZT + 3TC + NVP ABC or AZT + 3TC + NVP\nAZT + 3TC + ABC\n3 years and older ABC + 3TC + EFV ABC or AZT + 3TC + EFV or NVP ABC or AZT + 3TC + EFV\nAZT + 3TC + ABC\nGive Antiretroviral Drugs (Fixed Dose Combinations)\nAZT/3TC AZT/3TC/NVP ABC/AZT/3TC ABC/3TC\nWEIGHT (Kg) Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily\n60/30 mg tablet 300/150 mg tablet 60/30/50 mg tablet 300/150/200 mg tablet 60"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0037", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0038_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the dosing instructions for antiretroviral drugs in HIV-infected children based on their weight?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "xed Dose Combinations)\nAZT/3TC AZT/3TC/NVP ABC/AZT/3TC ABC/3TC\nWEIGHT (Kg) Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily\n60/30 mg tablet 300/150 mg tablet 60/30/50 mg tablet 300/150/200 mg tablet 60/60/30 mg tablet 300/300/150 mg tablet 60/30 mg tablet 600/300 mg tablet\n3 - 5.9 1 - 1 - 1 - 1 -\n6 - 9.9 1.5 - 1.5 - 1.5 - 1.5 -\n10 - 13.9 2 - 2 - 2 - 2 -\n14 - 19.9 2.5 - 2.5 - 2.5 - 2.5 -\n20 - 24.9 3 - 3 - 3 - 3 -\n25 - 34.9 - 1 1 1 - 0.5\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:22)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nGive Antiretroviral Drugs\nLOPINAVIR / RITONAVIR (LPV/r), NEVIRAPINE (NVP) & EFAVIRENZ (EFV)\nLOPINAVIR / RITONAVIR (LPV/r) EFAVIRENZ (EFV)\nNEVIRAPINE (NVP)\nWEIGHT (KG)\n(cid:100)(cid:258)(cid:396)(cid:336)(cid:286)(cid:410)(cid:3)(cid:282)(cid:381)(cid:400)(cid:286)(cid:3)(cid:1006)(cid:1007)(cid:1004)(cid:882)(cid:1007)(cid:1009)(cid:1004)(cid:373)(cid:336)(cid:876)(cid:373)(cid:1016)(cid:3)(cid:410)(cid:449)(cid:349)(cid:272)(cid:286)(cid:3)(cid:282)(cid:258)(cid:349)(cid:367)(cid:455) Target dose 15 mg/Kogn ce daily\n80/20 mg liquid 100/25 mg tablet 10 mg/ml liquid 50 mg tablet 200 mg tablet 200 mg tablet\nTwice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Once daily\n3 - 5.9 1 ml - 5 ml 1 - -\n6 - 9.9 1.5 ml - 8 ml 1.5 - -\n10 - 13.9 2 ml 2 10 ml 2 - 1\n14 - 19.9 2.5 ml 2 - 2.5 - 1.5\n20 - 24.9 3 ml 2 - 3 - 1.5\n25 - 34.9 - 3 - - 1 2\nABACAVIR (ABC), ZIDOVUDINE (AZT or ZDV) & LAMIVUDINE (3TC)\nZIDO"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0038", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0039_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended dosing for abacavir, zidovudine, and lamivudine in HIV-infected children based on weight?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- 9.9 1.5 ml - 8 ml 1.5 - -\n10 - 13.9 2 ml 2 10 ml 2 - 1\n14 - 19.9 2.5 ml 2 - 2.5 - 1.5\n20 - 24.9 3 ml 2 - 3 - 1.5\n25 - 34.9 - 3 - - 1 2\nABACAVIR (ABC), ZIDOVUDINE (AZT or ZDV) & LAMIVUDINE (3TC)\nZIDOVUDINE (AZT or ZD V)\nABACAVIR (AB C)\n(cid:100)(cid:258)(cid:396)(cid:336)(cid:286)(cid:410)(cid:3)(cid:282)(cid:381)(cid:400)(cid:286)(cid:3)(cid:1005)(cid:1012)(cid:1004)(cid:882)(cid:1006)(cid:1008)(cid:1004)(cid:373)(cid:336)(cid:876)(cid:373)(cid:1016)(cid:3)(cid:410)(cid:449)(cid:349)(cid:272)(cid:286)(cid:3)(cid:282)(cid:258)(cid:349)(cid:367)(cid:455) LAMIVUDINE (3T C )\nTarget dose: 8mg/Kg/dose twice daily\nWEIGHT (KG)\n20 mg/ml liquid 60 mg dispersible tablet 300 mg tablet 10 mg/ml liquid 60 mg tablet 300 mg tablet 10 mg/ml liquid 30 mg tablet 150 mg tablet\nTwice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily Twice daily\n3 - 5.9 3 ml 1 - 6 ml 1 - 3 ml 1 -\n6 - 9.9 4 ml 1.5 - 9 ml 1.5 - 4 ml 1.5 -\n10 - 13.9 6 ml 2 - 12 ml 2 - 6 ml 2 -\n14 - 19.9 - 2.5 - - 2.5 - - 2.5 -\n20 - 24.9 - 3 - - 3 - - 3 -\n25 - 34.9 - - 1 - - 1 - - 1\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nSide Effects ARV Drugs\nVery common side-effets: Potentially serious side effects: Side effects occurring later during"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0039", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0040_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the signs and symptoms that require urgent care for side effects of ARV drugs in HIV-infected children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment:\nwarn patients and suggest ways patients can warn patients and tell them to seek care discuss with patients\nmanage;\nmanage when patients seek care\nAbacavir (ABC) Seek care urgently:\nFever, vomiting, rash - this may indicate hypersensitivity to\nabacavir\nLamivudine (3TC) Nausea\nDiarrhoea\nLopinavir/ritonavir Nausea Changes in fat distribution:\nVomiting Arms, legs, buttocks, cheeks become THIN\nBreasts, tummy, back of neck become FAT\nDiarrhoea\nElevated blood cholesterol and glucose\nNevirapine (NVP) Nausea Seek care urgently:\nDiarrhoea Yellow eyes\nSevere skin rash\nFatigue AND shortness of breath\nFever\nZidovudine Nausea Seek care urgently:\n(ZDV or AZT) Diarrhoea Pallor (anaemia)\nHeadache\nFatigue\nMuscle pain\nEfavirenz (EFV) Nausea Seek care urgently:\nDiarrhoea Yellow eyes\nStrange dreams Psychosis or confusion\nDifficulty sleeping Severe skin rash\nMemory problems\nHeadache\nDizziness\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nManage Side Effects of ARV Drugs\nSIGNS or SYMPTOMS APPROPRIATE CARE RESPONSE\nYellow eyes (jaundice) or Stop drugs and REFER URGENTLY\nabdominal pain\nRash If on abacavir, assess carefully. Is it a dry or wet lesion? Call for advice. If the rash is severe, generalized, or peeling, involves the mucosa or is associated with\nfever or vomiting: stop drugs and REFER URGENTLY\nNausea Advise that the drug should be given with food. If persists for more than 2 wee"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0040", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0041_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child receiving medication be urgently referred due to vomiting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "neralized, or peeling, involves the mucosa or is associated with\nfever or vomiting: stop drugs and REFER URGENTLY\nNausea Advise that the drug should be given with food. If persists for more than 2 weeks or worsens, call for advice or refer.\nVomiting Children may commonly vomit medication. Repeat the dose if the medication is seen in the vomitus, or if vomiting occurred 30 minutes of the dose being given.\nIf vomiting persists, the caregiver should bring the child to clinic for evaluation.\nIf vomiting everything, or vomiting associated with severe abdominal pain or difficulty breathing, REFER URGENTLY.\nDiarrhoea Assess, classify, and treat using diarrhoea charts. Reassure mother that if due to ARV, it will improve in a few weeks. Follow-up as per chart booklet. If not\nimproved after two weeks, call for advice or refer.\nFever Assess, classify, and treat using feve chart.\nHeadache Give paracetamol. If on efavirenz, reassure that this is common and usually self-limiting. If persists for more than 2 weeks or worsens, call for advice or refer.\nSleep disturbances, This may be due to efavirenz. Give at night and take on an empty stomach with low-fat foods. If persists for more than 2 weeks or worsens, call for advice or\nnightmares, anxiety refer.\nTingling, numb or painful feet If new or worse on treatment, call for advice or refer.\nor legs\nChanges in fat distribution Consider switching from stavudine to abacavir, consider to viral load. Refer if needed.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0041", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0042_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the recommended dosages of paracetamol and morphine for pain relief in HIV-infected children based on age or weight?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "orse on treatment, call for advice or refer.\nor legs\nChanges in fat distribution Consider switching from stavudine to abacavir, consider to viral load. Refer if needed.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTREAT THE HIV INFECTED CHILD\nGive Pain Relief to HIV Infected Child\nGive paracetamol or ibuprofen every 6 hours if pain persists.\nFor severe pain, morphine syrup can be given.\nPARACETAMOL ORAL MORPHINE\nAGE or WEIGHT\nTABLET (100 mg) SYRUP (120 mg/5ml) (0.5 mg/5 ml)\n2 up to 4 months (4 - <6 kg) - 2 ml 0.5 ml\n4 up to 12 months (6 - <10 kg) 1 2.5 ml 2 ml\n12 months up to 2 years (10 - <12 kg) 1 1/2 5 ml 3 ml\n2 up to 3 years (12 - <14 kg) 2 7.5 ml 4 ml\n3 up to 5 years (14 -<19 kg) 2 10 ml 5 ml\nRecommended dosages for ibuprofen(cid:29)(cid:3)(cid:24)(cid:16)(cid:20)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:74)(cid:18)(cid:78)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:25)(cid:16)(cid:27)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:68)(cid:91)(cid:76)(cid:80)(cid:88)(cid:80)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:24)(cid:19)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:68)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:17)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:243)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:87)"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0042", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0043_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the criteria to assess improvement in a child with dysentery during follow-up after 3 days?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "id:3)(cid:76)(cid:17)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:243)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:78)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:242)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:83)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:78)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:71)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:87)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:36)(cid:89)(cid:82)(cid:76)(cid:71)\nibuprofen in children under the age of 3 months.\nIMMUNIZE EVERY SICK CHILD AS NEEDED\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:26)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nFOLLOW-UP\nGIVE FOLLOW-UP CARE FOR ACUTE CONDITIONS\nDYSENTERY\nCare for the child who returns for follow-up using all the boxes that match the\nchild's previous classifications. After 3 days:\nIf the child has any new problem, assess, classify and treat the new problem as on Assess the child for diarrhoea. > See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart.\nthe ASSESS AND CLASSIFY chart.\nAsk:\nAre there fewer stools?\nIs there less blood in the stool?\nPNEUMONIA Is there less fever?\nIs there less abdominal pain?\nAfter 3 days: Is the child eating better?\nCheck the child for general danger signs."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0043", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0044_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended treatment if a child with dysentery shows no improvement or has worsening symptoms?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment:\nAssess the child for cough or difficult breathing.\nIf the child is dehydrated, treat dehydration.\nAsk:\nIf number of stools, amount of blood in stools, fever, abdominal pain, or eating are worse or\nIs the child breathing slower? See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart.\nthe same:\nIs there a chest indrawing?\nChange to second-line oral antibiotic recommended for dysentery in your area. Give it for 5 days.\nIs there less fever? Advise the mother to return in 3 days. If you do not have the second line antibiotic, REFER to\nIs the child eating better? hospital.\nExceptions - if the child: is less than 12 months old, or"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0044", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0045_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with persistent diarrhoea be urgently referred to hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment: was dehydrated on the first visit, or REFER to hospital.\nIf any general danger sign or stridor, refer URGENTLY to hospital. if he had measles within the last 3 months\nIf chest indrawing and/or breathing rate, fever and eating are the same or worse, refer\nURGENTLY to hospital. If fewer stools, less blood in the stools, less fever, less abdominal pain, and eating better,\nIf breathing slower, no chest indrawing, less fever, and eating better, complete the 5 days of continue giving ciprofloxacin until finished.\nantibiotic.\nEnsure that mother understands the oral rehydration method fully and that she also understands\nthe need for an extra meal each day for a week.\nPERSISTENT DIARRHOEA\nAfter 5 days: MALARIA\nAsk:\nHas the diarrhoea stopped? If fever persists after 3 days:\nHow many loose stools is the child having per day? Do a full reassessment of the child. > See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart.\nDO NOT REPEAT the Rapid Diagnostic Test if it was positive on the initial visit."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0045", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0046_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with persistent fever and no apparent cause other than malaria be referred to hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment:\nIf the diarrhoea has not stopped (child is still having 3 or more loose stools per day), do a full Treatment:\nreassessment of the child. Treat for dehydration if present. Then refer to hospital.\nIf the child has any general danger sign or stiff neck, treat as VERY SEVERE FEBRILE DISEASE.\nIf the diarrhoea has stopped (child having less than 3 loose stools per day), tell the mother to follow\nIf the child has any othercause of fever other than malaria, provide appropriate treatment.\nthe usual feeding recommendations for the child's age.\nIf there is no other apparent cause of fever:\nIf fever has been present for 7 days, refer for assessment.\nDo microscopy to look for malaria parasites. If parasites are present and the child has finished a\nfull course of the first line antimalarial, give the second-line antimalarial, if available, or refer the\nchild to a hospital.\nIf there is no other apparent cause of fever and you do not have a microscopy to check for\nparasites, refer the child to a hospital.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE FOLLOW-UP CARE FOR ACUTE CONDITIONS\nEAR INFECTION\nAfter 5 days:\nFEVER: NO MALARIA\nReassess for ear problem. > See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart.\nMeasure the child's temperature.\nIf fever persists after 3 days:\nDo a full reassessment of the child. > See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart. Treatment:\nRepeat the malaria test. If there is (cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:72)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:22)(cid:27)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:131)(cid:38)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:12), refer URGENTLY to\nhospital."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0046", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0047_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child with acute ear infection and persistent ear pain or discharge be treated?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment: Acute ear infection:\nIf the child has any general danger sign or stiff neck, treat as VERY SEVERE FEBRILE DISEASE. If ear pain or discharge persists, treat with 5 more days of the same antibiotic. Continue wicking\nto dry the ear. Follow-up in 5 days.\nIf a child has a positive malaria test, give first-line oral antimalarial. Advise the mother to return in 3\nIf no ear pain or discharge, praise the mother for her careful treatment. If she has not yet\ndays if the fever persists.\nfinished the 5 days of antibiotic, tell her to use all of it before stopping.\nIf the child has any other cause of fever other than malaria, provide treatment.\nChronic ear infection:\nIf there is no other apparent cause of fever: Check that the mother is wicking the ear correctly and giving quinolone drops tree times a day.\nIf the fever has been present for 7 days, refer for assessment. Encourage her to continue.\nMEASLES WITH EYE OR MOUTH COMPLICATIONS, GUM OR FEEDING PROBLEM\nMOUTH ULCERS, OR THRUSH\nAfter 7 days:\nReassess feeding. > See questions in the COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart.\nAfter 3 days:\nAsk about any feeding problems found on the initial visit.\nLook for red eyes and pus draining from the eyes.\nLook at mouth ulcers or white patches in the mouth (thrush).\nCounsel the mother about any new or continuing feeding problems. If you counsel the mother to make\nSmell the mouth.\nsignificant changes in feeding, ask her to bring the child back again.\nIf the child is classified as MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION, ask the mother to return 30 days"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0047", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0048_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What should be done if pus is draining from a child's eye with an infection?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment for eye infection:\nafter the initial visit to measure the child's WFH/L, MUAC.\nIf pus is draining from the eye, ask the mother to describe how she has treated the eye infection. If"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0048", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0049_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a mother be referred to the hospital or taught the correct treatment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment has been correct, refer to hospital. If treatment has not been correct, teach mother correct"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0049", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0050_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should treatment for an infection be stopped if there is no pus or redness?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment.\nIf the pus is gone but redness remains, continue the treatment.\nANAEMIA\nIf no pus or redness, stop the treatment.\nAfter 14 days:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0050", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0051_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the treatment plan for mouth ulcers in children according to this guideline?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment for mouth ulcers:\nGive iron. Advise mother to return in 14 days for more iron.\nIf mouth ulcers are worse, or there is a very foul smell from the mouth, refer to hospital.\nContinue giving iron every 14 days for 2 months.\nIf mouth ulcers are the same or better, continue using half-strength gentian violet for a total of 5\nIf the child has palmar pallor after 2 months, refer for assessment.\ndays."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0051", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0052_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended duration for continuing nystatin treatment for thrush if the condition is the same or better and the child is feeding well?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment for thrush:\nIf thrush is worse check that treatment is being given correctly.\nIf the child has problems with swallowing, refer to hospital.\nIf thrush is the same or better, and the child is feeding well, continue nystatine for a total of 7 days.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:21)(cid:28)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE FOLLOW-UP CARE FOR ACUTE CONDITIONS\nUNCOMPLICATED SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nAfter 14 days or during regular follow up:\nDo a full reassessment of the child. > See ASSESS & CLASSIFY chart.\nAssess child with the same measurements (WFH/L, MUAC) as on the initial visit.\nCheck for oedema of both feet.\nCheck the child's appetite by offering ready-to use therapeutic food if the child is 6 months or older."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0052", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0053_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the management for children with complicated, uncomplicated, moderate, and no acute malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment:\nIf the child has COMPLICATED SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION (WFH/L less than -3 z-scores or\nMUAC is less than 115 mm or oedema of both feet AND has developed a medical complication\nor oedema, or fails the appetite test), refer URGENTLY to hospital.\nIf the child has UNCOMPLICATED SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION (WFH/L less than -3 z-scores\nor MUAC is less than 115 mm or oedema of both feet but NO medical complication and passes\nappetite test), counsel the mother and encourage her to continue with appropriate RUTF feeding. Ask\nmother to return again in 14 days.\nIf the child has MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION (WFH/L between -3 and -2 z-scores or MUAC\nbetween 115 and 125 mm), advise the mother to continue RUTF. Counsel her to start other foods\naccording to the age appropriate feeding recommendations (see COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart). Tell\n(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:68)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:68)(cid:92)(cid:86)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:38)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:88)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:68)(cid:92)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:58)(cid:41)(cid:43)(cid:18)(cid:47)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:16)(cid:21)(cid:3)(cid:93)(cid:16)\nscores or more, and/or MUAC is 125 mm or more.\nIf the child has NO ACUTE MALNUTRITION (WFH/L is -2 z-scores or more, or MUAC is 125 mm or\nmore), praise the mother, STOP RUTF and counsel her about the age appropriate feeding\nrecommendations (see COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart).\nMODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nAfter 30 days:\nAssess the child using the same measurement (WFH/L or MUAC) used on the initial visit:\nIf WFH/L, weigh the child, measure height or length and determine if WFH/L.\nIf MUAC, measure using MUAC tape.\nCheck the child for oedema of both feet.\nReassess feeding. See questions in the COUNSEL THE MOTHER chart."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0053", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0054_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a child with moderate acute malnutrition be managed during follow-up visits?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Treatment:\nIf the child is no longer classified as MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION, praise the mother and\nencourage her to continue.\nIf the child is still classified as MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION, counsel the mother about any\nfeeding problem found. Ask the mother to return again in one month. Continue to see the child monthly\nuntil the child is feeding well and gaining weight regularly or his or her WFH/L is -2 z-scores or more or\nMUAC is 125 mm. or more.\nException:\nIf you do not think that feeding will improve, or if the child has lost weight or his or her MUAC has\ndiminished, refer the child.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE FOLLOW-UP CARE FOR HIV EXPOSED AND INFECTED CHILD\nCONFIRMED HIV INFECTION NOT ON ART\nFollow up regularly as per national guidelines.\nHIV EXPOSED\nAt each follow-up visit follow these instructions:\nFollow up regularly as per national guidelines. Ask the mother: Does the child have any problems?\nDo a full assessment including checking for mouth or gum problems, treat, counsel and follow up any\nAt each follow-up visit follow these instructions:\nnew problem\nAsk the mother: Does the child have any problems?\nCounsel and check if mother able or willing now to initiate ART for the child.\nDo a full assessment including checking for mouth or gum problems, treat, counsel and follow up any\nProvide routine child health care: Vitamin A, deworming, immunization, and feeding assessment and\nnew"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0054", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0055_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What components are included in routine child health care according to the guideline?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "o a full assessment including checking for mouth or gum problems, treat, counsel and follow up any\nProvide routine child health care: Vitamin A, deworming, immunization, and feeding assessment and\nnew problem\ncounselling\nProvide routine child health care: Vitamin A, deworming, immunization, and feeding assessment and\nContinue cotrimoxazole prophylaxis if indicated.\ncounselling\nInitiate or continue isoniazid preventive therapy if indicated.\nContinue cotrimoxazole prophylaxis\nIf no acute illness and mother is willing, initiate ART (See Box Steps when Initiating ART in children)\nContinue ARV prophylaxis if ARV drugs and breastfeeding are recommended; check adherence: How\nMonitor CD4 count and percentage.\noften, if ever, does the child/mother miss a dose?\n(cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:81)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:17)\n(cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0055", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0056_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What steps should be taken regarding HIV testing and follow-up care for a child during a home visit?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "cid:86)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:51)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:81)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:92)\nHome care:\nPlan for the next follow-up visit\nCounsel the mother about any new or continuing problems\nHIV testing:\nIf appropriate, put the family in touch with organizations or people who could provide support\nIf new HIV test result became available since the last visit, reclassify the child for HIV according to the Advise the mother about hygiene in the home, in particular when preparing food\ntest result.\nPlan for the next follow-up visit\n(cid:53)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:91)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:78)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(ci"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0056", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0057_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the follow-up care for a child with confirmed HIV infection on ART?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "4)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:91)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:78)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:53)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:73)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:70)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)\nto the test result.\nIf child is confirmed HIV infected\nStart on ART and enrol in chronic HIV care.\nContinue follow-up as for CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION ON ART\nIf child is confirmed uninfected\nContinue with co-trimoxazole prophylaxis if breastfeeding or stop if the test resuls are after 6 weeks\nof cessation of breastfeeding.\nCounsel mother on preventing HIV infection through breastfeeding and about her own health\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:20)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nGIVE FOLLOW-UP CARE FOR HIV EXPOSED AND INFECTED CHILD\nCONFIRMED HIV INFECTION ON ART: THE FOUR STEPS OF\nFOLLOW-UP CARE\nFollow up regularly as per national guidelines.\nSTEP 1: ASSESS AND CLASSIFY STEP 2: MONITOR PROGRESS ON ART\nASK: Does the child have any IF ANY OF FOLLOWING PRESENT, REFER\nproblems? NON-URGEN"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0057", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0058_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the criteria for non-urgent referral of a child on ART during follow-up care?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "-UP CARE\nFollow up regularly as per national guidelines.\nSTEP 1: ASSESS AND CLASSIFY STEP 2: MONITOR PROGRESS ON ART\nASK: Does the child have any IF ANY OF FOLLOWING PRESENT, REFER\nproblems? NON-URGENTLY:\nHas the child received care at another If any of these\nhealth facility since the last visit? present, refer\nCHECK: for general danger signs - If NON-\npresent, complete assessment, give URGENTLY:\npre-referral treatment, REFER Record the Child's weight Not gaining\nURGENTLY. and height weight for 3\nASSESS, CLASSIFY, TREAT and Assess adherence months\nCOUNSEL any sick child as Ask about adherence: how Loss of\nappropriate. often, if ever, does the milestones\nCHECK for ART severe side effects child miss a dose? Record Poor\nyour assessment. adherence\nSevere Assess and record clinical Stage\nskin rash stage worse than\nDifficulty Assess clinical stage. before\nbreathing (cid:38)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86) CD4 count\nand stage at previous visits. lower than\nIf present, give before\nsevere Monitor laboratory results\nabdominal any pre- Record results of tests LDL higher\npain referral that have been sent. than 3.5"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0058", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0059_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the key issues to discuss when counseling the mother or caregiver during ART and routine treatments for a stable child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment, mmol/L\nYellow\neyes REFER TG higher\nURGENTLY than 5.6\nFever,\nmmol/L\nvomiting,\nrash (only Manage side effects\nif on Send tests that are due\nAbacavir)\nCheck for other ART side effects\nSTEP 3: PROVIDE ART, STEP 4: COUNSEL THE MOTHER OR CAREGIVER\nCOTRIMOXAZOLE AND ROUTINE\nTREATMENTS Use every visit to educate and provide support to\nthe mother or caregiver\nIf child is stable: continue with the\nART regimen and cotrimoxazole doses.\nKey issues to discuss include:\nCheck for appropriate doses:\nremember these will need to increase How the child is progressing, feeding, adherence,\nas the child grows side-effects and correct management, disclosure\nGive routine care: Vitamin A (to others and the child), support for the caregiver\nsupplementation, deworming, and"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0059", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0060_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the definition of severe acute malnutrition in children aged 6 months or more?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "immunization as needed Remember to check that the mother and other\nfamily members are receiving the care that\nthey need\nSet a follow-up visit: if well, follow-up as per\nnastional guidelines. If problems, follow-up as\nindicated.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:21)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nCOUNSEL THE MOTHER\nFEEDING COUNSELLING\nAssess Child's Appetite\nAll children aged 6 months or more with SEVERE ACUTE MALNUTRITION (oedema of both feet or WFH/L less than -3 z-scores or MUAC less than 115 mm) and no medical"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0060", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "prevention", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0061_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How is a child's appetite assessed during visits for moderate acute malnutrition treatment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "complication should be assessed for appetite.\nAppetite is assessed on the initial visit and at each follow-up visit to the health facility. Arrange a quiet corner where the child and mother can take their time to get accustomed to eating the\nRUTF. Usually the child eats the RUTF portion in 30 minutes.\nExplain to the mother:\nThe purpose of assessing the child's appetite.\nWhat is ready-to-use-therapeutic food (RUTF).\nHow to give RUTF:\nWash hands before giving the RUTF.\nSit with the child on the lap and gently offer the child RUTF to eat.\nEncourage the child to eat the RUTF without feeding by force.\nOffer plenty of clean water to drink from a cup when the child is eating the RUTF.\nOffer appropriate amount of RUTF to the child to eat:\nAfter 30 minutes check if the child was able to finish or not able to finish the amount of RUTF given and decide:\nChild ABLE to finish at least one-third of a packet of RUTF portion (92 g) or 3 teaspoons from a pot within 30 minutes.\nChild NOT ABLE to eat one-third of a packet of RUTF portion (92 g) or 3 teaspoons from a pot within 30 minutes.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:22)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nFEEDING COUNSELLING\nAssess Child's Feeding\nAssess feeding if child is Less Than 2 Years Old, Has MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION, ANAEMIA, CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION, or is HIV EXPOSED. Ask questions about the child's usual\nfeeding and feeding during this illness. Compare the mother's answers to the"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0061", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0062_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What feeding assessment questions should be asked for a child with moderate acute malnutrition, anemia, confirmed HIV infection, or who is HIV exposed?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Has MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION, ANAEMIA, CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION, or is HIV EXPOSED. Ask questions about the child's usual\nfeeding and feeding during this illness. Compare the mother's answers to the Feeding Recommendations for the child's age.\nASK - How are you feeding your child?\nIf the child is receiving any breast milk, ASK:\nHow many times during the day?\nDo you also breastfeed during the night?\nDoes the child take any other food or fluids?\nWhat food or fluids?\nHow many times per day?\nWhat do you use to feed the child?\nIf MODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION or if a child with CONFIRMED HIV INFECTION fails to gain weight or loses weight between monthly measurements, ASK:\nHow large are servings?\nDoes the child receive his own serving?\nWho feeds the child and how?\nWhat foods are available in the home?\nDuring this illness, has the child's feeding changed?\nIf yes, how?\nIn addition, for HIV EXPOSED child:\nIf mother and child are on ARV treatment or prophylaxis and child breastfeeding, ASK:\nDo you take ARV drugs? Do you take all doses, miss doses, do not take medication?\nDoes the child take ARV drugs (If the policy is to take ARV prophylaxis until 1 week after breastfeeding has stopped)? Does he or she take all doses, missed doses,\ndoes not take medication?\nIf child not breastfeeding, ASK:\nWhat milk are you giving?\nHow many times during the day and night?\nHow much is given at each feed?\nHow are you preparing the milk?\nLet the mother demonstrate or explain how a feed is prepared, and ho"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0062", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0063_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the feeding recommendations for children from birth up to 2 years, including HIV-exposed children on ARV prophylaxis?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "What milk are you giving?\nHow many times during the day and night?\nHow much is given at each feed?\nHow are you preparing the milk?\nLet the mother demonstrate or explain how a feed is prepared, and how it is given to the infant.\nAre you giving any breast milk at all?\nAre you able to get new supplies of milk before you run out?\nHow is the milk being given? Cup or bottle?\nHow are you cleaning the feeding utensils?\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nFEEDING COUNSELLING\nFeeding Recommendations\nFeeding recommendations FOR ALL CHILDREN during sickness and health, and including HIV EXPOSED children on ARV prophylaxis\nNewborn, birth up to 1 week 1 week up to 6 6 up to 9 months 9 up to 12 months 12 months up to 2 years 2 years and older\nmonths\nImmediately after birth, put your baby in Breastfeed as often Breastfeed as Breastfeed as often Breastfeed as often Give a variety of\nskin to skin contact with you. as your child wants. often as your child as your child wants. as your child wants. family foods to\nAllow your baby to take the breast within Look for signs of wants. Also give a variety of Also give a variety of your child,\nthe first hour. Give your baby colostrum, hunger, such as Also give thick mashed or finely mashed or finely including animal-\nthe first yellowish, thick milk. It protects beginning to fuss, porridge or well- chopped family food, chopped family food, source foods and\nthe baby from"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0063", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
