setting where the national authorities recommend to avoid all breastfe"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0065", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0066_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the feeding recommendations for HIV-exposed infants on infant formula up to 6 months of age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ING\nFeeding Recommendations for HIV EXPOSED Child on Infant Formula\nThese feeding recommendations are for HIV EXPOSED children in setting where the national authorities recommend to avoid all breastfeeding or when the mother has chosen\nformula feeding.\nPMTCT: If the baby is on AZT for prophylaxis, continue until 4 to 6 weeks of age.\nUp to 6 months 6 up to 12 monts 12 months up to 2 years Safe preparation of replacement feeding\nInfant formula\nAlways use a marked cup or glass and\nspoon to measure water and the\nscoop to measure the formula\npowder.\nWash your hands before preparing a\nfeed.\nBring the water to boil and then let it\ncool. Keep it covered while it cools.\nFORMULA FEED exclusively. Do not give Give 1-2 cups (250 - 500 ml) of infant Give 1-2 cups (250 - 500 ml) of boiled,\nMeasure the formula powder into a\nany breast milk. Other foods or fluids formula or boiled, then cooled, full then cooled, full cream milk or infant\nmarked cup or glass. Make the scoops\nare not necessary. cream milk. Give milk with a cup, not a formula.\nlevel. Put in one scoop for every 25 ml\nPrepare correct strength and amount bottle. Give milk with a cup, not a bottle.\nof water.\njust before use. Use milk within two Give: Give: Add a small amount of the cooled\n(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:86)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:39)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:178)(cid:68)(cid:3)(ci"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0066", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0067_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the recommended feeding amounts and methods for infants based on their age?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": ")(cid:3)(cid:39)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:178)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:74)(cid:72)\nboiled water and stir. Fill the cup or\ncan store formula for 24 hours.\nglass to the mark with the water. Stir\nCup feeding is safer than bottle\nwell.\nfeeding. Clean the cup and utensils * * Feed the infant using a cup.\nwith hot soapy water.\nStart by giving 2-3 tablespoons of food 2 or family foods 3 or 4 times per day. Give Wash the utensils.\nGive the following amounts of formula 8 - 3 times a day. Gradually increase to 1/2 3/4 cup (1 cup = 250 ml) at each meal.\nto 6 times per day: cup (1 cup = 250 ml) at each meal and to\nOffer 1-2 snacks between meals. (cid:38)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:78)\nAge in months Approx. amount and times giving meals 3-4 times a day.\nper day Offer 1-2 snacks each day when the Continue to feed your child slowly, Cow' s or other animal milks are not\n0 up to 1 60 ml x 8 child seems hungry. patiently. suitable for infants below 6 months of\n1 up to 2 90 ml x 7 For snacks give small chewable items Encourage - but do not force - your child age (even modified).\n2 up to 4 120 ml x 6 that the child can hold. Let your child try to to eat. For a child between 6 and 12 month of\n4 up to 6 150 ml x 6 eat the snack, but provide help if needed. age: boil the milk and let it cool (even"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0067", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0068_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "How should a mother gradually stop breastfeeding over one month?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "6 that the child can hold. Let your child try to to eat. For a child between 6 and 12 month of\n4 up to 6 150 ml x 6 eat the snack, but provide help if needed. age: boil the milk and let it cool (even if\npasteurized).\nFeed the baby using a cup.\n* A good daily diet should be adequate in quantity and include an energy-rich food (for example, thick cereal with added oil); meat, fish, eggs, or pulses; and fruits and vegetables.\n(cid:3490)\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nFEEDING COUNSELLING\nStopping Breastfeeding\nSTOPPING BREASTFEEDING means changing from all breast milk to no breast milk.\nThis should happen gradually over one month. Plan in advance for a safe transition.\n1. HELP MOTHER PREPARE:\nMother should discuss and plan in advance with her family, if possible\nExpress milk and give by cup\n(cid:41)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:74)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:88)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:74)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:80)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:78)(cid:12)\nLearn how to prepare a store milk s"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0068", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0069_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the feeding recommendations for a child with persistent diarrhea who is taking other milk?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "id:88)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:80)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:78)(cid:12)\nLearn how to prepare a store milk safely at home\n2. HELP MOTHER MAKE TRANSITION:\nTeach mother to cup feed (See chart booklet Counsel part in Assess, classify and treat the sick young infant aged up to 2 months)\nClean all utensils with soap and water\n(cid:54)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:74)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:78)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:70)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:78)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:88)(cid:83)\n3. STOP BREASTFEEDING COMPLETELY:\nExpress and discard enough breast milk to keep comfortable until lactation stops\nFeeding Recommendations For a Child Who Has PERSISTENT DIARRHOEA\nIf still breastfeeding, give more frequent, longer breastfeeds, day and night.\nIf taking other milk:\nreplace with increased breastfeeding OR\nreplace with fermented milk products, such as yoghurt OR\nreplace half the milk with nutrient-rich semisolid food.\nFor other foods, follow feeding recommendations for the child's age.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0069", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0070_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the feeding recommendations for a sick child, including those with diarrhea?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "replace with fermented milk products, such as yoghurt OR\nreplace half the milk with nutrient-rich semisolid food.\nFor other foods, follow feeding recommendations for the child's age.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:26)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nEXTRA FLUIDS AND MOTHER'S HEALTH\nAdvise the Mother to Increase Fluid During Illness\nFOR ANY SICK CHILD:\nBreastfeed more frequently and for longer at each feed. If child is taking breast-milk substitutes, increase the amount of milk given.\nIncrease other fluids. For example, give soup, rice water, yoghurt drinks or clean water.\nFOR CHILD WITH DIARRHOEA:\nGiving extra fluid can be lifesaving. Give fluid according to Plan A or Plan B on TREAT THE CHILD chart.\nCounsel the Mother about her Own Health\nIf the mother is sick, provide care for her, or refer her for help.\nIf she has a breast problem (such as engorgement, sore nipples, breast infection), provide care for her or refer her for help.\nAdvise her to eat well to keep up her own strength and health.\nCheck the mother's immunization status and give her tetanus toxoid if needed.\nMake sure she has access to:\nFamily planning\nCounselling on STD and AIDS prevention.\nGive additional counselling if the mother is HIV-positive\n(cid:53)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:74)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0070", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0071_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a mother return to the health worker for follow-up after a child is diagnosed with pneumonia?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": ":86)(cid:86)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:74)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:16)(cid:88)(cid:83)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:88)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:68)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:87)(cid:75)\nEmphasize good hygiene, and early treatment of illnesses\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:22)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nWHEN TO RETURN\nAdvise the Mother When to Return to Health Worker\nFOLLOW-UP VISIT: Advise the mother to come for follow-up at the earliest time listed for the child's\nproblems.\nIf the child has: Return for\nfollow-up in:\nPNEUMONIA 3 days\nDYSENTERY\nMALARIA, if fever persists\nFEVER: NO MALARIA, if fever persists\nWHEN TO RETURN IMMEDIATELY\nMEASLES WITH EYE OR MOUTH\nAdvise mo"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0071", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0072_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a mother return immediately for a child with measles?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ms.\nIf the child has: Return for\nfollow-up in:\nPNEUMONIA 3 days\nDYSENTERY\nMALARIA, if fever persists\nFEVER: NO MALARIA, if fever persists\nWHEN TO RETURN IMMEDIATELY\nMEASLES WITH EYE OR MOUTH\nAdvise mother to return immediately if the child has any of these signs:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0072", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0073_direct_question", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with persistent diarrhoea return for medical evaluation?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "COMPLICATIONS\nMOUTH OR GUM ULCERS OR THRUSH Any sick child Not able to drink or breastfeed\nBecomes sicker\nPERSISTENT DIARRHOEA 5 days\nDevelops a fever\nACUTE EAR INFECTION\nCHRONIC EAR INFECTION If child has COUGH OR COLD, also return if: Fast breathing\nCOUGH OR COLD, if not improving Difficult breathing\nUNCOMPLICATED SEVERE ACUTE 14 days If child has diarrhoea, also return if: Blood in stool\nMALNUTRITION Drinking poorly\nFEEDING PROBLEM\nANAEMIA 14 days\nMODERATE ACUTE MALNUTRITION 30 days\nCONFIRMED HIV INFECTION According to national\nHIV EXPOSED recommendations\nNEXT WELL-CHILD VISIT: Advise the mother to return for next immunization according to"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0073", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "direct_question", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
