{"id": "qa_sats_0025_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the PCHS protocol, what are the clinical signs indicating a threatened limb in an adult?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "F KCEHC %29 woleb fi - 20pS kcehC tniop 1 serocs RR fI suser ot evom dna 20 evig SWET no erom ro tset esoculg kcirp regnif a oD ssensuoicsnoc fo level decudeR htlaeH roineS ot revo dnah dna ypeels erom gnidulcni ,trela ton( )PCHS( lanoisseforP eraC )lamron naht lamron sa evom ro tis ot elbanU tset esoculg kcirp regnif a oD dlihc eht rof TRO tratS aeohrraiD PCHS ot revo dnaH noitardyhed dna ylno gnitimoV - noitirtunlaM tset esoculg kcirp regnif a oD gnitsaw ereves elbisiv gnittip htiw - noitirtunlaM tset esoculg kcirp regnif a oD teef HTOB fo amedeo tset esoculg kcirp regnif a oD suser ot evom L/lomm 3 woleb fI setebaid fo yrotsiH\nERACHTLAEH\nROINES\nPCHS htiw kcehc ”IH“ fI\nNOITERCSID\nS’LANOISSEFORP\nfi nibolgomeah kcirp regniF ,RP gnideelB :gnideelb fo yrotsiH PCHS htiw kcehc ssel ro 8 amuart fo etis eht morf ro OP\nAdult Clinical Signs Adult Clinical Signs\n3 ADULT CLInICAL sIGns Level of consciousness reduced / confused A Is the patient Alert? If not,\nAny patient that is not fully alert (i.e. confused, V Is the patient responding to Voice? If not,\nonly responding to a verbal stimulus, painful P Is the patient responding to Pain?\nLearning Objectives: stimulus or unresponsive). U The patient who is Unresponsive to voice\nAnD to pain is Unconscious.\n• Be familiar with the adult emergency signs Threatened limb\n• Be familiar with the adult very urgent and urgent signs A patient presenting with A threatened limb presents as:\na painful, pale, pulseless, P Pain P Pulselessness P Capillar"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0025", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0026_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does WHO recommend for identifying a threatened limb in an adult patient?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "y signs Threatened limb\n• Be familiar with the adult very urgent and urgent signs A patient presenting with A threatened limb presents as:\na painful, pale, pulseless, P Pain P Pulselessness P Capillary Refill Delay\nweak, numb limb. P Pallor P Paralysis or pins & needles P Temperature\n3.1 EMERGEnCY sIGns\nEMERGEnCY It is important to note that if a patient has 3.3 URGEnT sIGns URGEnT\nany emergency signs then a TEWs does\nObstructed airway – not breathing nOT need to be calculated to categorise haemorrhage - controlled haemorrhage - controlled\nseizures - current them as RED. There should be no delay in This refers to a situation where a patient Dislocation of finger OR toe\ntaking the patient to the resuscitation area. presents with an active bleed and you as the\nBurn – facial /inhalation The first set of vitals may be obtained in the triage provider apply direct pressure with a dry Fracture – closed (no break in the skin)\nhypoglycaemia – glucose less than 3 mmol/L resuscitation area or in the ambulance. dressing and are able to control the bleed. Burn - other\nThis does not refer to a patient presenting with\nCardiac arrest dry blood. Abdominal pain\nDiabetic – glucose over 17 (no ketonuria)\nBurn - facial inhalation Abdominal pain\nVomiting persistently\nAny patient in whom the airway has potentially been exposed to heat (e.g.trapped in a house fire, In all females of child-bearing age additional\nhot water burn to face with possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face or mouth)"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0026", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0027_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, what investigations should be performed in all females of child-bearing age who have sustained a hot water burn to the face?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "potentially been exposed to heat (e.g.trapped in a house fire, In all females of child-bearing age additional\nhot water burn to face with possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face or mouth) may get investigations (i.e. urine dipstick and urine Pregnancy & trauma\nrapid swelling of the airway. Adults presenting with this emergency sign may have singed facial pregnancy test) should be performed to pick Pregnancy & PV bleed\nhairs (eyelashes, eyebrows), carbonaceous material in and around their nose/mouth and should be up a possible ectopic pregnancy.\ntriaged Red. Moderate pain\nOther emergency signs for adults include an obstructed airway (patient not breathing), a patient Assessment Questions\nconvulsing, and hypoglycaemia with a glucose less than 3 mmol/L or cardiac arrest.\nClearly indicate whether the following statements\nare true or false:\n3.2 VERY URGEnT sIGns\n1. Singed facial hairs and soot around the nose and mouth are an indication of inhalation burn\nVERY URGEnT in a patient that has been trapped in a burning house.\nTrue False\nhigh energy transfer Focal neurology – acute Burn – circumferential\n(severe mechanism of injury) (stroke) 2. Haemorrhage controlled refers to a patient whose active bleed was controlled by the triage\nprovider by applying direct pressure with a dry dressing.\nshortness of breath - acute Aggression Burn – chemical\nTrue False\nLevel of consciousness Threatened limb Poisoning / Overdose\nreduced / confused 3. A patient in cardiac arrest is immediat"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0027", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0028_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per SATS protocol, what are examples of high energy transfers?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ry dressing.\nshortness of breath - acute Aggression Burn – chemical\nTrue False\nLevel of consciousness Threatened limb Poisoning / Overdose\nreduced / confused 3. A patient in cardiac arrest is immediately categorised Red.\nCoughing blood Eye injury Diabetic – glucose over True False\n11 and ketonuria\nChoose the correct answer:\nChest pain Dislocation of larger joint Vomiting fresh blood\n(not finger or toe) 4. Examples of high energy transfers include:\n(a) Motor vehicle accident at 60 km/h\nstabbed neck Fracture - compound Pregnancy and\n(b) Fall from a height of ten meters\n(with a break in skin) abdominal trauma\n(c) Pedestrian vehicle accident at 50 km/h\nhaemorrhage – uncontrolled Burn over 20% Pregnancy and (d) High velocity gunshot wound\n(arterial bleed) abdominal pain (e) All of the above\nseizure – post ictal Burn – electrical Severe Pain 5. A threatened limb may present as:\n(a) Pain in the affected limb\nHigh energy transfer (severe mechanism of injury) (b) A pale, pulseless affected limb\nIn our context this refers to high speed injuries. Examples of these include a motor vehicle accident of (c) A weak or numb affected limb\n40 km/h or more, pedestrian vehicle accident, a fall from a roof or a high velocity gunshot wound. (d) The affected limb is cold and has poor perfusion\n(e) All of the above\n12 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 13\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediatric Clinical Signs\nIS THE CHILD BREATHING?\n4 PAEDIATRIC CLInICAL sIGns\nIf active, talking, or cry"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0028", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0029_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per SATS protocol, what are the three steps to assess if a child is breathing?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "f the above\n12 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 13\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediatric Clinical Signs\nIS THE CHILD BREATHING?\n4 PAEDIATRIC CLInICAL sIGns\nIf active, talking, or crying, the child is obviously\nbreathing. If in any doubt you must AssEss three things\nLearning Objectives: to check if the child is breathing (see Figure 6):\n• LOOK - to see if the chest is moving.\n• Be familiar with the paediatric emergency signs\n• LIsTEn - for any breathing sounds.\n• Understand the ABC-c-c-DO approach for emergency signs Are they normal?\n• Be familiar with the paediatric very urgent and urgent signs • FEEL - Can you feel the breath at the nose or\nmouth of the child?\nIf the child is not breathing (or you are not sure if\n4.1 EMERGEnCY sIGns: The ABC-c-c-DO approach there is breathing), you need to take the child to the"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0029", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0030_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does WHO recommend for identifying emergency signs during triage?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage of patients involves looking for signs of serious illness or injury. These emergency signs relate to resuscitation area where the breathing needs to be\nthe Airway-Breathing-Circulation/Coma/Convulsion-Dehydration-Other and are easily remembered artificially supported by ventilating the child with a\nas “ABC-c-c-DO”. Each letter refers to an emergency sign which, when identified, should alert you to bag valve mask (BVM) device whilst the child is further\na patient who is seriously ill and needs immediate intervention. It is important to note that if a patient assessed and managed appropriately.\nhas any emergency signs then a TEWS does NOT need to be calculated. There should be no delay\nFigure 6: Look, listen and feel\nin taking the patient to the resuscitation area. The first set of vitals may be taken in the resuscitation IS THE BREATHING OBSTRUCTED?\narea or in the ambulance on the way to the hospital. Noisy breathing can be a sign that the airway maybe threatened or partially obstructed. This is most\ncommon in patients with a decreased level of consciousness, upper respiratory tract infections or\nEMERGEnCY aspiration of foreign bodies. Obstructed breathing can also be due to blockage by the tongue or the\npatient’s own secretions if these are not being swallowed.\nAirway and Breathing not breathing or reported apnoea\nObstructed breathing ARE THERE ABNORMAL RESPIRATORY NOISES?\nCentral cyanosis or spO 2 less than 92% Are there any noises heard when breathing in? A harsh noi"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0030", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0031_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does WHO recommend for assessing central cyanosis in a child with suspected respiratory distress?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Breathing not breathing or reported apnoea\nObstructed breathing ARE THERE ABNORMAL RESPIRATORY NOISES?\nCentral cyanosis or spO 2 less than 92% Are there any noises heard when breathing in? A harsh noise on breathing in is called stridor, a short nois e\nRespiratory distress (severe) when breathing out in young infants is called grunting. Both noises are signs of severe respiratory problems.\nCirculation Cold hands + 2 or more of the following:\nNB: A blocked nose is an extremely common cause of noisy breathing, but it is NOT life-threatening.\n(i) pulse weak and fast\n(ii) capillary refill time 3 sec or more\n(iii) lethargic DOES THE CHILD SHOW CENTRAL CYANOSIS?\nUncontrolled bleeding (not nosebleed) DEFInITIOn: Cyanosis occurs when there is an abnormally low level of oxygen in the blood.\nThis produces a bluish or purplish discoloration of the tongue, the inside of the mouth and the skin. This\nComa AVPU: Responds only to Pain (P) OR Unresponsive (U)\nsign may be absent in a child who has severe anaemia.\nConfusion\nTo AssEss for central cyanosis:\nConvulsions Convulsing or immediately post-ictal and not alert LOOK - at the mouth and tongue. A bluish or purplish discoloration of the tongue and the inside of the\nDehydration Diarrhoea or vomiting + 2 or more of the following: mouth indicates central cyanosis.\n(i) Lethargy/floppy infant MEAsURE - If oxygen saturation monitoring is available you can check the child’s oxygen levels.\n(ii) Very sunken eyes First ensure that the probe is correct"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0031", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0032_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the protocol, what oxygen saturation level indicates an emergency in an infant with central cyanosis?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "entral cyanosis.\n(i) Lethargy/floppy infant MEAsURE - If oxygen saturation monitoring is available you can check the child’s oxygen levels.\n(ii) Very sunken eyes First ensure that the probe is correctly sited and that a good regular trace is showing on the monitor. If\n(iii) Skin pinch very slow - 2 sec or more oxygen saturation is < 92% in room air the child has low oxygen levels and this is an emergency.\nOther Facial /inhalation burn DOES THE CHILD HAVE SEVERE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS?\nhypoglycaemia recorded at any time - glucose less than 3 mmol/L To AssEss whether the child has severe respiratory distress Signs of severe respiratory distress:\nPurpuric rash check for the following signs: • Very fast breathing\n• Is the child having trouble getting breath so that it is • Severe lower chest wall indrawing\nHOW TO Triage? difficult to talk, eat or breastfeed? • Use of auxiliary muscles\nKeep in mind the ABC-c-c-DO steps: To assess if the child has airway or breathing • Is the child breathing very fast? • Head nodding\nAirway, Breathing, Circulation, Coma, problems you need to know: • Does the child have severe chest indrawing? This can • Inability to feed because of\nConvulsion, Dehydration and Other. • Is the child breathing? be intercostal (between the ribs), subcostal (below the respiratory problems\nAB • Is the airway obstructed? ribcage) , suprasternal (above the sternum) or sternal • Grunting and flaring\n4.1.1 AIRWAY AnD BREAThInG • Is the child blue (centrally cyanosed)? indrawin"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0032", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0033_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per SATS protocol, what are the key signs to look for when assessing a child's airway and breathing?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "tory problems\nAB • Is the airway obstructed? ribcage) , suprasternal (above the sternum) or sternal • Grunting and flaring\n4.1.1 AIRWAY AnD BREAThInG • Is the child blue (centrally cyanosed)? indrawing (the breastbone is sucked in on inspiration).\nARE UsUALLY AssEssED TOGEThER\n• Does the child have nasal flaring or a grunting noise on expiration?\nThe letters A and B in\n• Is the child using the accessory muscles of the neck for breathing? This can cause the head to\n“ABC-c-c-DO” represent “airway and breathing”.\nnod or bob with every breath. This is particularly seen in young babies.\nIt is evident that an open (patent) airway is needed for breathing. An airway\nor breathing problem is life-threatening and must receive your attention before you move on to • Exhaustion: If the child’s breathing is very laboured, especially if it has been like this for some\nother systems. It is therefore convenient that the first two letters of the alphabet represent the two time, then s/he may become exhausted? If this happens then the signs of increased work of\nmost important areas to look for emergency or priority signs. If there is no problem with the airway o r breathing can actually decrease as the child is to tired - this is a very dangerous sign.\nbreathing, you should look for signs in the areas represented by C. If you see these signs then it is likely that the child has severe respiratory distress.\n14 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 15\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediat"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0033", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0034_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per SATS protocol, at what oxygen saturation level should a child with respiratory distress be given oxygen therapy?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "s represented by C. If you see these signs then it is likely that the child has severe respiratory distress.\n14 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 15\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediatric Clinical Signs\nMEAsURE: In any child with respiratory distress, you should check oxygen saturation levels if you have IS THE CHILD CONVULSING NOW OR POST-ICTAL?\nan oxygen saturation monitor. First ensure that the probe is well sited and that you have a good trace This assessment depends on your observation of the child and on the history from the parent. Childre n\non the monitor – if the oxygen saturation is less than 92% in room air, this child has an emergency who have a history of convulsion, but are alert during triage, will need a complete clinical history an d\nsign and needs oxygen therapy. However if a child is obviously in severe respiratory distress, oxygen investigation by a clinician, but at triage they are not assigned an emergency clinical sign, as they do n ot\nsaturation levels do not need to be checked at triage to confirm this emergency sign, rather take the usually require any resuscitation immediately.\nchild to the resuscitation area immediately.\nThe child may be seen to have a convulsion during the triage process or while waiting in the outpatie nt\nIf the child is breathing adequately, go to the next section to quickly continue the assessment for department. You can recognize a convulsion by the sudden loss of consciousness associated wit h"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0034", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0035_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does WHO recommend for a child showing stiffening of the arms and legs along with uncontrolled jerky movements?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "emergency signs. If the child has an airway or breathing problem, you should initiate appropriate uncontrolled jerky movements of the limbs and/or the face. There is stiffening of the child’s arms and leg s"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0035", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0036_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, what are the signs to assess for severe dehydration in a child with diarrhoea or vomiting?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "treatment and then quickly resume the assessment. and uncontrolled movements of the limbs. The child may lose control of the bladder, and is unconsciou s\nduring the convulsion and drowsy afterwards .\nC\n4.1.2 CIRCULATIOn AssEssMEnT Sometimes, in small infants, the jerky limb movements may be absent, but there may be more subt le\nFIRsT FEEL ThE ChILDs hAnDs – IF ThEY ARE WARM YOU DO nOT nEED TO ChECK ThE CIRCULATIOn AnY twitching movements of the face, mouth, eyes, hands or feet. You have to observe the infant careful ly.\nFURThER. MOVE On TO ThE nEXT EMERGEnCY sIGn.\nD To assess if the child is severely\nIF ThE hAnDs ARE COLD OR COOL – YOU nEED TO RAPIDLY AssEss FOR OThER sIGns OF CIRCULATORY 4.1.4 sEVERE DEhYDRATIOn AssEssMEnT\ndehydrated you need to know:\nshOCK: PULsE VOLUME AnD RATE; CAPILLARY REFILL TIME AnD LEThARGY. In this section we will look at the assessment of severe\n• Is the child lethargic?\ndehydration in the child with diarrhoea or vomiting. If the\nIS THE PULSE WEAK AND FAST? To assess if the child has circulation problems you child is severely malnourished the signs of dehydration • Does the child have very\nThe radial pulse (the pulse at the wrist) should need to know: are not as reliable. sunken eyes?\nbe felt. If this is strong and not obviously fast , • Does the child have cool or cold hands? IF YEs • Does a skin pinch take 2 seconds\nthe pulse is adequate; no further assessmen t DOES THE CHILD HAVE DIARRHOEA OR VOMITING? or more to go back?\n- Is the capillary refi"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0036", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0037_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the protocol, what signs indicate a child has severe dehydration and needs immediate resuscitation?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "have cool or cold hands? IF YEs • Does a skin pinch take 2 seconds\nthe pulse is adequate; no further assessmen t DOES THE CHILD HAVE DIARRHOEA OR VOMITING? or more to go back?\n- Is the capillary refill time (CRT) 3 seconds or more?\nis needed. The radial pulse is used as an initia l This information comes from the parent or guardian.\n- If the CRT is prolonged is the pulse weak and fast?\nscreen because it is easy to access withou t If the child has no diarrhoea or vomiting, do not check for dehydration. Move to the next assessment.\n- Is the child lethargic\nundressing the patient. If the child has diarrhoea or vomiting assess for severe dehydration.\nIf the radial pulse is difficult to find, you need to look for a more central pulse (a pulse nearer to the A history of diarrhoea or vomiting and the presence of two or more of the signs in the box above mean s\nheart). In an infant (less than one year of age) the best place to look is at the middle of the upper the child has severe dehydration and needs to be taken to the resuscitation area immediately.\narm, the brachial pulse. If the child is lying down you could look for the femoral pulse in the groin.\nThe pulse should be strong. If the more central pulse feels weak, decide if it also seems fast. This is a IS THE CHILD LETHARGIC?\nsubjective judgement and an exact count is not taken. If the central pulse is weak and fast, the child In the older child lethargy is quite easy to assess. You have already\nneeds further assessment and po"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0037", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0038_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, how can a clinician assess for lethargy in an older child during triage?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ective judgement and an exact count is not taken. If the central pulse is weak and fast, the child In the older child lethargy is quite easy to assess. You have already\nneeds further assessment and possible treatment for shock. assessed the state of consciousness of the child using the AVPU scale.\nAll these procedures can and should be practised on yourself , Now observe if the child appears drowsy and does not show interest in\nyour friends, your children and family, and finally on rea l what is happening around him/her. A lethargic child may not look at the\npatients. Practice is the best way to improve on finding pulse s mother or watch your face when you talk. The child may stare blankly\nand measuring capillary refill. and appear not to notice what is going on around him/her.\nDoes the child know his/her name and answer questions sensibly? If the\nNote that we do not recommend blood pressure to assess fo r\nchild responds to voice but remains drowsy, he/she is lethargic. In the\nshock at triage because of two reasons:\nyounger child, signs of lethargy are harder to assess.\n1) Low blood pressure is a late sign of shock in children and\nthey will already have other obvious signs and 2) the blood\nDOEs ThE ChILD hAVE VERY SUNKEN EYES?\npressure in children is less predictive at triage than in adults.\nFigure 7: Look at the child’s eyes to determine if they appear unusually sunken in\n4.1.3 C-C COMA AnD COnVULsIOns AssEssMEnT Feeling the brachial pulse in an infant their sockets (see Fig"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0038", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0039_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the protocol, how can you assess if a child is in a coma?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "n in adults.\nFigure 7: Look at the child’s eyes to determine if they appear unusually sunken in\n4.1.3 C-C COMA AnD COnVULsIOns AssEssMEnT Feeling the brachial pulse in an infant their sockets (see Figure 8). Ask the mother if the child’s eyes are more\nsunken than usual.\nFigure 8: Sunken eyes\nIS THE CHILD IN A COMA? To assess for coma and convulsions\nDOES A SKIN PINCH GO BACK VERY SLOWLY (2 SECONDS OR MORE)?\nA child who is awake is obviously conscious and you can you need to know:\nThis is a simple test to look at how elastic the skin is. If the child is not dehydrated, the skin will be elastic\nmove to the next component of the assessment. If the child (i) Is the child’s level of consciousness\nand, when pinched and released, will return to normal straight away. Try this on yourself.\nis asleep, ask the mother if the child is just sleeping. If there disturbed?\nThe dehydrated\nis any doubt, you need to assess the level of consciousness.\n(ii) Is the child convulsing now? child will have lost\nTry to wake the child by talking to him/her, e.g. call his/her fluid. The body\nname loudly. A child who does not respond to this should be gently shaken. A little shake to the arm o r moves fluid from less\nleg should be enough to wake a sleeping child. If this is unsuccessful, apply a firm squeeze to the nai l important places,\nbed, enough to cause some pain. A child who does not such as the skin,\nA Is the child Alert? If not, wake to voice or being shaken or to pain is unconscious. to maintain"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0039", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0040_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per SATS protocol, how should the skin pinch test be performed to assess hydration in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "o the nai l important places,\nbed, enough to cause some pain. A child who does not such as the skin,\nA Is the child Alert? If not, wake to voice or being shaken or to pain is unconscious. to maintain the\nV Is the child responding to Voice? To help you assess the consciousness level of a child, a circulation. The skin\nIf not, simple scale (AVPU) is used: becomes less elastic\nP Is the child responding to Pain? and, when pinched,\nA child who is not alert, but responds to voice, is lethargic.\nU The child who is Unresponsive An unconscious child may or may not respond to pain. Any is slow to return.\nto voice (or being shaken) child with a coma scale of “P” or “U” is an emergency and Locate the area on\nAND to pain is Unconscious. needs to be taken to the resuscitation area immediately. the child’s abdomen\nhalfway between Figure 9: Skin pinch\n16 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 17\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Paediatric Clinical Signs\nthe umbilicus and the side of the abdomen. Avoiding using your fingertips, as this is painful. Pinch the Tiny baby (less than two months of age)\nskin in a vertical (head to foot) direction and not across the child’s body. You should pick up all the If the child appears very young, ask the mother his age. If the child is ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nlayers of the skin and the fat tissue underneath. Pinch for one second and then release. See whethe r obviously not a young baby, you do not need to ask this question. Tiny baby \nthe skin goes back very"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0040", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0041_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What does WHO recommend for assessing dehydration in small babies with diarrhoea?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "rs of the skin and the fat tissue underneath. Pinch for one second and then release. See whethe r obviously not a young baby, you do not need to ask this question. Tiny baby \nthe skin goes back very slowly (2 seconds or more). Refer to shCP\nSmall babies are more difficult to assess properly, more prone to\nSevere dehydration is present if the child has a history of diarrhoea plus any two of the following sign s: getting infections (from other patients), and more likely to deteriorate\nlethargy, sunken eyes or very slow skin pinch (2 seconds or more ). quickly if unwell. All tiny babies of under two months of age should therefore be seen very urgently.\nO\n4.1.5 OThER EMERGEnCY sIGns Perform a finger prick glucotest in Inconsolably crying/ severe pain\nFacial inhalational burns the following cases: The inconsolable crying child is conscious but cries ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n• Reduced level of consciousness constantly and will not settle. Ask the parent of caregiver if Pain  check with shCP\nAny child in whom the airway has potentially been exposed • Unable to sit or move as usual the child is upset/frightened by the unfamiliar environment for analgesia initiation\nto heat (e.g.trapped in a house fire, hot water burn to face • Current or recent seizure or whether this is the presenting complaint. If a child has\nwith possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face • Known with diabetes severe pain and is in agony, s/he should be prioritized to\nor mouth) may get rapid swelling of the airwa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0041", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0042_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, what is the triage priority for a child presenting with severe pain and a blood glucose level less than 3 mmol/L?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "t. If a child has\nwith possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face • Known with diabetes severe pain and is in agony, s/he should be prioritized to\nor mouth) may get rapid swelling of the airway. Children • Severe malnutrition receive very urgent assessment and pain relief. Severe pain may be due to severe conditions such as\npresenting with this emergency sign should be triaged Red. acute abdomen, meningitis, etc. You are not required to do a formal pain scale assessment, but for\nyour information there are some examples of pain scales appended at the end of part four.\nGlucose <3mmol/L at any time\nChildren with a low blood sugar are often very sick and need to be seen immediately. They should Presenting\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn WARnInG\nbe triaged Red if found to have a blood glucose concentration of less than 3 mmol/L. complaint -\n‘more sleepy ! Reduced level of consciousness If glucose less than 3 mmol/L\nPurpuric Rash than normal’  do finger prick glucose test  take to resuscitation area\nChildren who present with a complaint of a rash - should be assessed to see if the spots blanch with When the\npressure from fingertip or glass test (i.e. turns white when pressure is applied). If the spots are non- mother complains that her child that is more sleepy than normal (NOT just in natural sleep), the child\nblanching (i.e. does not turn white when pressure is applied) - then there is a risk of meningococca l may be in the early stages of serious conditions such as meningitis"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0042", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0043_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, what should be done if a child presents with a rash that does not blanch when pressure is applied?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in natural sleep), the child\nblanching (i.e. does not turn white when pressure is applied) - then there is a risk of meningococca l may be in the early stages of serious conditions such as meningitis, hypoglycaemia, septicaemia etc,\ndisease. Children presenting with this emergency sign should be triaged Red. If you are unsure about a and will need to be identified and managed very urgently. Even if the child appears alert at triage\nchild presenting with a rash ask the senior healthcare professional. still assign a Very Urgent clinical sign as we have to take seriously the mothers concern.\nYou have now learned how to recognize the obvious emergency signs in paediatric patients. The\nnext section will cover the very urgent and urgent signs respectively. It is important to note that if a Poisoning or Overdose\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\npatient has any emergency signs then a TEWS does NOT need to be calculated. There should be A child with a history of swallowing drugs or other dangerou s\nno delay in taking the patient to the resuscitation area. The first set of vitals may be taken in the substances needs to be assessed very urgently, as s/he can Poisoning/Overdose\nresuscitation area or in the ambulance on the way to the hospital. deteriorate rapidly and might need specific treatment depending  Refer to shCP\non the substance taken. The mother will tell you if she has brough t\n• If any EMERGENCY signs have been found the TEWS should NOT be calculated at triage, the the child because of poss"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0043", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0044_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the guideline, what should be done if a child presents with new focal neurological signs but is fully conscious?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": " Refer to shCP\non the substance taken. The mother will tell you if she has brough t\n• If any EMERGENCY signs have been found the TEWS should NOT be calculated at triage, the the child because of possible intoxication. Consult SHCP for advice regarding very urgent management.\nchild is within the RED category and should be taken to the resuscitation area.\n• Children within the RED category need emergency care and should be seen immediately. Focal neurology acute\n• Always ensure that the child with emergency signs is handed over directly to a SHCP. A child who is fully conscious but has new focal neurological signs will need very urgent assessment.\nIn this case, the child does not require emergency treatment because they do not have any ABCD"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0044", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0045_protocol_lookup", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Per the triage protocol, what are examples of VERY URGENT signs that may indicate a vascular stroke in a child?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "emergency signs, but may need urgent imaging and intervention. Examples of focal neurological\n• I f there are no EMERGEnCY signs, check to see whether the child has any VERY URGEnT signs.\nsigns include cranial nerve palsies and acutely paralysed limbs - their presence may indicate a form\nof vascular stroke and management may be time dependent. The child may have a weakness on\n4.2 VERY URGEnT sIGns\nVERY URGEnT one side of the body and may not be able to move as normal. The parent may also complain that\nIf the child does not have any of the emergency their child has a new onset of a squint or a paralysed face.\nABC-c-c-DO signs, the triage provider proceeds to assess the child Tiny baby - younger than\non the very urgent signs. This should not take more than a few 2 months Severe Trauma – severe mechanism of injury ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nseconds. Some of these signs will have been noticed during the Inconsolable crying / Usually this is an obvious case, but one needs to think of moto r Bleeding  apply pressure\nABCD triage discussed so far, and others need to be re-checked. severe pain vehicle accident injuries, falls from a height greater than to the site of trauma and\nFollow the list of very urgent signs to quickly complete this section to Presenting complaint - one metre in height, gunshot wounds and other traumatic cover open wounds\nd ne e e c d id e p r w om he p t t h m er a t n h a e g c e h m ild e h n a t. s any very urgent or urgent signs that More sleepy than normal mechanism"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0045", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "protocol_lookup", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
