baby, you do not need to ask this question. Tiny baby \nthe skin goes back very"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0040", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0041_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be urgently referred due to signs of severe dehydration or other emergency signs?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "rs of the skin and the fat tissue underneath. Pinch for one second and then release. See whethe r obviously not a young baby, you do not need to ask this question. Tiny baby \nthe skin goes back very slowly (2 seconds or more). Refer to shCP\nSmall babies are more difficult to assess properly, more prone to\nSevere dehydration is present if the child has a history of diarrhoea plus any two of the following sign s: getting infections (from other patients), and more likely to deteriorate\nlethargy, sunken eyes or very slow skin pinch (2 seconds or more ). quickly if unwell. All tiny babies of under two months of age should therefore be seen very urgently.\nO\n4.1.5 OThER EMERGEnCY sIGns Perform a finger prick glucotest in Inconsolably crying/ severe pain\nFacial inhalational burns the following cases: The inconsolable crying child is conscious but cries ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n• Reduced level of consciousness constantly and will not settle. Ask the parent of caregiver if Pain  check with shCP\nAny child in whom the airway has potentially been exposed • Unable to sit or move as usual the child is upset/frightened by the unfamiliar environment for analgesia initiation\nto heat (e.g.trapped in a house fire, hot water burn to face • Current or recent seizure or whether this is the presenting complaint. If a child has\nwith possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face • Known with diabetes severe pain and is in agony, s/he should be prioritized to\nor mouth) may get rapid swelling of the airwa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0041", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0042_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with a non-blanching rash be urgently referred for further assessment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "t. If a child has\nwith possible steam inhalation or chemical burn to face • Known with diabetes severe pain and is in agony, s/he should be prioritized to\nor mouth) may get rapid swelling of the airway. Children • Severe malnutrition receive very urgent assessment and pain relief. Severe pain may be due to severe conditions such as\npresenting with this emergency sign should be triaged Red. acute abdomen, meningitis, etc. You are not required to do a formal pain scale assessment, but for\nyour information there are some examples of pain scales appended at the end of part four.\nGlucose <3mmol/L at any time\nChildren with a low blood sugar are often very sick and need to be seen immediately. They should Presenting\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn WARnInG\nbe triaged Red if found to have a blood glucose concentration of less than 3 mmol/L. complaint -\n‘more sleepy ! Reduced level of consciousness If glucose less than 3 mmol/L\nPurpuric Rash than normal’  do finger prick glucose test  take to resuscitation area\nChildren who present with a complaint of a rash - should be assessed to see if the spots blanch with When the\npressure from fingertip or glass test (i.e. turns white when pressure is applied). If the spots are non- mother complains that her child that is more sleepy than normal (NOT just in natural sleep), the child\nblanching (i.e. does not turn white when pressure is applied) - then there is a risk of meningococca l may be in the early stages of serious conditions such as meningitis"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0042", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0043_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with a rash be urgently referred for further assessment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in natural sleep), the child\nblanching (i.e. does not turn white when pressure is applied) - then there is a risk of meningococca l may be in the early stages of serious conditions such as meningitis, hypoglycaemia, septicaemia etc,\ndisease. Children presenting with this emergency sign should be triaged Red. If you are unsure about a and will need to be identified and managed very urgently. Even if the child appears alert at triage\nchild presenting with a rash ask the senior healthcare professional. still assign a Very Urgent clinical sign as we have to take seriously the mothers concern.\nYou have now learned how to recognize the obvious emergency signs in paediatric patients. The\nnext section will cover the very urgent and urgent signs respectively. It is important to note that if a Poisoning or Overdose\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\npatient has any emergency signs then a TEWS does NOT need to be calculated. There should be A child with a history of swallowing drugs or other dangerou s\nno delay in taking the patient to the resuscitation area. The first set of vitals may be taken in the substances needs to be assessed very urgently, as s/he can Poisoning/Overdose\nresuscitation area or in the ambulance on the way to the hospital. deteriorate rapidly and might need specific treatment depending  Refer to shCP\non the substance taken. The mother will tell you if she has brough t\n• If any EMERGENCY signs have been found the TEWS should NOT be calculated at triage, the the child because of poss"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0043", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0044_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be referred to the resuscitation area or a senior healthcare professional due to emergency signs?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": " Refer to shCP\non the substance taken. The mother will tell you if she has brough t\n• If any EMERGENCY signs have been found the TEWS should NOT be calculated at triage, the the child because of possible intoxication. Consult SHCP for advice regarding very urgent management.\nchild is within the RED category and should be taken to the resuscitation area.\n• Children within the RED category need emergency care and should be seen immediately. Focal neurology acute\n• Always ensure that the child with emergency signs is handed over directly to a SHCP. A child who is fully conscious but has new focal neurological signs will need very urgent assessment.\nIn this case, the child does not require emergency treatment because they do not have any ABCD"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0044", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0045_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with focal neurological signs be urgently referred for imaging and possible intervention?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "emergency signs, but may need urgent imaging and intervention. Examples of focal neurological\n• I f there are no EMERGEnCY signs, check to see whether the child has any VERY URGEnT signs.\nsigns include cranial nerve palsies and acutely paralysed limbs - their presence may indicate a form\nof vascular stroke and management may be time dependent. The child may have a weakness on\n4.2 VERY URGEnT sIGns\nVERY URGEnT one side of the body and may not be able to move as normal. The parent may also complain that\nIf the child does not have any of the emergency their child has a new onset of a squint or a paralysed face.\nABC-c-c-DO signs, the triage provider proceeds to assess the child Tiny baby - younger than\non the very urgent signs. This should not take more than a few 2 months Severe Trauma – severe mechanism of injury ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nseconds. Some of these signs will have been noticed during the Inconsolable crying / Usually this is an obvious case, but one needs to think of moto r Bleeding  apply pressure\nABCD triage discussed so far, and others need to be re-checked. severe pain vehicle accident injuries, falls from a height greater than to the site of trauma and\nFollow the list of very urgent signs to quickly complete this section to Presenting complaint - one metre in height, gunshot wounds and other traumatic cover open wounds\nd ne e e c d id e p r w om he p t t h m er a t n h a e g c e h m ild e h n a t. s any very urgent or urgent signs that More sleepy than normal mechanism"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0045", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
