{"id": "qa_sats_0055_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with malnutrition and pitting oedema of both feet be urgently referred for management according to WHO severe malnutrition guidelines?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "pper thighs, buttocks and with a simple splint e.g. a padded wire splint or triangular bandage that does not interfere with\nupper arms. If you are concerned that a child might be malnourished look rapidly at these body area s. the blood supply or nerve bundle, and provide analgesia according your site’s guidelines.\nSeverely malnourished children are prone to hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to\nbe managed urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition guidelines to prevent complications .\nDislocation of finger or toe ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nMalnutrition with pitting oedema of both feet ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn This often causes an obvious deformity; the dislocated digit Pain  check with shCP\nThis is another form of severe malnutrition. It is ! Malnutrition with visible severe will need to be reduced under some form of anaesthesia. for analgesia initiation\nas dangerous as the one described above and wasting  do a finger prick glucose\nis recognised by the pitting oedema of both test and check with shCP\nfeet; they may also present with general body\nswelling. If you are concerned that a child might\nbe malnourished look rapidly at the child’s feet and check for WARnInG\noedema of both feet. These children often appear listless and If glucose is less than\napathetic and have skin and hair changes. They are also prone 3 mmol/L  move\nto hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to be to resuscitation\nmanaged urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition\nguid"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0055", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0056_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the urgent management indications for children with severe malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "e skin and hair changes. They are also prone 3 mmol/L  move\nto hypothermia, hypoglycaemia and infections and need to be to resuscitation\nmanaged urgently according to the WHO severe malnutrition\nguidelines to prevent complications.\n22 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 23\nPaediatric Clinical Signs Triage Early Warning Score TEWS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0056", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0057_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be triaged as urgent based on the TEWS?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "5 TRIAGE EARLY WARnInG sCORE TEWs\n• If any URGENT signs have been found the TEWS should be calculated and additional\ninvestigations checked to ensure that the child does not need to be assigned to the\nRED or ORAnGE category and taken for emergency or very urgent care. Learning Objectives:\n• Children within the Yellow category should be seen within 60 minutes.\n• Be familiar with the different age appropriate versions of the TEWS\n• Always check for additional tasks that should be done.\n• If there are no URGENT signs, calculate the TEWS and check for additional investigations to • Understand how to calculate the TEWS in paediatric and adult patients\ndetermine what the child’s final triage colour is.\nThe TEWS is a composite score representing physiologic parameters at triage. There are different age\nappropriate versions: the younger child TEWs is for patients smaller than 95cm or younger than 3 year s\nAssessment Questions\n(see Figure 12); the older child TEWs is for patients 96cm to 150cm or 3 years to around 12 years (see Figur e\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\n13); and the adult TEWs is for patients older than 12 years or taller than 150cm. Older children, wher e\n1. Grunting and nasal flaring are signs of severe respiratory distress. you are unsure which form to use, should be measured. If they are over 150cm then the adult versio n\nshould be used. This standardised scoring system has been validated and many of the boxes in the TEW S\nTrue Fa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0057", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0058_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be immediately taken to the majors area for urgent management according to the guideline?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ure which form to use, should be measured. If they are over 150cm then the adult versio n\nshould be used. This standardised scoring system has been validated and many of the boxes in the TEW S\nTrue False\ncalculwr are shaded grey. These boxes cannot be assigned a score. This means that for temperature, fo r\nexample, it is only possible to score 0 or 2 points, depending on the value of the recording.\n2. Oxygen saturation levels should always be checked in a child that looks blue.\nTrue False YOUnGER YOUnGER ChILD TEWs\nThAn 3 YEARs\n/ sMALLER\n3. If no emergency sign is identified in step one, but an urgent sign is identified in step two, the ThAn 95 cm 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\npatient is immediately taken to the majors area for urgent management.\nUnable to\nTrue False Mobility Normal move as\nfor age\nnormal\nChoose the correct answer:\nless than 50 or\n4. In the ABC-c-c-DO approach ABC stands for airway, breathing, circulation. RR 20 20 - 25 26 - 39 40 - 49 more\nWhat does c-c-D stand for?\nless than 160 or\n(a) convulsions, chest pain, dehydration hR 70 70 - 79 80 - 130 131 - 159 more\n(b) coma, cancer, disabilities\nFeels Feels Hot\n(c) coma, convulsions, dehydration\nTemp Cold 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\n(d) coma, craniopharyngioma, dehydration Under 35˚ 38.4˚\n(e) chronic pain, constipation, dehydration\nReacts to Reacts to Unres-\nAVPU Alert\n5. The following are emergency signs Voice Pain ponsive\n(a) oxygen saturation levels more than 92% Trauma No Yes\n(b) facial or inhalation burn Figure 12: Younger Child TEWS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0058", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0059_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be escalated to emergency care based on the emergency signs listed?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "eacts to Unres-\nAVPU Alert\n5. The following are emergency signs Voice Pain ponsive\n(a) oxygen saturation levels more than 92% Trauma No Yes\n(b) facial or inhalation burn Figure 12: Younger Child TEWS (younger than 3 years)\n(c) stridor, snoring and secretions\n(d) closed fracture\n3 to 12 OLDER ChILD TEWs\n(e) b and c above YEARs OLD /\n95 to 150 cm\ntall 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\nUnable to\nNormal\nMobility walk as\nfor age\nnormal\nless than 27 or\nRR 15 -16 17 - 21 22 - 26\n15 more\nless than 130 or\nhR 60 - 79 80 - 99 100 - 129\n60 more\nFeels Feels Hot\nTemp Cold 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ 38.4˚\nReacts to Reacts to Unres-\nAVPU Confused Alert\nVoice Pain ponsive\nTrauma No Yes\nFigure 13: Older Child TEWS (age 3 - 12 years)\n24 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 25"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0059", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0060_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be escalated to higher care based on mobility and trauma assessment in the TEWS?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage Early Warning Score TEWS Triage Early Warning Score TEWS\nMOBILITY\nOLDER ThAn ADULT TEWs\n12 YEARs / Mobility is observed by noting the mode in which the patient has to be mobilised. A small baby is\nTALLER ThAn carried by the carer – as might a small infant or child. You need to assess if the level of movement\n150 cm tall) 3 2 1 0 1 2 3\nis normal for that particular child. In the event that the child or adult is in a wheelchair due to\nStretcher/ permanent paralysis 2 points are given for mobility as the child in a wheelchair is more at risk.\nMobility Walking With Help\nImmobile\nTRAUMA\nless than more\nRR 9 - 14 15 - 20 21 - 29 Trauma is present if there is ANY injury to the patient within the past 48 hours.\n9 than 29\nless than more 5.2 Terminology and key concepts\nhR 41 - 50 51 - 100 101 - 110 111 - 129\n41 than 129 1. Confusion: An older child may be reported to have confused behaviour or this may be\nLess than more discovered at triage. A confused child appears disorientated, s/he may not be interacting\nsBP 71 71 - 80 81-100 101 -199 than 199 normally with the carer or the environment, s/he may be delirious or hallucinating. A confused\nchild may be talking a lot and aggressive or s/he may be quiet and fearful looking. This sign is\nHot OR\nCold OR difficult to detect in younger children who are usually pre-verbal - so it is only included on the\nTemp 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ TEWS for the older child.\n38.4˚\n2. Acute vs Chronic: Many patients in South Africa are chronically un"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0060", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0061_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a senior doctor or sister be involved in reviewing a child's triage code?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "hildren who are usually pre-verbal - so it is only included on the\nTemp 35˚ - 38.4˚ Over\nUnder 35˚ TEWS for the older child.\n38.4˚\n2. Acute vs Chronic: Many patients in South Africa are chronically unwell with serious conditions\nAVPU Confused Alert Reacts to Reacts to Unres- such as TB or HIV/AIDS. In these patients, daily resting physiology such as respiratory rate may\nVoice Pain ponsive be abnormal. This is important to bear in mind, as their TEWS will be high and they will be given\nTrauma No Yes an overly high triage code (they may be overtriaged). It is not for the inexperienced triage\nprovider to decide whether this is the case or not, as sick patients may be missed. However, it is\nFigure 14: Adult TEWS (older than 12 years) appropriate to ask a senior doctor or sister to review the patient’s triage code.\n5.1 Observations at triage 3. The younger and older child: Younger and older children have different resting vital signs to\nadults. These are often difficult to obtain, and the patients often cry, which pushes up their\nTo calculate the TEWS the first step is to perform the DO nOT UPsET ThE ChILD respiratory and heart rate. The values still need to be recorded as you measure them, so that\nobservations required by the TEWS. this affects your observations for TEWS a correct score can be given in the TEWS, but tell the senior doctor or sister that the child was\ncrying when they were recorded. These staff members can then decide whether the child has\nREsPIRATORY RATE\nbeen ov"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0061", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0062_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child’s respiratory rate be reassessed if they were crying during the initial measurement?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ore can be given in the TEWS, but tell the senior doctor or sister that the child was\ncrying when they were recorded. These staff members can then decide whether the child has\nREsPIRATORY RATE\nbeen overtriaged or not.\nThe patient’s respiratory rate is calculated by counting the breaths for 30 seconds and then multiplying\nby two. This should be done at the first opportunity when the child is quiet. If the child is small it is bes t Assessment Questions\nto leave him/her in the mother’s lap. Do not undress the child to count the respiratory rate as doing this Clearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\nmay upset him/her. Count the breathing rate by observing rather than touching the child. 1. If a child is nine years old we use the older child TEWS.\nhEART RATE True False\nTo measure the heart rate, the triage provider can manually count the heart rate by feeling the pulse\n2. Always undress a child and weigh them so that it is easier to obtain their vital signs.\nfor 30 seconds and then multiplying by two. Alternatively a heart rate monitor can be used (ideally\nnot from a saturation monitor – as the heart rate on these monitors can be unrelaible and can True False\nchange frequently if the child moves).\n3. To accurately obtain a respiratory rate always start when the patient is at rest, count\nTEMPERATURE respirations for 30 seconds and multiply by two.\nThe temperature is measured using either an electronic or mercury thermometer (preferably a low-\nTrue False"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0062", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0063_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be escalated to higher care based on their level of consciousness?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "n the patient is at rest, count\nTEMPERATURE respirations for 30 seconds and multiply by two.\nThe temperature is measured using either an electronic or mercury thermometer (preferably a low-\nTrue False\nreading thermometer). Rectal temperature should NOT be taken routinely.\nAVPU Choose the correct answer:\nAVPU is done by seeing if the child is alert. If not obviously alert then the triage provider needs to 4. The adult TEWS consists of the following parameters:\nobserve the child’s response to their (or the parent’s) voice. If the child responds to voice and is then (a) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\nalert – he is an ‘A’ – but if he remains drowsy\n(b) Mobility, capillary refill time, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\nthen he is a ‘V’’. If he makes no response to V means the patient is not fully alert and\n(c) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, systolic blood pressure, AVPU and trauma\nvoice then the triage provider need to assess REsPOnDs OnLY TO YOUR VOICE. Another way\nthe response to a painful stimulus (e.g. nailbed of describing this = ‘lethargic’ (d) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, AVPU and trauma\npressure or sternal rub) – if the child responds to (e) Mobility, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation level and blood glucose concentration\nP means the patient is not alert\nthis s/he is a ‘P’. If there is no response to either\nand does not respond to your voice but\nverbal or pain stimuli the patie"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0063", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0064_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should additional investigations be performed in a child with hyperglycaemia or malnutrition?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ion level and blood glucose concentration\nP means the patient is not alert\nthis s/he is a ‘P’. If there is no response to either\nand does not respond to your voice but\nverbal or pain stimuli the patient is labelled as 5. The older child TEWS consists of the following parameters:\nREsPOnDs OnLY TO A PAInFUL sTIMULUs\nunresponsive. (a) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\n(b) Mobility, capillary refill time, heart rate, temperature and blood pressure\n(c) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature and trauma\n(d) Mobility, respiratory rate, heart rate, temperature, AVPU and trauma\n(e) Mobility, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation level and blood glucose concentration\n26 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 27\nAdditional Investigations Additional Investigations\nDIABETEs AnD hYPERGLYCAEMIA\n6 ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOns ADDITIOnAL\n(GLUCOTEST 11 OR MORE)\n! InVEsTIGATIOn\nAll patients with a glucose concentration of 11 mmol/L or Perform a urine dipstick to\nLearning Objectives: more require a urine dipstick to check for ketones. check for ketones\n• Be familiar with the additional investigations and when to perform them ChILD hAs MALnUTRITIOn WITh sEVERE VIsIBLE\nWAsTInG or WITh PITTInG OEDEMA OF BOTh FEET\n• Know how additional investigations may change the triage priority level\nThis child is at risk of hypoglycaemia, as well as hypothermia\nWARnInG\nFor all patients (especially children) immediate additional investigations may be indica"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0064", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0065_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child be escalated to resuscitation and a finger prick glucotest be performed?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ons may change the triage priority level\nThis child is at risk of hypoglycaemia, as well as hypothermia\nWARnInG\nFor all patients (especially children) immediate additional investigations may be indicated to identify ADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn If glucose is below 3 mmol/L\n!\npotentially serious complications of their presenting conditions. Check if the patient has any of the Perform a finger prick glucotest  move to resuscitation and\nconditions listed below and act accordingly. immediately to exclude hypoglycaemia hand over to SHCP\nRESPIRATORY RATE (RR) SCORES MORE THAN\n1 POInT On TEWs:\nWARnInG ABDOMInAL PAIn OR BACKAChE In FEMALEs\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn For children if oxygen saturation PERFORM A FInGER PRICK The adult female with abdominal pain may have an ectopic\n! Measure oxygen saturation (for is below 92% on room air  GLUCOTEsT In ThE pregnancy leading to severe pain and discomfort as time\nchildren on finger, toe, hand or Move to resuscitation area and FOLLOWInG CAsEs: progresses. She may be at risk for a ruptured ectopic or othe r\nearlobe, depending on available administer nasal prong or • Reduced level of complications in pregnancy requiring emergency surgery.\nsaturation probe and co-operation) facemask oxygen consciousness These additional investigations may rapidly identify\n• Unable to sit or move as usual very ill patients and change their category. They also\nA raised RR for age may indicate serious underlying pathology such as chest infection requiring prevent and"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0065", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0066_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a patient be escalated to a senior healthcare professional due to altered level of consciousness?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "tify\n• Unable to sit or move as usual very ill patients and change their category. They also\nA raised RR for age may indicate serious underlying pathology such as chest infection requiring prevent and/or\nsupplemental oxygen • Current or recent seizure identify serious ADDITIOnAL\n• Known with diabetes complications. ! InVEsTIGATIOn\nREDUCED LEVEL OF COnsCIOUsnEss ADDITIOnAL Perform a urine dipstick\n• Severe malnutrition\nAll patients that are not fully alert (i.e. confused or only ! InVEsTIGATIOn and urine pregnancy test\nresponding to verbal or painful stimulus) need to have a Perform a finger prick\nfinger prick glucotest done and should be handed over glucotest immediately to Assessment Questions\nto the senior healthcare professional. exclude hypoglycaemia\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false:\nIf the child is not alert, or the caregiver volunteers that\nthe child is more sleepy than normal, this may indicate WARnInG 1. A patient with a glucose of 11 mmol/L needs to have a urine dipstick done to check for\na serious evolving medical condition such as meningitis If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  ketones in the urine.\nor if associated with a history of trauma there could be move to resuscitation and True False\na traumatic brain injury. hand over to SHCP\nAny sick child who has not been feeding well or 2. Check the finger prick haemoglobin on all patients that have a history of diabetes.\nhas been vomiting may become drowsy because of a low blood sugar level"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0066", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0067_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a finger prick glucotest be performed to exclude hypoglycaemia in children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "ick child who has not been feeding well or 2. Check the finger prick haemoglobin on all patients that have a history of diabetes.\nhas been vomiting may become drowsy because of a low blood sugar level. Tiny babies and\nTrue False\nmalnourished children are particularly at risk of hypoglycaemia.\n3. Do a urine dipstick and urine pregnancy test on all adult females presenting with abdominal\nUnABLE TO sIT OR MOVE As nORMAL\nADDITIOnAL pain.\nIf the patient is unable to walk or move as normal or the ! InVEsTIGATIOn\ncaregiver reports that the child is lethargic or unable to Perform a finger prick True False\nmove as usual, this may also be a sign of serious illness or glucotest immediately to\nof a low blood sugar level. exclude hypoglycaemia Choose the correct answer:\nRECEnT sEIZURE/FIT 4. Perform a finger prick glucotest on the following cases:\nThe patient who is actively fitting will have been taken WARnInG (a) Current or recent seizure\nto the resuscitation area. In any child with a history of If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  (b) Facial burn\nrecent seizure there could be easily identifiable and move to resuscitation and (c) Reduced level of consciousness\ntreatable causes, including hypoglycaemia, pyrexia hand over to SHCP\n(d) History of diabetes\n(febrile seizure) or high blood pressure.\n(e) a, c and d\nhIsTORY OF DIABETEs\nAll patients with diabetes are at risk of either becoming hypoglyacaemic (usually drowsy or confused) o r 5. The following two additional investigations may upgrade a p"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0067", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0068_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with vomiting without diarrhoea be referred to SHCP for assessment?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "a, c and d\nhIsTORY OF DIABETEs\nAll patients with diabetes are at risk of either becoming hypoglyacaemic (usually drowsy or confused) o r 5. The following two additional investigations may upgrade a patient to the Red category:\nhyperglycaemic with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). All therefore need a glucotest done at presentation. (a) Oxygen saturation levels in children and finger prick haemoglobin\n(b) Finger prick haemoglobin levels and urine dipstick\nADDITIOnAL InVEsTIGATIOn WARnInG\n! Perform a finger prick glucotest If glucose is below 3 mmol/L  (c) Urine dipstick test and oxygen saturation levels\nimmediately to exclude move to resuscitation and (d) Finger prick glucotest and oxygen saturation levels\nhypoglycaemia hand over to SHCP (e) Finger prick glucotest and finger prick haemoglobin\nIf glucose is hi  hand over to SHCP\n28 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 29\nAdditional Tasks Additional Tasks\nVOMITInG WIThOUT DIARRhOEA AnD ThE ChILD Is DEhYDRATED\n7 ADDITIOnAL TAsKs ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nVomiting alone which is severe enough to result in dehydration\nVomiting without\nmay indicate a diagnosis other than simple gastroenteritis e.g. diarrhoea  refer to\nLearning Objectives: For all patients (especially children) whether triaged urinary tract infection; a surgical problem with bowel obstruction,; shCP for assessment\ndiabetic ketoacidosis or even meningitis or another severe\nRED, ORAnGE, YELLOW OR GREEn immediate\n• Be familiar with the additional tasks additional ta"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0068", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0069_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should senior advice be sought in a child with signs of severe infection or potential complications?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "lem with bowel obstruction,; shCP for assessment\ndiabetic ketoacidosis or even meningitis or another severe\nRED, ORAnGE, YELLOW OR GREEn immediate\n• Be familiar with the additional tasks additional tasks may be indicated to stabilise the infection. It may not be appropriate to automatically commence\nthis child on a trial of oral rehydration – and so senior advice should be sought.\n• Know when to perform additional tasks patient and/or identify or prevent potentially serious"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0069", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0070_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a small baby under 2 months with a suspected fracture be referred for urgent medical evaluation?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "complications. Check if the child has any of the IF ThE ChILD hAs A\nconditions listed below and act accordingly. CLOsED FRACTURE ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nTInY BABY UnDER 2 MOnThs These children will be P C a lo in s e  d c fr h a e c c tu k r e w  ith i m SH m C o P b f i o li r s e in a iti f a fe ti c o t n e d o f l im an b a w lg i e th s i s a im a p n l d e review\nSmall babies under 2 months are more difficult to assess, their ADDITIOnAL TAsK experiencing significant padded splint or a triangular bandage sling and get a"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0070", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0071_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a toddler who has ingested a poison be referred to specialist care?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "symptoms are often non-specific, they have lower immunity than Tiny baby under pain - both medication and wheelchair or stretcher if required\nother children so are more prone to infections, and they deteriorate 2 months  immobilization of the affected\nmore quickly. They therefore need to be assessed as a priority. refer to shCP limb will provide some relief from the pain and should be initiated prior to formal assessment\nPOIsOnInG OR OVERDOsE ADDITIOnAL TAsK ChEsT PAIn\nToddlers are inquisitive and like to explore their environments. ADDITIOnAL TAsK Chest Pain  perform an Patients with chest pain may be having an acute\nThey may accidentally ingest a variety of household substances, Poisoning /overdose  ECG to rule out potential myocardial infarct (AMI). An immediate ECG is required to\npesticides or medications. A child who has ingested a poison refer to shCP AMI and hand over to SHCP rule out a potential AMI.\ncan deteriorate quickly. They may require a specific antidote\nand if a poison or medication has been ingested recently ACTIVE OnGOInG BLEEDInG ADDITIOnAL TAsK\ni c m h m ar e c d o i a a l t ) e . It in i t s e t r h v e e r n e t f i o o r n e m im a p y o b rt e a n n t e t e o d c e o d n ( s e u . l g t . a a S c H t C iva P t f e o d r children ADDITIOnAL TAsK Children have a small circulating blood A to c t t h iv e e s b ite le e o d f t r  au a m p a p l w y i d th ir e a c d t r p y r e d s re su ss re in g\nand adults even if the patient appears stable. Pain o"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0071", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0072_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should you refer a child or adult with pain or inconsolable crying to a senior healthcare professional?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "have a small circulating blood A to c t t h iv e e s b ite le e o d f t r  au a m p a p l w y i d th ir e a c d t r p y r e d s re su ss re in g\nand adults even if the patient appears stable. Pain or inconsolable volume, and are at risk of becoming and perform finger prick haemoglobin to\ncrying  check with shocked quickly if there is ongoing bleeding obtain a baseline and hand over to SHCP\nIF ChILD APPEARs TO BE In PAIn or Is InCOnsOLABLY CRYInG shCP for initiation of from a trauma site\nThis may be due to a medical cause like an ear infection or analgesia and review hIsTORY OF RECEnT BLEEDInG - EIThER ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nfrom severe headache due to meningitis. Or there may be an RECTAL, ORAL OR FROM A sITE OF TRAUMA history of bleeding  check finger prick\nobvious cause such as a fracture or laceration. Pain is obviously unpleasant for both the child and This patient may be anaemic from blood loss haemoglobin.\ncarer. It is good practice to initiate analgesia as soon as possible for children and adults. If less than 8 g/dl then hand over to SHCP\nBURn PREsEnTInG COMPLAInT Is ABDOMInAL PAIn\nThe patient will be experiencing pain and especially ADDITIOnAL TAsK Abdominal pain is a common complaint in ADDITIOnAL TAsK\nchildren have the potential to deteriorate rapidly Pain  check with shCP for children and it has a wide variety of possible causes including For a younger child\nfrom significant fluid losses and may develop initiation of analgesia and review urinary tract infection, dia"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0072", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0073_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a senior healthcare professional be alerted for a child with a burn or other illness?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "CP for children and it has a wide variety of possible causes including For a younger child\nfrom significant fluid losses and may develop initiation of analgesia and review urinary tract infection, diabetic ketoacidosis, hepatitis or other  Place a urine bag,\nhypothermia. causes. A urine dipstick for children and adults will assist in if olderchild, give\nexcluding or diagnosing these. container\nIf the burn is recent (<3hrs) immediate intervention may limit the extent of tissue damage.\nThese additional tasks may assist to rapidly identify very ill patients and\nADDITIOnAL TAsK prevent and identify serious complications. They also improve quality of care by providing relief from pain.\nIf the burn occurred recently (within 3hrs)  it is still worthwhile to cool the burnt area with\nwater, for example, by running cool tap water over the burnt area for 30 minutes. The child A senior healthcare professional should be alerted in the following cases:\nshould then be dried and wrapped in a clean sheet or blanket to avoid hypothermia . • Tiny baby younger than 2 months old • Very high or very low temperatures\nThe burn can be covered in cling wrap if available, or a clean dry sheet or towel will also be suitable. • Reduced level of consciousness • Vomiting only with dehydration\n• A child in pain including fractures and burns • The severely malnourished child\nTEMPERATURE 38.5˚C OR MORE • Poison ingestion or overdose • A child with active bleeding\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nA very high temperature will"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0073", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0074_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with a temperature of 38.5°C or more be given paracetamol and referred to a senior healthcare professional?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in including fractures and burns • The severely malnourished child\nTEMPERATURE 38.5˚C OR MORE • Poison ingestion or overdose • A child with active bleeding\nADDITIOnAL TAsK\nA very high temperature will result in physiological\nhigh temperature  remove excessive\nchanges that may affect the TEWS, and make the Additional Tasks at triage\nclothing and check with shCP for\npatient feel uncomfortable and in some children initiation of analgesia and review ADULT PATIEnTs\nmay be associated with a febrile seizure.\nPROBLEM IMMEDIATE TAsKs\nTEMPERATURE 35.5˚C OR LESS ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n1. Temperature 38.5° or more Paracetamol 1 g orally stat (document in the notes)\nHypothermia might be a sign of severe sepsis with Low temperature  warm\nvery small babies, ex-premature babies and severely the patient with additional 2. Temperature 35° or less Warm the patient with blankets if available\nmalnourished children being the most at risk. blankets for children with a 3. Diabetes and hyperglycaemia Urine dipstick to check for ketones\ncap if available and hand over to SHCP (glucotest 11 mmol/L or more)\nDIARRhOEA & VOMITInG\n4. History of bleeding Finger prick haemoglobin\nEven if the child has no or some dehydration, the child ADDITIOnAL TAsK\n5. Bleeding PR, PO or from the site of trauma Finger prick haemoglobin\nis at risk of becoming dehydrated whilst waiting to be Diarrhoea & vomiting  take child\nseen and should receive oral rehydration to treat and/o r to ORT corner and advise caregiver 6. Abdominal pa"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0074", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0075_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with diarrhoea and vomiting be referred for oral rehydration therapy?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "in\nis at risk of becoming dehydrated whilst waiting to be Diarrhoea & vomiting  take child\nseen and should receive oral rehydration to treat and/o r to ORT corner and advise caregiver 6. Abdominal pain or backache in males Urine dipsticks\nprevent further dehydration to start ORT by cup and spoon 7. PV bleeding Urine dipsticks, Urine preganancy test\nFinger prick haemoglobin\n30 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 31\nAdditional Tasks Triage in Context\nPAEDIATRIC PATIEnTs 8 TRIAGE In COnTEXT\nPROBLEM IMMEDIATE TAsKs\n1. Poisoning OR overdose Refer to Senior Healthcare Professional (SHCP) 8.1 Pre-hospital\n2. Child in pain OR inconsolable crying Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation The use of triage within a pre-hospital setting is generally well accepted and understood universally .\n3. Child with a burn Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation Whilst this need is quite obvious (even critical) during major incidents, it’s the daily application of triage\nIf burn occurred within 3 hours, cool the burnt area principles in the despatch of ambulance resources where it has the most benefit. In these scenarios ,\nCover burn in clingwrap or clean dry sheet"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0075", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "signs_symptoms", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0076_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a patient be referred to a senior healthcare provider based on temperature or dehydration?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "triage permits the EMS despatcher to apply rules based decision making to what is an otherwise\n4. Temperature 38.5 ° or more Remove excessive clothing & discuss with SHCP impossible choice.\n5. Temperature 35 ° or less Warm the child with blankets if available. Refer to SHCP\nThe pre-hospital use of triage in the field varies from region to region, but is generally categorised into fou r\n6. Diarrhoea Take to ORT corner and advise caregiver to give ORT by cup priorities (represented by the colours red, yellow, green and blue). Such triage typically uses instability o f\nand spoon vital signs to differentiate high from low priority patients. Discrepancies in triage appear when personne l\n7. Vomiting without diarrhoea but with dehydration Refer to SHCP for assessment of differing levels of medical experience and qualifications need to assess patients as there are no clea r\ndefinitions of ‘unstable’ physiology. The terms ‘stable’ and ‘unstable’ are poorly understood and fail to\n8. Presenting complaint - abdominal pain younger child - urine bag / older child - urine container\naccurately reflect the patient’s clinical condition.\n9. Closed fracture Check with SHCP for analgesia initiation\nAccurate pre-hospital triage is essential for appropriate call out of secondary resources;, accurate\nImmobilize affected limb with a simple padded splint or\ntriagular bandage notification of receiving hospitals, and quality assessment and audit of the ambulance service. This i s\nparticularly pertinent in aeromedical callout requests and use of this specialised resource. For these\n10. Active ongoing bleeding Apply pressure to the site of trauma\nreasons triage tools based on objective physiological discriminators are essential.\nPerform finger prick haemoglobin to obtain a baseline\nRefer to SHCP\n8.2 Patient streaming\n11. History of recent bleeding Perform finger prick haemoglobin"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0076", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0077_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a patient be referred to the SHCP based on triage acuity level?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage assigns the patient to an acuity level, which then dictates the amount of time the patient can wa it\nIf less than 8 g/dl then refer to SHCP\nsafely before being seen: Red immediate, Orange within 10 minutes, Yellow within an hour and Green within\nfour hours. It is the recommended practice to “stream” these patient categories to different areas and /\nAssessment Questions or healthcare providers within the facility. The normal streaming pattern would be Reds to resus, Orange s\nClearly indicate whether the following statements are true or false: and Yellows to Majors and Greens to Minors, which would be manned by staff dedicated to these area s.\nFor the most part, streaming patients according to acuity will also stream them according to resourc e\n1. A tiny baby under two months should always be referred to the SHCP once they have been use: full monitoring, access to high-powered drugs and interventions with full team response is necessa ry\ncomprehensively triaged. to effectively treat the Red patient, while a room with a chair and a single practitioner may be all th at\nTrue False is necessary to see and treat the patients in the Green stream. How and where patients are streame d\ndepends on the load, manning and infrastructure of the Emergency Centre or Health Facility.\n2. A patient with an active ongoing bleed should place the bleeding area under running water. It is important in any system that prioritises order-to-be-seen by anything other than “first-come-\nfirst-served” to have a plan to see the lower priority patients. Streaming is a possible mechanism to\nTrue False\nachieve this. Without streaming, the Green patient will keep being pushed to the back of the queue\n3. A child that is vomiting only with no diarrhoea should first be referred to the SHCP for further by the patient of higher acuity, who by necessity should be seen first. With streaming, the higher\nassessment before commencing the child on a trial oral rehydration. acuity patients are seen before they die while the lower acuity patients are seen in another area\nbefore they leave!\nTrue False\n8.3 Infrastructure"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0077", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0078_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with a burn injury from boiling oil be given further medical evaluation?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage is a process, not a place, but for the most part will need an area for triage of those patients not\nChoose the correct answer:\nsent directly through to resus or majors. This area should allow for privacy and be set up in order to\n4. In a child with a burn injury from boiling oil that occurred 20 minutes ago: perform the vital signs for the TEWS, additional investigations and tasks.\n(a) The burnt area may be covered in cling wrap if available The room should preferably allow for one-way flow of patients from the waiting room into a\n(b) Cool running tap water over the burnt area for 30 minutes may subwaiting area in the area to which they have been streamed. If not possible, some sort of\nlimit the extent of tissue damage demarcation of the area or patients should be made in order to separate those already triaged from\n(c) The burnt area should be wrapped in a clean sheet to prevent hypothermia those who have not been assessed by the triage officer.\n(d) a and b\n8.4 Alignment of staff to temporal flow of patients\n(e) a,b and c\nIt is important that triage, the tool to ensure that patients are assessed timeously, does not become\nthe bottleneck in the system. Importantly, if the doctor is ready for the next patient, but cannot see\n5. The senior healthcare professional should be consulted in the following cases:\nthem as they are “first getting triaged”, the object is being defeated!\n(a) Poison ingestion and overdose\nMore often the bottleneck occurs due to failure to align staff with the flow of patients. For the most\n(b) A severely malnourished child\npart, it is possible to predict the times of day when the flow of patients into the EC is heavier (typical\n(c) Vomiting only with dehydration “saddle-shaped” curve). Staffing for triage needs to reflect this flow.\n(d) a and c\nThere should also be some sort of plan in place to deal with unexpected influx of patients: each\n(e) a, b and c facility should have an upper limit of patients that they are willing to have waiting for triage, over\nwhich a contingency plan needs to be activated: eg a regional hospital has an agreement that\nif there are five or more patients needing triage, a nurse is called from majors to help triage the\npatients until the level is back down to less than five waiting.\n32 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012 33\nSummary and References\n9 sUMMARY"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0078", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0079_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should patients triaged as Green be reviewed by a clinical nurse practitioner or doctor according to the SATS guidelines?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Triage is an essential first step in efficient and effective emergency care –whether on the roadside\nor in the public or private hospital arena. A robust triage tool will help to save lives and reduce\nmorbidity. The South African Triage Scale has been derived by a panel of experts in Emergency\nMedicine (doctors, nurses and paramedics), and is scientifically proven. It has been shown to improve\nwaiting times and make the emergency centre run more smoothly. However, attention needs to be\npaid to those patients triaged Green, especially in peak times, and the SATG recommends the use of\nstreaming with a clinical nurse practitioner or doctor to see this group.\nThe SATS has been validated as part of a Masters in Philosophy (MPhil) with 700 public sector patients,\nan MPhil with 2000 private sector patients and a PhD. Feedback following publication in four major\njournals has contributed to the process.\nThis is Edition 3 and we accept that the tool may not be perfect, that is why your feedback is so\nimportant. In addition, there will be ongoing research aimed at keeping the tool accurate and\nappropriate. If necessary, subsequent editions will follow.\nOnline resources:\nFor further information please visit\nwww.emssa.org.za/sats\nContact details:\nMichele Twomey\nSATS Implementation Advisor\n082 850 3281\nmicheletwomey@gmail.com\n10 REFEREnCEs\n1. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC.Effect of introduction of nurse triage on waiting times in a South African emergency\ndepartment. Emerg Med J 2008;25:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0079", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0080_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the warning signs that indicate a patient should be escalated to a higher level of care according to the South African Triage Scale?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "850 3281\nmicheletwomey@gmail.com\n10 REFEREnCEs\n1. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC.Effect of introduction of nurse triage on waiting times in a South African emergency\ndepartment. Emerg Med J 2008;25:395-397.\n2. Twomey M, Wallis LA, Thompson ML, Myers JE. The South African Triage Scale (adult version) provides reliable acuity\nratings. Int Emerg Nurs. Published Online First: 19 September 2011.doi:10.1016/j.ienj.2011.08.002.\n3. Twomey M, Wallis LA, Thompson ML, Myers JE. The South African Triage Scale (adult version) provides valid acuity\nratings when used by doctors and enrolled nursing assistants. African J Emerg Med 2012;2:3-12.\n4. Bruijns SR, Wallis LA, Burch VC. A prospective evaluation of the Cape triage score in the emergency department of\nan urban public hospital in South Africa. Emerg Med J. 2008;25:398-402.\n5. Policy for implementation of the triage of patients in Western Cape emergency units. Circular H7 of 2006.\nDepartment of Health, Provincial Government, update 2012.\n6. Gottschalk S, Wood D, De Vries S, Wallis L, Bruijns S, On behalf of the Cape Triage Group . The cape triage score: a\nnew triage system South Africa. Proposal from the Cape triage group. Emerg Med J 2006;23:149-153.\n7. Augustyn J. The South African Triage Scale: a tool for emergency nurses. Prof Nurs Today 2011;15:24–29.\n8. Isaacs AA, Hellenberg D. Implementing a structured triage system at a community health centre (CHC) using\nKaizen. SA Fam Pract. 2009;51\n9. Goudge J, Cornell J, McIntyre D, et al."}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0080", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_sats_0081_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the warning signs that indicate a patient should be escalated to higher levels of care according to triage systems?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "day 2011;15:24–29.\n8. Isaacs AA, Hellenberg D. Implementing a structured triage system at a community health centre (CHC) using\nKaizen. SA Fam Pract. 2009;51\n9. Goudge J, Cornell J, McIntyre D, et al. Private sector financing. In: Ntuli A, Suleman F, Barron P, McCoy D: editors.\nSouth African Health Review 2001. Durban:Health Systems Trust; 2001.\n10. Bradshaw D, Groenewald P, Laubscher R, Nannan N, Nojilana B, Norman R, Pieterse D and Schneider M. Initial\nBurden of Disease Estimates for South Africa, 2000. Cape Town: South African Medical Research Council, 2003.\n11. Iserson KV, Moskop JC. Triage in Medicine, Part I: Concept, History, and Types. Ann Emerg Med. 2007;49:275-281.\n12. FitzGerald G, Jelinek GA, Scott D, Gerdtz MF. Emergency department Triage revisited. Emerg Med J 2010;27:86-92.\n13. Moll HA. Challenges in the validation of Triage systems at emergency departments. J Clin Epi 2010;63:384-88.\n14. Twomey M, de Sa A, Wallis LA, Myers JE. Inter-rater reliability of the South African Triage Scale: Assessing two\ndifferent cadres of health care workers in a real time environment. African J Emerg Med 2011;1:113-118.\n34 SATS TRAINING MANUAL 2012\nDepartment of Health\nPO Box 2060, Cape Town, 8000\nFor more information contact:\nMichele Twomey (SATS Implementation Advisor)\nMobile: +27 850 3281 email: micheletwomey@gmail.com\nDepartmental Website: www.health.gov.za\nProvincial Website: www.westerncape.gov.za\nThe South African"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "sats_0081", "source": "sats", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "triage", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0000_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with measles be referred to the hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "COMPLICATIONS**** If mouth ulcers, treat with gentian violet\nFollow-up in 3 days\nMeasles now or within the last 3 Green: Give Vitamin A treatment\nmonths. MEASLES\n(cid:13)(cid:3)(cid:55)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:91)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:53)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:91)(cid:76)(cid:80)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:19)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:131)(cid:38)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:74)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:17)\n**Look for local tenderness; oral sores; refusal to use a limb; hot tender swelling; red tender skin or boils; lower abdominal pain or pain on passing urine in older children.\n*** If no malaria test available: High malaria risk - classify as MALARIA; Low malaria risk AND NO obvious cause of fever - classify as MALARIA.\n**** Other important complications of measles - pneumonia, stridor, diarrhoea, ear infection, and acute malnu"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0000", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0001_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with an ear problem be urgently referred to hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "classify as MALARIA; Low malaria risk AND NO obvious cause of fever - classify as MALARIA.\n**** Other important complications of measles - pneumonia, stridor, diarrhoea, ear infection, and acute malnutrition - are classified in other tables.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:23)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nDoes the child have an ear problem?\nIf yes, ask: Look and feel: Tender swelling behind the Pink: Give first dose of an appropriate antibiotic\nIs there ear pain? Look for pus draining from ear. MASTOIDITIS Give first dose of paracetamol for pain\nIs there ear discharge? the ear. Classify EAR PROBLEM Refer URGENTLY to hospital\nIf yes, for how long? Feel for tender swelling\nPus is seen draining from Yellow: Give an antibiotic for 5 days\nbehind the ear.\nthe ear and discharge is ACUTE EAR Give paracetamol for pain\nreported for less than 14 INFECTION Dry the ear by wicking\ndays, or Follow-up in 5 days\nEar pain.\nPus is seen draining from Yellow: Dry the ear by wicking\nthe ear and discharge is CHRONIC EAR Treat with topical quinolone eardrops for 14 days\nreported for 14 days or INFECTION Follow-up in 5 days\nmore.\nNo ear pain and Green: No treatment\nNo pus seen draining from NO EAR INFECTION\nthe ear.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nCHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION Oedema of both feet Pink: Give first dose appropriate antibiotic\nLOOK AND FEEL: Classify"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0001", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0002_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with acute malnutrition be urgently referred to hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": ")(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION\nCHECK FOR ACUTE MALNUTRITION Oedema of both feet Pink: Give first dose appropriate antibiotic\nLOOK AND FEEL: Classify OR COMPLICATED Treat the child to prevent low blood\nLook for signs of acute malnutrition NUTRITIONAL WFH/L less than -3 z- SEVERE ACUTE sugar\nLook for oedema of both feet. STATUS scores OR MUAC less MALNUTRITION Keep the child warm\nDetermine WFH/L* ___ z-score. than 115 mm AND any Refer URGENTLY to hospital\nMeasure MUAC**____ mm in a child 6 months or older. one of the following:\nMedical\nIf WFH/L less than -3 z-scores or MUAC less than 115 complication present\nmm, then: or\nCheck for any medical complication present: Not able to finish RUTF\nAny general danger signs or\nAny severe classification Breastfeeding\nproblem.\nPneumonia with chest indrawing\nIf no medical complications present: WFH/L less than -3 z- Yellow: Give oral antibiotics for 5 days\nChild is 6 months or older, offer RUTF*** to scores UNCOMPLICATED Give ready-to-use therapeutic food for a child\neat. Is the child: OR SEVERE ACUTE aged 6 months or more\nMUAC less than 115 mm MALNUTRITION Counsel the mother on how to feed the child.\nNot able to finish RUTF portion? Assess for possible TB infection\nAND\nAble to finish RUTF portion? Advise mother when to return immediately\nAble to finish RUTF.\nFollow up in 7 days\nChild is less than 6 months, assess\nbreastfeeding: WFH/L between -3 and - Yellow: Assess the child's feeding and co"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0002", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0003_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with moderate acute malnutrition or possible severe anemia be urgently referred to hospital?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "n? Advise mother when to return immediately\nAble to finish RUTF.\nFollow up in 7 days\nChild is less than 6 months, assess\nbreastfeeding: WFH/L between -3 and - Yellow: Assess the child's feeding and counsel the\n2 z-scores MODERATE ACUTE mother on the feeding recommendations\nDoes the child have a breastfeeding OR MALNUTRITION If feeding problem, follow up in 7 days\nproblem? MUAC 115 up to 125 mm. Assess for possible TB infection.\nAdvise mother when to return immediately\nFollow-up in 30 days\nWFH/L - 2 z-scores or Green: If child is less than 2 years old, assess the\nmore NO ACUTE child's feeding and counsel the mother on\nOR MALNUTRITION feeding according to the feeding\nrecommendations\nMUAC 125 mm or more.\nIf feeding problem, follow-up in 7 days\n*WFH/L is Weight-for-Height or Weight-for-Length determined by using the WHO growth standards charts.\n** MUAC is Mid-Upper Arm Circumference measured using MUAC tape in all children 6 months or older.\n***RUTF is Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food for conducting the appetite test and feeding children with severe acute malanutrition.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:25)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR ANAEMIA\nCheck for anaemia Severe palmar pallor Pink: Refer URGENTLY to hopsital\nLook for palmar pallor. Is it: SEVERE ANAEMIA\nSevere palmar pallor*? Classify\nSome palmar pallor? ANAEMIA Classification Some pallor Yellow: Give iron**\narrow ANAEMIA Give mebendazole if child is 1 year or older and\nhas not"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0003", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0004_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with severe palmar pallor be referred for further care?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Is it: SEVERE ANAEMIA\nSevere palmar pallor*? Classify\nSome palmar pallor? ANAEMIA Classification Some pallor Yellow: Give iron**\narrow ANAEMIA Give mebendazole if child is 1 year or older and\nhas not had a dose in the previous 6 months\nAdvise mother when to return immediately\nFollow-up in 14 days\nNo palmar pallor Green: If child is less than 2 years old, assess the\nNO ANAEMIA child's feeding and counsel the mother according\nto the feeding recommendations\nIf feeding problem, follow-up in 5 days\n*Assess for sickle cell anaemia if common in your area.\n**If child has severe acute malnutrition and is receiving RUTF, DO NOT give iron because there is already adequate amount of iron in RUTF.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK FOR HIV INFECTION\nUse this chart if the child is NOT enrolled in HIV care.\nPositive virological test in Yellow: Initiate ART treatment and HIV care\nASK child CONFIRMED HIV Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis*\nClassify OR INFECTION (cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nHas the mother or child had an HIV test? HIV Positive serological test i"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0004", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0005_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should an HIV-exposed infant be tested to confirm HIV status?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nHas the mother or child had an HIV test? HIV Positive serological test in a counselling to the mother\nstatus\nIF YES: child 18 months or older Advise the mother on home care\nDecide HIV status: Asess or refer for TB assessment and INH\nMother: POSITIVE or NEGATIVE preventive therapy\nChild: Follow-up regularly as per national guidelines\nVirological test POSITIVE or NEGATIVE Mother HIV-positive AND Yellow: Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis\nSerological test POSITIVE or NEGATIVE negative virological test in HIV EXPOSED Start or continue ARV prophylaxis as\na breastfeeding child or only recommended\nIf mother is HIV positive and child is negative or stopped less than 6 weeks Do virological test to confirm HIV status**\nunknown, ASK: ago (cid:36)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:81)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:89)(cid:76)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:72)\nWas the child breastfeeding at the time or 6 weeks before OR counselling to the mother\nthe test? Mother HIV-positive, child Advise the mother on home care\nIs the child breastfeeding now? not yet tested Follow-up regularly as per national guide"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0005", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0006_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What is the recommended follow-up for an HIV-positive mother and untested child who is breastfeeding?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "6 weeks before OR counselling to the mother\nthe test? Mother HIV-positive, child Advise the mother on home care\nIs the child breastfeeding now? not yet tested Follow-up regularly as per national guidelines\nIf breastfeeding ASK: Is the mother and child on ARV\nOR"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0006", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "complications", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0007_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should cotrimoxazole prophylaxis be given to HIV-exposed or HIV-infected children?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "prophylaxis?\nPositive serological test in a\nIF NO, THEN TEST:\nchild less than 18 months\nMother and child status unknown: TEST mother. old\nMother HIV positive and child status unknown: TEST child.\nNegative HIV test in mother Green: Treat, counsel and follow-up existing infections\nor child HIV INFECTION\nUNLIKELY\n* Give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis to all HIV infected and HIV-exposed children utill confirmed negative after cessation of breastfeeding.\n** If virological test is negative, repeat test 6 weeks after the breatfeeding has stopped; if serological test is positive, do a virological test as soon as possible.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:27)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nTHEN CHECK THE CHILD'S IMMUNIZATION, VITAMIN A AND DEWORMING STATUS"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0007", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "prevention", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0008_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Are there any warning signs that indicate a child may need urgent medical evaluation after receiving vaccinations?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "IMMUNIZATION SCHEDULE: Follow national guidelines\nAGE VACCINE\nBirth BCG* OPV-0 Hep B0 VITAMIN A\n6 weeks DPT+HIB-1 OPV-1 Hep B1 RTV1 PCV1*** SUPPLEMENTATION\nGive every child a\n10 weeks DPT+HIB-2 OPV-2 Hep B2 RTV2 PCV2 dose of Vitamin A\nevery six months\nfrom the age of 6\nmonths. Record the\ndose on the child's\nchart.\n14 weeks DPT+HIB-3 OPV-3 Hep B3 RTV3 PCV3 ROUTINE WORM"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0008", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "prevention", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0009_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child with any general danger sign be referred after receiving an appropriate antibiotic and other urgent treatments?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "TREATMENT\nGive every child\nmebendazole every 6\n9 months Measles **\nmonths from the age\nof one year. Record\nthe dose on the\n18 months DPT child's card.\n*Children who are HIV positive or unknown HIV status with symptoms consistent with HIV should not be vaccinated.\n**Second dose of measles vaccine may be given at any opportunistic moment during periodic supplementary immunization activities as early as one month following the first dose.\n***HIV-positive infants and pre-term neonates who have received 3 primary vaccine doses before 12 months of age may benefit from a booster dose in the second year of life.\nASSESS OTHER PROBLEMS:\nMAKE SURE CHILD WITH ANY GENERAL DANGER SIGN IS REFERRED after first dose of an appropriate antibiotic and other urgent treatments. Treat all children with a general danger sign to prevent low\nblood sugar.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:28)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nHIV TESTING AND INTERPRENTING RESULTS\nHIV testing is RECOMMENDED for:\n(cid:36)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:81)(cid:78)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(c"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0009", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0010_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child not be diagnosed with HIV based on a positive serological test?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "3)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:43)(cid:44)(cid:57)(cid:16)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:89)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:86)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:11)(cid:44)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:92)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:78)(cid:81)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:182)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:76)(cid:85)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:15)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:86)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:12)\nTypes of HIV Tests\nWhat does the test detect? How to interpret the test?\nSEROLOGICAL These tests detect antibodies made by HIV antibodies pass from the mother to the child. Most antibodies have gone by 12 months of age, but in some instances they do not\nTESTS immune cells in response to HIV. disappear until the child is 18 months of age.\n(Including rapid They do not detect the HIV virus itself. This means that a positive serological test in children less than 18 months in NOT a reliable way to check for infection of the child"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0010", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0011_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a virological test be used instead of a serological test for HIV in children under 18 months?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "of age.\n(Including rapid They do not detect the HIV virus itself. This means that a positive serological test in children less than 18 months in NOT a reliable way to check for infection of the child.\ntests)\nVIROLOGICAL These tests directly detect the presence of Positive virological (PCR) tests reliably detect HIV infection at any age, even before the child is 18 months old.\nTESTS the HIV virus or products of the virus in the If the tests are negative and the child has been breastfeeding, this does not rule out infection. The baby may have just become infected.\n(Including DNA blood. (cid:55)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:75)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:76)(cid:91)(cid:3)(cid:90)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:78)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:68)(cid:73)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:69)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:75)(cid:68)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:82)(cid:80)(cid:83)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:83)(cid:83)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:178)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0011", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0012_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should an HIV-exposed child less than 18 months of age be retested for HIV?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:87)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:68)(cid:69)(cid:79)(cid:92)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:88)(cid:79)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:88)(cid:87)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:73)(cid:72)(cid:70)(cid:87)(cid:76)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:17)\nor RNA PCR)\nFor HIV exposed children 18 months or older, a positive HIV antibody test result means the child is infected.\nFor HIV exposed children less than 18 months of age:\nIf PCR or other virological test is available, test from 4 - 6 weeks of age.\nA positive result means the child is infected.\nA negative result means the child is not infected, but could become infected if they are still breast feeding.\nIf PCR or other virological test is not available, use HIV antibody test. A positive result is consistent with the fact that the child has been exposed to HIV, but does not tell us if the child is definitely infected.\nInterpreting the HIV Antibody Test Results in a Child less than 18 Months of Age\nBreastfeeding status POSITIVE (+) test NEGATIVE (-) test\nNOT BREASTFEEDING, and has not in HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they could be infected. HIV negative Child is not HIV infected\nlast 6 weeks Repeat test at 18 months.\nBREASTFEEDING HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they Child can still be infected by breastfeeding. Repeat test once breastfeeding has been\ncould be infected. Repeat test at 18 months or once discontin"}], "metadata": {"source_id": "who_imci_0012", "source": "who_imci", "doc_type": "guideline_section", "style": "red_flag_check", "section_type": "treatment", "prompt_version": "v1"}}
{"id": "qa_who_imci_0013_red_flag_check", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a clinical triage decision-support assistant for low-resource settings (e.g. sub-Saharan Africa primary care). Base your reasoning on WHO IMCI/ETAT and SATS guidelines. You are NOT a substitute for clinician judgment. Always recommend in-person clinical evaluation for emergencies. If uncertain, escalate."}, {"role": "user", "content": "When should a child exposed to HIV through breastfeeding be retested for infection?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "HIV EXPOSED and/or HIV infected - Manage as if they Child can still be infected by breastfeeding. Repeat test once breastfeeding has been\ncould be infected. Repeat test at 18 months or once discontinued for more than 6 weeks.\nbreastfeeding has been discontinued for more than 6 weeks.\n(cid:51)(cid:68)(cid:74)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:20)(cid:19)(cid:3)(cid:82)(cid:73)(cid:3)(cid:26)(cid:25)(cid:3)\nWHO PAEDIATRIC STAGING FOR HIV INFECTION\n(cid:55)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:76)(cid:86)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:71)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:3)(cid:80)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:75)(cid:76)(cid:79)(cid:71)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:88)(cid:85)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:74)(cid:3)(cid:73)(cid:82)(cid:79)(cid:79)(cid:82)(cid:90)(cid:3)(cid:88)(cid:83)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:71)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:86)(cid:83)(cid:82)(cid:81)(cid:86)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:82)(cid:3)(cid:36)(cid:53)(cid:57)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:85)(cid:72)(cid:68)(cid:87)(cid:80)(cid:72)(cid:81)(cid:87)(cid:17)(cid:3)(cid:39)(cid:72)(cid:87)(cid:72)(cid:85)(cid:80)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:87)(cid:75)(cid:72)(cid:3)(cid:70)(cid:79)(cid:76)(cid:81)(cid:76)(cid:70)(cid:68)(cid:79)(cid:3)(cid:86)(cid:87)(c